__d(function(e,a,t,h,i,n,o){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(n,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(n,"SEERAH",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return s}});const s=[{id:1,year:"571 CE",hijri:"53  BH",month:"",title:"The birth of the Prophet Muhammad, son of Abdullah, peace be upon him.",desc:"The biographers and historians have differed in determining the day, month, and year of his birth, peace be upon him, but they agreed that his birth, peace be upon him, was on a Monday in the Year of the Elephant. Ibn al-Qayyim said: \"There is no dispute that he was born, peace be upon him, in the heart of Mecca, and that his birth was in the Year of the Elephant.\" It is narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said: (The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, was born in the Year of the Elephant), and this is what no scholar doubts. Khalifa ibn Khayyat, Ibn al-Jazari, Ibn Dihya, and Ibn al-Jawzi have reported consensus on this. As for his birth on Monday, it is narrated from Abu Qatadah al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: \"The Prophet, peace be upon him, was asked about fasting on Mondays. He said: 'That is the day I was born, and the day I was sent (as a prophet) or the revelation was sent down to me.'\" Ibn Kathir said: \"Far be it, nay, he erred, who said: He was born on Friday the seventeenth of Rabi' al-Awwal.\" The point of disagreement was in determining the month and the day within it. Ibn Ishaq narrated from a group of the companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, that they said to him: \"O Messenger of Allah, tell us about yourself.\" He said: (Yes, I am the supplication of my father Abraham, and the good news of Jesus, and my mother saw when she was pregnant with me that a light emerged from her that illuminated the palaces of Syria for her). His birth, peace be upon him, was after the death of his father Abdullah, as he was a fetus in his mother's womb when his father passed away, so the Prophet, peace be upon him, grew up an orphan. His birth took place in the house of Abu Talib in the valley of Banu Hashim.",location:"The house of Abu Talib in the Shi'b of Banu Hashim",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/1",icon:"star"},{id:2,year:"575 CE",hijri:"49  BH",month:"",title:"The first incident of the Prophet's chest being opened.",desc:"Anas bin Malik narrated that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, was playing with some boys when Gabriel, peace be upon him, came to him. Gabriel took him and laid him down, then opened his chest and took out his heart. He extracted a clot from it and said, \"This is the devil's share of you.\" Then he washed it in a golden basin with Zamzam water, then sewed it up, and returned it to its place. The boys came running to his mother \u2013 meaning his wet nurse \u2013 and said, \"Muhammad has been killed!\" They met him, and his complexion had changed. Anas said, \"I used to see the mark of that stitch on his chest.\"",location:"Honorable Mecca",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/2",icon:"child"},{id:3,year:"575 CE",hijri:"48  BH",month:"",title:"The death of the Prophet's mother, Aminah bint Wahb, and the upbringing of the Prophet, peace be upon him, by Umm Ayman.",desc:"When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, reached six years of age, his mother, the noble Aminah bint Wahb, took him to visit her maternal uncles, Banu Adi ibn al-Najjar, accompanied by his wet nurse, Umm Ayman, may Allah be pleased with her. They were traveling on two camels. His mother disembarked with him at the house of al-Nabighah, near the grave of his father, Abdullah ibn Abd al-Muttalib. She stayed with her relatives for a month, and then his mother returned with him to Mecca. When they reached Al-Abwa, his mother Aminah bint Wahb passed away. Umm Ayman, may Allah be pleased with her \u2013 whose name was Barakah bint Tha'labah ibn Hisn \u2013 returned with him. Umm Ayman, may Allah be pleased with her, loved the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, with an intense love and cared for him with the utmost attention, protecting him with her care and compassion. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, was dutiful to her and treated her kindly. When he grew older, peace and blessings be upon him, he freed her, and then married her to Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, by whom she had Usamah ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with them both. Umm Ayman, may Allah be pleased with her, passed away five months after the passing of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him.",location:"Al-Abwa",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/3",icon:"heart"},{id:4,year:"575 CE",hijri:"48  BH",month:"",title:"The guardianship of Abdul Muttalib over the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and his knowledge of his affairs.",desc:"When Aminah, the mother of the Messenger, peace be upon him, passed away while he was six years old, his guardianship fell to his grandfather, Abdul Muttalib ibn Hashim. Abdul Muttalib was a man of honor and virtue among his people, and Quraysh called him \"Al-Fadl\" (the Virtuous) due to his generosity and merit. He assumed the responsibilities of Rifadah (providing food for pilgrims) and Siqayah (providing water for pilgrims) after the death of his father, Hashim ibn Abd Manaf. Abdul Muttalib loved his grandson Muhammad with a great love, preferring him over all his other sons and showing him more tenderness than he showed his own children. Ibn Ishaq, may Allah have mercy on him, said: \"The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was with his grandfather Abdul Muttalib ibn Hashim \u2013 meaning, after the death of his mother Aminah bint Wahb. A bed would be laid for Abdul Muttalib in the shade of the Kaaba, and his sons would sit around that bed until he came out to it. None of his sons would sit on it out of reverence for him. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, would come as a young boy, strong and full, and sit on it. His uncles would try to pull him away from it, but when Abdul Muttalib saw that, he would say, 'Leave my son; by Allah, he has a great destiny.' Then he would seat him with him on his bed, stroke his back with his hand, and be pleased with what he saw him do.\" Abdul Muttalib would not eat food unless he said, \"Bring my son to me!\" and he would be brought to him. When Abdul Muttalib was nearing death, he bequeathed to Abu Talib the care and protection of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him.",location:"The Shadow of the Kaaba",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/4",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:5,year:"577 CE",hijri:"46  BH",month:"",title:"The death of Abdul Muttalib and his bequest of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, to Abu Talib.",desc:"When the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, reached eight years of age, his grandfather, Abd al-Muttalib, passed away. When Abd al-Muttalib was on his deathbed, he entrusted his son, Abu Talib, with the care and protection of the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, and to look after him. He entrusted him to Abu Talib because Abdullah, the father of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and Abu Talib were from the same mother. So, when Abd al-Muttalib died, Abu Talib was the one who took charge of the affairs of the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, after his grandfather. Ibn Sa'd and Ibn Asakir narrated from Ibn Abbas, may God be pleased with them, and others, who said: When Abd al-Muttalib passed away, Abu Talib took the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, and he would stay with him. Abu Talib loved him with an intense love, not loving his own children as much. He would not sleep except next to him, and he would favor him with food. When Abu Talib's children ate together or individually, they would not be full. But if the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, ate with them, they would be full. When Abu Talib intended to give them breakfast or dinner, he would say, \"Wait until my son arrives.\" Then the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, would come and eat with them, and they would have leftovers from their food. If he was not with them, their food would not be enough to fill them. If it was milk, he would drink first, then the children would take a large bowl, like a platter, and drink from it until they were all satisfied from that single bowl. One of them would even drink a whole bowl by himself. Abu Talib would then say, \"Indeed, you are blessed!\"",location:"Honorable Mecca",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/5",icon:"dove"},{id:6,year:"581 CE",hijri:"42  BH",month:"",title:"The Prophet's journey with his uncle Abu Talib to Syria.",desc:"When the Prophet, peace be upon him, reached the age of twelve years, his uncle Abu Talib traveled to the Levant on a trade caravan, and he took him along. When the caravan camped at Busra, they passed by a monk there named Bahira, who was knowledgeable in the Gospel and an expert in Christianity. It was there that Bahira saw the Prophet, peace be upon him, and he began to observe him and speak with him. Then he turned to Abu Talib and asked, \"Who is this boy to you?\" Abu Talib replied, \"He is my son.\" (Abu Talib used to call him his son due to his intense love and compassion for him). Bahira said, \"He is not your son, and it is not fitting that the father of this boy should be alive.\" Abu Talib said, \"He is my nephew.\" Bahira asked, \"What happened to his father?\" Abu Talib replied, \"He died while his mother was pregnant with him.\" Bahira said, \"You have spoken the truth. Return him to his land and beware of the Jews, for by God, if they see him here, they will wish him great harm. For this nephew of yours is destined for a great matter.\" Abu Talib hastened back to Mecca with him. Al-Tirmidhi narrated that Abu Talib went out to the Levant, and the Prophet, peace be upon him, went out with him along with some elders of Quraysh. When they approached the monk (meaning Bahira), they dismounted and set up their camp. The monk came out to them. Previously, they used to pass by him, but he would not come out to them or pay them any attention. As they were setting up their camp, the monk went among them until he came and took the hand of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and said, \"This is the master of the worlds, this is the Messenger of the Lord of the worlds, whom Allah will send as a mercy to the worlds.\" Some elders of Quraysh said to him, \"What is your knowledge of this?\" He replied, \"When you approached from the mountain pass, no tree or stone remained but bowed in prostration, and only prophets bow in prostration. And I know him by the Seal of Prophethood below the cartilage of his shoulder, like an apple.\" Then he went back and prepared food for them. When he brought it to them, and the Prophet, peace be upon him, was tending to the camels, he said, \"Send for him.\" The Prophet, peace be upon him, came, and a cloud shaded him. When he approached the people, he found that they had preceded him to the shade of the tree. When the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sat down, the shade of the tree moved to cover him. Bahira said, \"Look at the shade of the tree, how it has moved to him!\" While he was standing among them, he implored them not to take him to the Romans, for if the Romans saw him, they would recognize him by his description and kill him. He looked and saw seven men approaching from the direction of Rome. He met them and asked, \"What brings you here?\" They said, \"We have come because this Prophet is to emerge this month. No road was left without people being sent to it, and we have been informed of his news, so we were sent to this road of yours.\" He asked, \"Did anyone who is better than you precede you?\" They said, \"We were only informed of his news for this road of yours.\" He asked, \"If Allah wills something to happen, can any person prevent it?\" They said, \"No.\" He said, \"Then pledge allegiance to him, and they stayed with him.\" The monk Bahira said, \"I adjure you by Allah, who is his guardian?\" Abu Talib said, \"He kept imploring him until Abu Talib returned him.\"",location:"Busra",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/6",icon:"hand-holding-heart"},{id:7,year:"585 CE",hijri:"39  BH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The War of al-Fij\u0101r, and the Prophet's witnessing of it, peace be upon him.",desc:"When the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, reached fourteen or fifteen years of age, the War of Al-Fijar broke out between Quraysh and those with them from Kinanah, and Qays Aylan. This was one of the greatest days of the Arabs. The cause of this war was that Urwah Al-Rahhal, son of Utbah bin Rabiah, granted protection to a caravan belonging to Al-Nu'man bin Al-Mundhir. Al-Barrad bin Qays, one of the Banu Damrah bin Bakr bin Abd Manat bin Kinanah, said to him, \"Will you protect it against Kinanah?\" He replied, \"Yes, and against all creation.\" So Urwah Al-Rahhal set out, and Al-Barrad followed him, seeking an opportunity. When they were at Tayman Dhi Dhilal in Al-Aliyah, Urwah was caught unaware. Al-Barrad attacked him and killed him during the sacred month. This is why it was called the War of Al-Fijar. A messenger came to Quraysh and said, \"Al-Barrad has killed Urwah during the sacred month at Ukaz.\" They set out, and Hawazin were unaware. Then the news reached them, and they pursued them, catching up to them before they entered the sanctuary. They fought until nightfall, and then they entered the sanctuary, so Hawazin ceased their pursuit. After this day, the people met for many days, with each tribe of Quraysh and Kinanah having a leader from among them, and each tribe of Qays having a leader from among them. The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, witnessed some of their days, including the Day of Al-Nakhlah, when he was fourteen years old. Some say twenty years old. After their return from it, in Dhu al-Qi'dah, the Pact of Al-Fudul took place. The reason for it was that a man from Zabid, from Yemen, sold goods to Al-As bin Wa'il Al-Sahmi, who delayed paying him the price. The man climbed Mount Abu Qubais and cried out, mentioning his grievance. Quraysh then formed the Pact of Al-Fudul to support the oppressed. The Prophet, peace be upon him, witnessed this pact with them.",location:"Makkah Al-Mukarramah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/7",icon:"book"},{id:8,year:"591 CE",hijri:"33  BH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The Pact of the Virtuous among the tribes of Quraysh.",desc:"The Hilf al-Fudul (Alliance of the Virtuous) took place in a sacred month. Tribes of Quraysh were called to it: Banu Hashim, Banu Muttalib, Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza, Zuhrah ibn Kilab, and Taym ibn Murrah. They gathered in the house of Abdullah ibn Jud'an al-Taymi, due to his age and nobility. They made a pact and a covenant that if they found anyone oppressed in Mecca, whether from its people or others, they would stand with him and be against whoever oppressed him until his rights were restored. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, witnessed this alliance and said about it: \"I witnessed an alliance in the house of Abdullah ibn Jud'an that I would not exchange for a herd of red camels, and if I were called to it in Islam, I would respond.\" This alliance contradicted the spirit of tribalistic pride that partisanship stirred. It is said that the reason for this alliance was that a man from Zubayd came to Mecca with merchandise, which Al-As ibn Wail al-Sahmi bought from him but withheld his payment. The man sought help from the allied tribes of Abd al-Dar, Makhzum, Jumah, Sahm, and Adi, but they paid him no heed. He then climbed Mount Abu Qubays and called out with verses describing his oppression, raising his voice. Al-Zubayr ibn Abd al-Muttalib then went forth and said, \"This cannot be left as is.\" They gathered and formed the alliance known as Hilf al-Fudul. Then they went to Al-As ibn Wail and retrieved the Zubaydi's right. The reason for its naming is that three men from the Jurhum tribe, namely Al-Fadl ibn Fadalah, Al-Fadl ibn Wada'ah, and Al-Fadl ibn al-Harith, had previously made a similar agreement. When the actions of the Quraysh resembled the actions of these early Jurhumis, all of whom were named Al-Fadl, the alliance was named Hilf al-Fudul.",location:"The house of Abdullah ibn Jud'an",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/8",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:9,year:"595 CE",hijri:"28  BH",month:"",title:"The marriage of the Prophet, peace be upon him, to Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, may Allah be pleased with her.",desc:"There are differing opinions regarding the age of the Prophet, peace be upon him, and the age of Khadijah when they married. It has been said that he was twenty-five. It has also been said that he was twenty-seven. And it has been said that he was thirty. And other opinions have been stated. As for the age of Khadijah, the sayings also vary between thirty-five, forty, and other ages. When Khadijah, may Allah be pleased with her, heard about the Prophet, peace be upon him, his trustworthiness, and his noble character, it is narrated that Khadijah's sister hired the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and his partner for a journey. When they completed the journey, some payment was still due to her. Her partner began to approach them to collect the dues, and he would say to Muhammad, peace be upon him: \"Go.\" Muhammad would reply: \"You go, for I feel shy.\" One time, when her partner came to them, she asked: \"Where is Muhammad? Why doesn't he come with you?\" He said: \"I told him, but he claimed he felt shy.\" She mentioned this to her sister Khadijah, who said: \"I have never seen a man more shy, more chaste, or... than Muhammad, peace be upon him.\" This impressed Khadijah, so she sent for him and said: \"Go to my father and propose to him.\" He said: \"Your father is a man of great wealth, and he will not agree to this.\" She said: \"Go, meet him and speak to him, and I will handle the rest.\" The Prophet, peace be upon him, married her while she was a widow.",location:"Makkah Al-Mukarramah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/9",icon:"route"},{id:10,year:"596 CE",hijri:"28  BH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The Prophet's journey to Syria for the second time.",desc:"When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, reached twenty-five years of age, he had no name in Mecca except \"Al-Amin\" (The Trustworthy), due to the perfection of his good qualities. His uncle, Abu Talib, said to him: \"O nephew, I am a man with no wealth, and times have become harsh upon us, and we have been afflicted by severe years. We have no provisions or trade. Your people's caravan is ready to depart for Syria, and Khadijah bint Khuwaylid is sending men from your people with her caravans to trade her wealth, and they gain profits. If you were to approach her and offer yourself for her business, she would hasten to you and prefer you over others, due to what she has heard of your purity. Although I dislike you going to Syria and fear for you from the Jews, we have no choice.\" Khadijah bint Khuwaylid was a merchant woman of noble lineage, great wealth, and extensive trade that she sent to Syria. Her caravans were like those of the rest of Quraysh. She used to hire men and give them money for trade on a profit-sharing basis. News of the Prophet's truthful speech, his great trustworthiness, and his noble character reached Khadijah. So, she sent for him to manage her trade. He set out with her servant Maysarah until they reached Syria. His uncles had instructed the caravan members to look after him until he reached Syria. They camped in the market of Busra, under a tree near the monastery of a monk named Nestorius. The monk looked at Maysarah, whom he recognized, and said: \"O Maysarah, who is this who has camped under this tree?\" Maysarah replied: \"A man from Quraysh, from the people of the Sanctuary.\" The monk said: \"No one has ever camped under this tree except a prophet.\" Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sold his goods, meaning his merchandise, and bought what he intended to buy. He then set off, returning to Mecca with Maysarah. When he arrived in Mecca with Khadijah's wealth, she sold what had come back for double its value or close to it. Maysarah told her about the monk's words and what he had witnessed of the Prophet's affairs. Thereupon, she proposed herself to him, may Allah be pleased with her. She was of the noblest lineage among the women of Quraysh, the most honorable, and the wealthiest. When she said this to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, he mentioned it to his uncles. His uncle Hamzah went with him and approached Khuwaylid ibn Asad to propose marriage. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, married her. She was the first woman he married, and he did not marry anyone else until she passed away, may Allah be pleased with her.",location:"Bosra",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/10",icon:"flag"},{id:11,year:"605 CE",hijri:"18  BH",month:"",title:"The Prophet, peace be upon him, places the Black Stone in its place in the Kaaba.",desc:"From Mujahid, from his freedman, that he told him: I was among those building the Kaaba in the pre-Islamic era... He said: We built until we reached the place of the Stone, and no one saw the Stone. It was in the midst of our stones, like a man's head, such that a man's face could almost be seen in it. Then one clan of Quraysh said: We will place it. And others said: We will place it. They said: Let an arbitrator decide between you. They said: The first man to emerge from the mountain pass. Then the Prophet, peace be upon him, came. They said: The trustworthy one has come to you. They told him, and he placed it in a garment, then he called their clans, and they took hold of its edges with him, and he, peace be upon him, placed it.",location:"The Kaaba",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/11",icon:"mountain"},{id:12,year:"610 CE",hijri:"13  BH",month:"\u0631\u0645\u0636\u0627\u0646",title:"The first revelation and the prophethood of the Prophet, peace be upon him.",desc:"The first form of revelation the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) received was true visions in his sleep. He would not see a vision except it would come to pass like the breaking of the dawn. Then, he began to love solitude, so he would retreat to the cave of Hira and worship there. While he was in the cave, Gabriel (peace be upon him) came to him with the divine words: \"{Read, [O Muhammad], by which your Lord created (1) Created man from a clinging substance. (2) Read, and your Lord is the most generous. (3) [He] who taught by the pen. (4) Taught man that which he knew not. (5)}\" [Al-Alaq: 1-5]. He then recounted this to his wife Khadijah, who in turn told Warqah ibn Nawfal. Warqah confirmed it, saying, \"This is the greatest law (Namus) that came to Moses.\" Then, the revelation ceased for a period. After that, Gabriel returned to him while he was sitting on a chair. The Prophet (peace be upon him) was frightened by him and returned to his family, saying, \"Wrap me, wrap me.\" Then Allah the Almighty revealed: \"{O you who enshrouds himself! (1) Arise and warn. (2) And your Lord magnify. (3) And your clothing purify. (4) And uncleanliness abandon. (5)}\" [Al-Muddaththir: 1-5]. This marked the beginning of his prophethood and the command to convey the message.",location:"Cave of Hira",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/12",icon:"sun"},{id:13,year:"610 CE",hijri:"13  BH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The cessation of revelation from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him.",desc:"The revelation ceased from the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, for a period of time, which distressed him and saddened him. Then Gabriel came to him with the chapter of Ad-Duha, his Lord swearing to him \u2013 and He is the One who honored him with what He honored him \u2013 that He had not forsaken him. Al-Bukhari narrated in his Sahih, from the narration of Jundub ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Gabriel was delayed in coming to the Prophet, peace be upon him. A woman from Quraysh \u2013 and she was Umm Jamil, Arwa bint Harb, the sister of Abu Sufyan and the wife of Abu Lahab \u2013 said: \"His devil has delayed him.\" Then it was revealed: \"By the morning brightness, and by the night when it settles, your Lord has not taken leave of you, nor has He detested you.\"",location:"Undetermined",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/13",icon:"users"},{id:14,year:"610 CE",hijri:"13  BH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The first of people in faith in Allah and His Messenger.",desc:"The first to believe in Allah and His Messenger was Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, his wife, peace be upon him, Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, and Ali ibn Abi Talib. Then Zayd ibn Harithah, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, embraced Islam. Through the invitation of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, Uthman ibn Affan, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, and Talhah ibn Ubayd Allah, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf embraced Islam. Then Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Asad, and Uthman ibn Maz'un embraced Islam, and then his brothers Qudamah and Abdullah, and his son al-Sa'ib ibn Uthman ibn Maz'un, Asma bint Abi Bakr al-Siddiq, and Aisha bint Abi Bakr al-Siddiq, who was young. Then Khalid ibn Sa'id ibn al-As embraced Islam, and his wife Aminah bint Bint Khalaf ibn As'ad al-Khuzaiyyah embraced Islam with him, and Bilal, Ammar ibn Yasir, and his mother Sumayyah, Suhayb ibn Sinan al-Numayri, known as al-Rumi, Amr ibn Absah al-Sulami, Amr ibn Sa'id ibn al-As, Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl, and his wife Fatimah bint al-Khattab, the sister of Umar ibn al-Khattab, Umayr ibn Abi Waqqas, Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and his brother Utbah ibn Mas'ud, Salit ibn Amr al-Amiri, Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah al-Makhzumi, and his wife Asma bint Salamah ibn Mukharribah al-Tamimiyyah, and Mas'ud ibn Rabi'ah ibn Amr al-Qari from the Banu al-Hun ibn Khuzaymah, who are al-Qarah, and Khunays ibn Hudhafah ibn Qays ibn Adi al-Sahmi, and Abdullah ibn Jahsh al-Asadi. The remaining early believers in the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, are: Ja'far ibn Abi Talib and his wife Asma bint Umays, Amir ibn Rabi'ah al-Anazi, from Anz ibn Wail, an ally of al-Khattab ibn Nufayl, Abu Ahmad ibn Jahsh al-A'ma, Hatib ibn al-Harith ibn Ma'mar al-Jumahi, and his wife Bint al-Mujallil al-Amiriyyah, Hattab ibn al-Harith, his brother, and his wife Fukayhah bint Yasar, and their brother Ma'mar ibn al-Harith ibn Ma'mar al-Jumahi, and al-Muttalib ibn Azhar ibn Abd Awf al-Zuhri, and his wife Ramlah bint Abi Awf al-Sahmiyyah, and al-Nahham, whose name is Nu'aym ibn Abdullah al-Adawi, and Amir ibn Fuheirah, a freed slave of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, and Hatib ibn Amr ibn Shams ibn Abd Wadd al-Amiri, the brother of Salit ibn Amr, and Abu Hudhayfah ibn Utbah ibn Rabi'ah, whose name is Hisham ibn Utbah...",location:"Undetermined",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/14",icon:"scroll"},{id:15,year:"613 CE",hijri:"9  BH",month:"",title:"What the Companions suffered from the persecution of Quraysh.",desc:"The first seven to openly declare their Islam were: the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, Abu Bakr, Ammar, his mother Sumayyah, Suhayb, Bilal, and Al-Miqdad. As for the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, God protected him through his uncle Abu Talib. As for Abu Bakr, his people protected him. As for the rest of them, the polytheists seized them, clothed them in iron armor, and roasted them in the sun. None of them escaped unharmed except that they complied with what the polytheists wanted and deceived them, except for Bilal. His soul was dear to him for the sake of God, the Almighty, and he was insignificant to his people. They seized him, gave him to the children, who paraded him through the valleys of Mecca, while he said: \"One, one.\" They put a rope around his neck and handed him over to the children to play with, until it left a mark on his neck. Bilal belonged to some of the Banu Jumah. The one who took the lead in torturing him was Umayyah ibn Khalaf. He would take him out when the midday heat was intense, throw him on his back in the open ground of Mecca, then order a large rock to be placed on his chest. Then he would say to him: \"You will remain like this until you die, or disbelieve in Muhammad and worship Al-Lat and Al-Uzza.\" While enduring this torture and affliction, he would say: \"One, one.\" It was as if his torture and affliction only increased his faith upon faith. Abu Bakr felt compassion for him when he saw him one day in this extreme humiliation, so he bought him and set him free, and he also freed six others who were being tortured for Islam.",location:"The Valley of Mecca",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/15",icon:"shield"},{id:16,year:"613 CE",hijri:"9  BH",month:"",title:"The Quraysh asked Abu Talib to restrain the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, from them.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, called to God Almighty in all his states; by night and by day, in secret and in public. Nothing diverted him from this, no one turned him back, and no obstacle prevented him. He followed people to their gatherings, assemblies, and congregations, and during festivals and the pilgrimage seasons, calling to whoever he met, whether free or slave, weak or strong, rich or poor. The disbelievers of his people, the polytheists of Quraysh, oppressed him and those who followed him. Among the harshest of them towards him were his uncle Abu Lahab and his wife Umm Jamil, the firewood carrier \u2013 Arwa bint Harb bin Umayya, the sister of Abu Sufyan. His uncle Abu Talib bin Abdul Muttalib, however, was compassionate towards him, treated him well, defended him, and protected him, opposing his people in this matter, even though he remained on their religion. His continued adherence to his people's religion was part of God Almighty's wisdom and a protection He provided for His Messenger. For had Abu Talib embraced Islam, he would have had no standing or influence among the polytheists of Quraysh, nor would they have feared or respected him. The biographers narrated that Quraysh came to Abu Talib and said, \"Your nephew has annoyed us in our gathering place and our mosque; stop him from us.\" He said, \"O Aqil, go and bring Muhammad to me.\" So he brought him in the heat of the afternoon. When he came to them, Abu Talib said, \"These cousins of yours claim that you annoy them in their gathering place and their mosque; so cease annoying them.\" The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, raised his gaze to the sky and said, \"Do you see this sun?\" They said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Then I am no more able to abandon that than you are to be ignited by a flame from it.\" Abu Talib then said, \"By God, my nephew has never lied. So go back.\"",location:"Makkah al-Mukarramah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/16",icon:"moon"},{id:17,year:"615 CE",hijri:"8  BH",month:"\u0631\u062c\u0628",title:"The First Hijra to Abyssinia.",desc:"Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: When Makkah became too constricted for us, and the companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, were harmed and persecuted, and they saw the afflictions and trials they faced in their religion, and that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, could not ward off that from them, while the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was protected by his people and his uncle, and nothing he disliked reached him that befell his companions. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said to them: Indeed, in the land of Abyssinia, there is a king under whom no one is wronged. So go to his land until Allah grants you relief and a way out of what you are in. So we went out to it until we gathered there, and we settled in the best of lands with the best of neighbors, secure in our religion, and we did not fear injustice from him... It is said that their departure to Abyssinia was in Rajab in the fifth year of the Prophetic Mission. Twelve men and four women of the Muslims migrated therein, including Uthman ibn Affan, and he was the first to leave, accompanied by his wife Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him.",location:"Abyssinia",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/17",icon:"star"},{id:18,year:"615 CE",hijri:"7  BH",month:"",title:"The Islam of Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him.",desc:"In the latter part of the sixth year of his prophethood, Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, embraced Islam. The reason for his conversion was that Abu Jahl once passed by the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, near Safa, insulted him, and wronged him. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, remained silent, not speaking to him. Then Abu Jahl struck him on the head with a stone, wounding him severely until blood flowed. He then left him and went to the gathering of Quraysh near the Kaaba and sat with them. A slave woman belonging to Abd Allah ibn Jud'an, in her dwelling on Safa, witnessed all of this. Hamzah arrived from hunting, his bow slung over his shoulder. The slave woman informed him of what she had seen Abu Jahl do. Hamzah became enraged and went out running, not stopping for anyone, intending to confront Abu Jahl if he met him. When he entered the Masjid, he stood over Abu Jahl and said, \"Do you curse my nephew while I am of his religion!\" Then he struck him with his bow, inflicting a terrible wound. Men from Banu Makhzum, Abu Jahl's tribe, rose up, and men from Banu Hashim, Hamzah's tribe, also rose up. Abu Jahl said, \"Leave Abu Imarah alone! For I have insulted his nephew with an ugly insult.\" Ibn Ishaq said: \"Then Hamzah returned to his house, and Satan came to him saying: 'You are the chief of Quraysh, yet you follow this apostate and abandon the religion of your forefathers. Death is better for you than what you have done.' Hamzah turned to himself and said: 'What have I done?! O Allah, if this is guidance, then make its confirmation in my heart. Otherwise, grant me a way out of what I have fallen into.' He spent a night such as he had never spent before, tormented by Satan's whispers, until morning. He then went to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and said: 'O nephew, I have fallen into a matter and do not know how to escape it. Is it guidance for someone like me to remain in a state I do not know whether it is right or grievous error? Tell me a story; I have longed, O nephew, for you to tell me.' The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, turned to him, reminded him, admonished him, warned him, and gave him good tidings. Allah then cast faith into his heart regarding what the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, had said. He said: 'I bear witness that you are truthful, a true testimony. Reveal your religion, O nephew, for by Allah, I would not wish to have all that the sky covers if it meant I were to return to my former religion.' Thus, Hamzah's embrace of Islam, may Allah be pleased with him, began as the pride of a man who refused to see his kinsman insulted. Then Allah expanded his chest, and he held fast to the firmest handhold, and the Muslims were greatly strengthened by him!",location:"As-Safa",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/18",icon:"child"},{id:19,year:"615 CE",hijri:"7  BH",month:"",title:"The second migration to Abyssinia.",desc:"It reached the Emigrants in Abyssinia that Quraysh had embraced Islam, so they returned to Mecca in Shawwal of the same year they had emigrated. When they were a short distance from Mecca, and they learned the truth of the matter, those who wished returned to Abyssinia, and none of the rest entered Mecca except in secret, or under the protection of a man from Quraysh. Then the affliction and torture from Quraysh intensified against them and the Muslims, and their tribes intervened on their behalf. It was difficult for Quraysh to accept what they heard from the Negus about good protection, and the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, saw no alternative but to advise his companions to emigrate to Abyssinia once again. The Muslims prepared for emigration again. Quraysh tried to thwart the second emigration operation, but the Muslims were faster and took refuge with the Negus of Abyssinia before they could be caught. Eighty-three men and eighteen or nineteen women emigrated. Quraysh sent Amr ibn al-As and Abdullah ibn Abi Rabi'ah, before they had embraced Islam, and sent with them valuable gifts for the Negus and his bishops. After they presented themselves to the Negus, they offered him the gifts and said to him: \"O King, there have come to your land foolish youths who have abandoned the religion of their people and have not entered your religion, and they have brought a new religion that neither we nor you know. Their noble kinsmen, their fathers, uncles, and tribes, have sent us to you to return them to them, for they are more concerned about them and know best what they have criticized them for.\" And they rebuked them for it. The bishops said: \"The King is right, so hand them over to them, so they can return them to their people and their land.\" The Negus sent for the Muslims and summoned them. They attended, having agreed to tell the truth, whatever the consequences. The Negus said to them: \"What is this religion that you have abandoned your people for, and have not entered my religion or the religion of any of these communities?\" Ja'far ibn Abi Talib \u2013 who was the spokesman for the Muslims \u2013 said: \"O King, we were a people of ignorance; we worshipped idols, ate carrion, committed indecencies, severed ties of kinship, mistreated neighbors, and the strong among us devoured the weak.\" He then enumerated the virtues of what the Prophet, peace be upon him, had brought them, and said: \"So we believed him, and we accepted him, and we followed him in the religion of Allah that he brought us. We worshipped Allah alone, associating nothing with Him. We forbade what was forbidden to us, and permitted what was permitted to us. Then our people attacked us, tortured us, and tried to turn us away from our religion, so that we would return to the worship of idols instead of the worship of Allah Almighty, and so that we would permit what we used to permit of evils. When they oppressed us, wronged us, made things difficult for us, and prevented us from practicing our religion, we came to your land, and we chose you over all others, and desired your protection, and hoped that we would not be wronged in your presence, O King.\" The Negus said to him: \"Do you have anything of what he brought from God?\" Ja'far said: \"Yes.\" The Negus said: \"Then recite it to me.\" So he recited to him a portion of Surah Maryam. The Negus wept until his beard was wet, and his bishops wept until their scriptures were wet when they heard what he recited to them. Then the Negus said to them: \"Indeed, this and what Jesus brought comes from the same source. Go, for by God, I will not hand them over to you, nor will they be able to.\" (addressing Amr ibn al-As and his companion). They left, and when they left, Amr ibn al-As said to Abdullah ibn Abi Rabi'ah: \"By God, tomorrow I will go to him about them with something that will uproot their existence.\" Abdullah ibn Abi Rabi'ah said to him: \"Do not do that, for they have kinship ties, even if they have opposed us.\" But Amr insisted on his opinion. The next day, he said to the Negus: \"O King, they say something great about Jesus, son of Mary.\" The Negus sent for them and asked them about their belief concerning the Messiah. They were frightened, but they agreed to tell the truth, whatever the consequences. When they entered and he asked them, Ja'far said: \"We say about him what our Prophet, peace be upon him, brought us: He is the servant of Allah, His Messenger, His Spirit, and His Word, which He cast to Mary, the pure virgin.\" The Negus took a twig from the ground and said: \"By God, Jesus, son of Mary, did not exceed what you have said with this twig.\" His bishops grumbled, and he said: \"Even if you grumble.\" Then he said to the Muslims: \"Go, you are safe in my land.\" (Al-Shuyum means safe in the Abyssinian language). Whoever insults you will pay a fine, whoever insults you will pay a fine, whoever insults you will pay a fine. I would not want a mountain of gold if it meant I had harmed one of you. (Al-Dabr means mountain in the Abyssinian language). Then he said to his retinue: \"Return their gifts to them, for I have no need of them. By God, Allah did not take bribes from me when He returned my kingdom to me, so I would take bribes in this matter. And He did not obey people concerning me, so I would obey them concerning Him.\" Umm Salamah said: \"So they left him, disgraced and with their mission rejected, and we stayed with him in the best of abodes, with the best of neighbors.\"",location:"Abyssinia",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/19",icon:"heart"},{id:20,year:"616 CE",hijri:"7  BH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The Islam of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him.",desc:"Al-Tirmidhi narrated in his Sunan, on the authority of Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"O Allah, strengthen Islam with the more beloved of these two men to You: Abu Jahl or Umar bin Al-Khattab.\" He said: \"And Umar was the more beloved of them to Him.\" The biographers narrated that Umar once went out, girded with his sword, intending to eliminate the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). He met a man who asked him, \"Where are you going, Umar?\" He replied, \"I intend to kill Muhammad.\" The man said, \"How can you be safe from Banu Hashim and Banu Zuhrah if you kill Muhammad?\" Umar said to him, \"I see that you have become a Sabian and abandoned the religion you were upon.\" He replied, \"Shall I not guide you to something astonishing, O Umar? Your sister and your brother-in-law have become Sabians and abandoned the religion you are upon.\" So Umar went to them, and Khabbab bin Al-Aratt was with them, holding a parchment containing Surah Taha, reading it to them. When Khabbab heard Umar's footsteps, he hid in the house, and Umar's sister, Fatima, concealed the parchment. Umar had heard Khabbab's recitation to them as he approached the house. When he entered, he asked, \"What is this murmuring I heard from you?\" They replied, \"Nothing but conversation we were having amongst ourselves.\" He said, \"Perhaps you have both become Sabians?\" His brother-in-law said to him, \"O Umar, what if the truth is in a religion other than yours?\" Umar lunged at his brother-in-law and beat him severely. His sister came and pulled him away from her husband, and he struck her with his hand, causing her face to bleed. She said, in anger, \"O Umar, if the truth is in a religion other than yours, I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.\" When Umar despaired and saw the blood on his sister's face, he regretted it and felt ashamed. He said, \"Give me this book you have so I can read it.\" His sister said, \"You are impure, and none shall touch it except the purified. Get up and perform ghusl.\" So he got up and performed ghusl, then took the book and read: \"In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful.\" He said, \"These are beautiful and pure names.\" Then he read Taha until he reached the verse: \"Indeed, I am Allah. There is no god but I, so worship Me and establish prayer for My remembrance.\" He said, \"How beautiful and noble is this speech! Guide me to Muhammad.\" He went to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and embraced Islam in his presence. Then the Muslims went out with him in two rows until they entered the Masjid. When the Quraysh saw them, they were overcome with a sadness they had never experienced before.",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/20",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:21,year:"616 CE",hijri:"6  BH",month:"",title:"The Islam of Tufayl ibn Amr al-Dawsi, may Allah be pleased with him.",desc:"The Quraysh used to warn people against listening to the Prophet, peace be upon him. When Tufayl ibn Amr arrived in Mecca, men from Quraysh approached him. Tufayl was a noble man, a poet, and intelligent. They said to him: \"O Tufayl, you have come to our land, and this man who is among us has caused us great trouble. He has divided our community and scattered our affairs. His words are like magic that separates a man from his father, a man from his brother, and a man from his wife. We fear for you and your people what has befallen us. So do not speak to him, nor listen to anything he says.\" He said: \"By God, they continued to persuade me until I resolved not to listen to him or speak to him. When I went to the mosque, I stuffed cotton into my ears, fearing that some of his words might reach me, as I did not want to hear them. I went to the mosque and saw the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, praying by the Kaaba. I stood near him, but God willed that I should hear some of his words. I heard beautiful speech. I said to myself: Woe is me! By God, I am an intelligent and poetic man, and I can distinguish the good from the bad. What prevents me from listening to what this man says? If what he brings is good, I will accept it, and if it is bad, I will abandon it.\" He said: \"I stayed until the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, went to his house. I followed him, and when he entered his house, I entered after him. I said: 'O Muhammad, your people told me such and such, and they kept frightening me about you until I plugged my ears with cotton so as not to hear your words. Then God willed that I should hear your words, and I heard beautiful speech. Present your message to me.'\" He said: \"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, presented Islam to me and recited the Quran to me. By God, I have never heard more beautiful speech, nor a more just command. So I embraced Islam and bore witness to the truth. I said: 'O Prophet of God, I am a man who is obeyed by my people, and I am returning to them to call them to Islam. Pray to God to grant me a sign that will help me in calling them to it.' He said: 'O God, grant him a sign.'\" He said: \"I went out to my people. When I reached a mountain pass that overlooked the settled people, a light appeared between my eyes like a lamp. I said: 'O God, not on my face. I fear they will think it is a disfigurement on my face for leaving their religion.' He said: 'It moved and fell on the head of my staff.' He said: 'The people below began to see that light on my staff like a hanging lamp, as I descended to them from the pass. When I reached them, I stayed among them.' When I dismounted, my father, an old man, came to me. I said: 'Stay away from me, my father. I am not of you, and you are not of me.' He said: 'Why, my son?' I said: 'I have embraced Islam and followed the religion of Muhammad, peace be upon him.' He said: 'My son, my religion is your religion.' I said: 'Go and wash yourself and purify your clothes, then come, and I will teach you what I have learned.' He went, washed himself, purified his clothes, then came, and I presented Islam to him, and he embraced Islam. Then my wife came to me. I said: 'Stay away from me. I am not of you, and you are not of me.' She said: 'Why? May my father and mother be sacrificed for you.' I said: 'Islam has separated me from you, and I have followed the religion of Muhammad, peace be upon him.' She said: 'My religion is your religion.' I said: 'Go and purify yourself.' She went and washed herself, then came, and I presented Islam to her, and she embraced Islam. Then I called Daws to Islam, but they were slow to respond. Then I came to the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, in Mecca, and said to him: 'O Prophet of God, fornication has overcome Daws. Pray to God against them.' He said: 'O God, guide Daws. Return to your people and call them, and be gentle with them.'\" He said: \"I continued in the land of Daws, calling them to Islam, until the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, migrated to Medina, and Badr, Uhud, and the Trench passed. Then I came to the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, with those of my people who had embraced Islam with me, while the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, was in Khaybar. I arrived in Medina with seventy or eighty households from Daws, then we joined the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, in Khaybar, and he gave us a share along with the Muslims.\" He continued to be with the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, until God granted him victory in Mecca. He was with him in Medina until God took his soul. When the Arabs apostatized, he went out with the Muslims and marched with them until they finished with Tulayha and all the land of Najd. Then he marched with the Muslims to Yamama and was martyred there.",location:"Makkah Al-Mukarramah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/21",icon:"dove"},{id:22,year:"616 CE",hijri:"6  BH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The Quraish siege of the Muslims in the Shi'b Abi Talib.",desc:"When Quraysh resolved to kill the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, all of Banu Abd al-Muttalib agreed to take the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, into their valley and protect him there. It is narrated from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said on the morning of the Day of Sacrifice, while he was in Mina: \"Tomorrow we will descend in the valley of Banu Kinanah, where they swore against disbelief.\" This refers to al-Muhassab, and that is because Quraysh and Kinanah allied against Banu Hashim and Banu Abd al-Muttalib, or Banu al-Muttalib, to not marry them or trade with them until they handed over the Prophet, peace be upon him, to them. So they all entered the valley, both the Muslims and the disbelievers among them. The polytheists united in their decision not to sit with them, associate with them, trade with them, or enter their homes, until they handed over the Messenger of Allah for killing. They wrote a document to that effect. Banu Hashim remained in their valley for three years, and the affliction, hardship, and hunger intensified upon them. When three years had passed, some men from Quraysh blamed each other for what had happened and agreed to annul the document. Thus, the boycott ended.",location:"The Shi'b of Abu Talib",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/22",icon:"hand-holding-heart"},{id:23,year:"619 CE",hijri:"3  BH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The Quraysh's breach of the Charter of Medina.",desc:"After two or three years of the unjust siege in the valley of Abu Talib, the document was annulled and the siege was lifted. This was because the Quraysh were divided between those who were pleased with this pact and those who disliked it. So, those who disliked it sought to annul the document. The one who took the initiative was Hisham ibn Amr from Banu Amer ibn Lu'ayy. He used to secretly supply the Banu Hashim in the valley with food at night. He went to Zuhayr ibn Abi Umayyah al-Makhzumi, whose mother was Atikah bint Abd al-Muttalib, and said: \"O Zuhayr, are you content to eat food and drink, while your maternal uncles are in the state you know?\" He replied: \"Woe to you, what can I do? I am only one man. By God, if I had another man with me, I would stand up to annul it.\" He said: \"You have found a man.\" He asked: \"Who is it?\" He replied: \"I am.\" Zuhayr then said to him: \"Find us a third man.\" So, he went to Al-Mu'tim ibn Adi, reminded him of the kinship ties between Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib, sons of Abd Manaf, and blamed him for agreeing with the Quraysh on this injustice. Al-Mu'tim said: \"Woe to you, what can I do? I am only one man.\" He said: \"You have found a second.\" He asked: \"Who is it?\" He replied: \"I am.\" He said: \"Find us a third.\" He said: \"I have done so.\" He asked: \"Who is it?\" He replied: \"Zuhayr ibn Abi Umayyah.\" He said: \"Find us a fourth.\" So, he went to Abu al-Bukhtari ibn Hisham and said to him much the same as he had said to Al-Mu'tim. He asked: \"Is there anyone who will help with this?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He asked: \"Who is it?\" He replied: \"Zuhayr ibn Abi Umayyah, Al-Mu'tim ibn Adi, and I am with you.\" He said: \"Find us a fifth.\" So, he went to Zam'ah ibn al-Aswad ibn al-Muttalib ibn Asad, spoke to him, and reminded him of their kinship and their rights. He asked him: \"Is there anyone else for this matter you are calling me to?\" He said: \"Yes.\" Then he named the people to him. They gathered at al-Hajun and pledged to stand up to annul the document. Zuhayr said: \"I will start with you, so I will be the first to speak.\" When morning came, they went to their assemblies. Zuhayr went, wearing a garment, circled the Kaaba seven times, then turned to the people and said: \"O people of Mecca, do we eat food and wear clothes while Banu Hashim are perishing, and no one buys from or sells to them? By God, I will not sit down until this cutting and unjust document is torn.\" Abu Jahl, who was in a corner of the mosque, said: \"You lie, by God, it will not be torn.\" Zam'ah ibn al-Aswad said: \"By God, you are the liar. We did not agree to its writing when it was written.\" Abu al-Bukhtari said: \"Zam'ah has spoken the truth. We do not agree with what was written in it, nor do we acknowledge it.\" Al-Mu'tim ibn Adi said: \"You both have spoken the truth, and whoever says otherwise is a liar. We absolve ourselves before God of it and of what was written in it.\" Hisham ibn Amr said something similar. Abu Jahl then said: \"This is a matter decided at night, and discussed elsewhere.\" Abu Talib was sitting in a corner of the mosque. They came to them because God had informed His Messenger, peace be upon him, about the document and that He had sent the termite upon it, and it had eaten everything in it of injustice, severance, and oppression, except for the mention of God, the Mighty and Majestic. He informed his uncle, who went to the Quraysh and told them that his nephew had said such and such. He said: \"If he is lying, we will leave you to deal with him, and if he is telling the truth, you will cease your severance and injustice towards us.\" They said: \"You have been fair.\" After the discussion between the people and Abu Jahl, Al-Mu'tim stood up to tear the document, and found that the termite had eaten it except for \"(In Your Name, O Allah),\" and whatever mention of God was in it, it had not eaten. Then the document was annulled, and the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, and those with him, came out of the valley. The polytheists had seen a great sign of his prophethood, but they were as God described them: \"(And if they see a sign, they turn away and say, 'This is continuous magic.')\" They turned away from this sign and increased their disbelief.",location:"Al-Hajun",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/23",icon:"book"},{id:24,year:"620 CE",hijri:"3  BH",month:"\u0631\u0645\u0636\u0627\u0646",title:"The passing of the Mother of the Believers, Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, may Allah be pleased with her.",desc:"She is Khadijah, daughter of Khuwaylid, son of Asad, son of Abd al-Uzza, son of Qusai, son of Kilab, among the best women of this world. Gabriel came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, Khadijah has come to you with a vessel containing food, or drink. When she comes to you, convey to her greetings from her Lord and from me, and give her glad tidings of a house in Paradise made of reeds, wherein there will be no noise nor toil.\" Khadijah was the first to embrace Islam with the Prophet, peace be upon him. She believed in him when people disbelieved, she sheltered him when people rejected him, and she affirmed him when people denied him. Allah blessed him with children through her: Al-Qasim, At-Tayyib, and At-Tahir, who died in infancy, and Ruqayyah, Zaynab, Umm Kulthum, and Fatimah. May Allah be pleased with her. She passed away, along with Abu Talib, in the same year. The calamities followed one another for the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, with their deaths. Khadijah was a minister of truth for Islam, and the Prophet, peace be upon him, found solace in her. \"Khadijah passed away three years before the Prophet, peace be upon him, migrated to Medina. He remained for two years, or close to that, and married Aisha when she was six years old, and then consummated the marriage when she was nine years old.\" The Mother of the Believers, Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, may Allah be pleased with her, passed away in Mecca and was buried in Al-Hajun.",location:"Al-Hajun",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/24",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:25,year:"620 CE",hijri:"3  BH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The departure of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, to Ta'if.",desc:"The Prophet, peace be upon him, went out to Taif, which is about sixty miles from Mecca. He walked there and back on foot, accompanied by his freed slave Zayd ibn Harithah. Whenever he passed a tribe on the way, he invited them to Islam, but none responded. When he reached Taif, he approached its leaders and invited them, but they did not respond. He stayed among the people of Taif for ten days, visiting every one of their nobles and speaking to them. They told him, \"Leave our land.\" They incited their fools against him. When he intended to leave, the fools and their slaves followed him, insulting and shouting at him, until the people gathered against him. They formed two lines and began to stone him and hurl insults at him, and they stoned his heels until his sandals were stained with blood. Zayd ibn Harithah protected him with his own body, sustaining a head wound. The fools continued to harass him until they forced him to take refuge in a garden belonging to Utbah and Shaybah, sons of Rabi'ah, three miles from Taif. The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, returned on the road to Mecca after leaving the garden, dejected and sorrowful, with a broken heart. When he reached Qarn al-Manazil, God sent Gabriel to him, accompanied by the Angel of the Mountains, asking if he wished to crush the people of Mecca between the two mountains. The Prophet, peace be upon him, replied, \"Rather, I hope that God, the Almighty, will bring forth from their loins those who worship God, the Almighty, alone, associating nothing with Him.\" In this response given by the Messenger, peace be upon him, his unique character and his great moral disposition are evident.",location:"Ta'if",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/25",icon:"route"},{id:26,year:"620 CE",hijri:"3  BH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The Islam of the Jinn and their coming in delegations to the Prophet, peace be upon him.",desc:"When the Prophet, peace be upon him, returned from Ta'if to Mecca, he stopped at Nakhlah. In the middle of the night, he rose to pray. The group of jinn, whom Allah the Almighty mentioned, passed by him. They were seven jinn from the people of Nasibin. They listened to the recitation of the Messenger, peace be upon him. When he finished his prayer, they turned back to their people as warners, having believed and responded to what they heard. Regarding them, Allah the Almighty revealed: \"And [mention, O Muhammad], when We directed to you a few of the jinn to listen to the Quran. When they attended it, they said, 'Be silent.' And when it was finished, they went back to their people, warning. They said, 'O our people, indeed we have heard a Book revealed after Moses, confirming that which was before it, guiding to the truth and to a straight path. O our people, respond to the one who calls to Allah and believe in him. He will forgive for you your sins and protect you from a painful punishment. And whoever does not respond to the one who calls to Allah will not cause failure on earth, and he will not have besides Him protectors. Those are in clear error.'\" [Al-Ahqaf]. And after several months from the meeting of the first delegation of jinn with the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, the second delegation came, eager to see the beloved chosen one, peace be upon him, and to listen to the words of the Lord of the worlds.",location:"Palm tree",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/26",icon:"flag"},{id:27,year:"620 CE",hijri:"3  BH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The death of Abu Talib, the uncle of the Prophet, peace be upon him.",desc:"After the illness intensified for Abu Talib, the uncle of the Prophet, peace be upon him, who had taken care of him when he was young, supported him when he was older, aided him in his call, and protected him from the harm of the polytheists, the Prophet, peace be upon him, entered upon him. Abu Jahl was with him. The Prophet said: \"O uncle, say: 'There is no god but Allah,' a word for which I will argue on your behalf before Allah.\" Abu Jahl and Abdullah ibn Abi Umayyah said: \"O Abu Talib, do you turn away from the religion of Abdul Muttalib?\" They continued to speak to him until the last thing he said to them was: \"On the religion of Abdul Muttalib.\" Then the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"I will surely ask forgiveness for you as long as I am not forbidden.\" Then it was revealed: {It is not for the Prophet and those who have believed to ask forgiveness for the polytheists, even if they were relatives, after it has become clear to them that they are companions of the Hellfire.} And it was revealed: {Indeed, you do not guide whom you love.} Abu Talib had been the fortress behind which the Prophet, peace be upon him, took refuge from the attacks of the prominent and the foolish, but he remained upon the religion of the elders among his forefathers, and thus did not succeed.",location:"Makkah al-Mukarramah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/27",icon:"mountain"},{id:28,year:"620 CE",hijri:"3  BH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The marriage of the Prophet, peace be upon him, to Sawdah bint Zam'ah, may Allah be pleased with her.",desc:"After the death of the Mother of the Believers, Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, may Allah be pleased with her, who was one of Allah's great blessings upon the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, married Sawdah bint Zam'ah ibn Qays ibn 'Abd Shams ibn 'Abd Wudd ibn Nasr ibn Malik ibn Hisl ibn 'Amir ibn Lu'ayy, al-Qurashiyyah. He married her in Mecca before the Hijra. She was among those who embraced Islam early and migrated the second migration to Abyssinia. Her husband was al-Sakran ibn 'Amr, who had embraced Islam and migrated with her. He died in the land of Abyssinia, or after returning to Mecca. When her waiting period ended, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, proposed to her and married her. She was the first woman he married after the death of Khadijah. She was in her sixth decade of life at that time. When the Prophet, peace be upon him, intended to divorce her, she reconciled with him by gifting her turn to Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, so he kept her. She did not bear him any children until he passed away, peace be upon him.",location:"Honorable Mecca",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/28",icon:"sun"},{id:29,year:"620 CE",hijri:"3  BH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The beginning of the Ansar's entry into Islam.",desc:"When Allah, the Almighty, willed to manifest His religion, honor His Prophet, peace be upon him, and fulfill His promise to him, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, went out during the Hajj season, presenting himself to the Arab tribes, as he had done in every season. While he was at Al-Aqabah, he met a group of Khazraj whom Allah intended good for. He asked them, \"Who are you?\" They replied, \"A group of Khazraj.\" He asked, \"Are you allies of the Jews?\" They said, \"Yes.\" He then asked, \"Will you sit with me so I may speak to you?\" They replied, \"Certainly.\" So they sat with him, and he invited them to Allah, the Almighty, presented Islam to them, and recited the Quran to them. Among the favors Allah bestowed upon them in Islam was that the Jews were with them in their land, and they used to warn them of the imminent appearance of a prophet whom they would follow, and then with him, they would kill the Arabs with a decisive victory. When the Prophet, peace be upon him, spoke to them and invited them to Allah, some of them said to each other, \"O people, know that, by Allah, this is the prophet whom the Jews warned you about. So do not let them get to him before you.\" They responded to his invitation and said to him, \"We have left our people, and there is no people between us who have as much enmity and conflict as they do. Perhaps Allah will unite them with you. We will return and invite them to your cause, and present to them what we have accepted from this religion. If Allah unites them with you, no man will be more honored than you.\" Then they departed, returning to their land, having believed and testified. When they arrived in Medina to their people, they spoke of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and invited them to Islam until it spread among them. There was no house among the Ansar that did not have mention of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him.",location:"Al-Aqabah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/29",icon:"users"},{id:30,year:"620 CE",hijri:"2  BH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The marriage of the Prophet to Aisha, daughter of Abu Bakr Al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with them both.",desc:"The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, married Aisha the Truthful, may Allah be pleased with her, when she was six years old, and consummated the marriage with her in Medina in Shawwal in the second year of the Hijra, when she was nine years old. She was the most favored and beloved of his wives to him, and he did not marry any virgin other than her.",location:"Al-Madinah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/30",icon:"scroll"},{id:31,year:"621 CE",hijri:"2  BH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The First Pledge of al-Aqabah",desc:"It was narrated from `Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, one of the chieftains on the night of `Aqabah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, his family, and his companions, said while a group of his companions were around him: \"Pledge allegiance to me on the condition that you will not associate anything with Allah, will not steal, will not commit adultery, will not kill your children, will not utter slander which you have forged between your hands and your feet, and will not disobey in what is right. Whoever among you fulfills this, his reward is with Allah. Whoever commits any of these acts and is punished in this world, it will be an expiation for him. Whoever commits any of these acts and Allah conceals him, then it is up to Allah; if He wills, He will forgive him, and if He wills, He will punish him.\" He said: \"So we pledged allegiance to him on that basis.\"",location:"Al-Aqabah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/31",icon:"shield"},{id:32,year:"621 CE",hijri:"1  BH",month:"",title:"The Night Journey, the Ascension, and the Obligation of the Five Daily Prayers.",desc:"In this great incident, the Prophet, peace be upon him, saw the prophets, and saw the Lote tree of the Utmost Boundary, and other great signs. In it, the five daily prayers were ordained, and this is something agreed upon by the narrations of the two Sahih collections. The Quraysh denied the occurrence of the incident of the Night Journey and Ascension, and this is also something agreed upon by the narrations of the two Sahih collections. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: \"I saw myself in the Hijr, and the Quraysh were asking me about my Night Journey. They asked me about things from Jerusalem that I had not retained. So I was distressed with a distress the like of which I had never experienced.\" He said: \"Then Allah raised it for me to look at. Whatever they asked me about, I informed them. I saw myself in a gathering of prophets. There was Moses, standing and praying. He was a man of strong build, curly-haired, as if from the men of Shin\u016b'ah. And there was Jesus, son of Mary, peace be upon him, standing and praying. The person most resembling him in likeness was Urwah ibn Mas'ud al-Thaqafi. And there was Abraham, peace be upon him, standing and praying. The person most resembling him was your companion\" - meaning himself. Then the time for prayer came, and I led them in prayer. When I finished the prayer, someone said: \"O Muhammad, this is Malik, the keeper of Hellfire. Greet him.\" So I turned to him, and he greeted me first.\"",location:"Unspecified",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/32",icon:"moon"},{id:33,year:"621 CE",hijri:"1  BH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The Hijra of Abu Salamah and Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with them.",desc:"Abu Salamah Abdullah ibn Abd al-Asad was the first of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, among the Emigrants from Quraysh from Banu Makhzum, to migrate to Medina. His migration took place after the first pledge of Aqabah and before the second pledge of Aqabah, when the Quraysh had harmed him after his return from Abyssinia. He resolved to return there, and then it reached him that they had brothers in Medina, so he decided to migrate to them. Umm Salamah said: When Abu Salamah decided to go to Medina, he prepared a camel for me, then placed me upon it, and put my son Salamah ibn Abi Salamah in my lap. Then he went out leading my camel, and the Banu Abd al-Asad went with him, while the Banu Mughirah detained me with them, and my husband Abu Salamah went to Medina. She said: So I was separated from my son and my husband. She said: I used to go out every morning and sit in al-Ab\u1e6da\u1e25, and I would not stop crying until evening, for a year or close to it, until a man from my paternal uncles, one of the Banu Mughirah, passed by me and saw my state and took pity on me. He said to the Banu Mughirah: Will you not release this poor woman? Have you separated her from her husband and her child? She said: They then said to me: Go to your husband if you wish. She said: At that, the Banu Abd al-Asad returned my son to me. She said: So I prepared my camel, then took my son and placed him in my lap, then I set out to go to my husband in Medina. She said: And I had no one from God's creation with me, until when I reached al-Tan'im, I met Uthman ibn Talhah ibn Abi Talhah, brother of Banu Abd al-Dar. He said: Where are you going, O daughter of Umm Ayyah? I said: I am going to my husband in Medina. He said: Do you not have anyone with you? I said: I have no one with me except Allah and this son of mine. He said: By Allah, you are not to be left behind. He took the camel's reins and set out with me, hurrying me along. By Allah, I have never accompanied any man among the Arabs whom I considered more honorable than him. When he reached a stopping place, he would make the camel kneel, then he would move away from me until I dismounted. When I dismounted, he would move his camel away, unload it, then tie it to a tree. Then he would move to a tree and lie down beneath it. When it was time to depart, he would stand by my camel, prepare it, then move away from me and say: Mount. When I mounted and settled on my camel, he would come, take the reins, and lead me until he dismounted me. He continued to do this for me until he brought me to Medina. When he saw the village of Banu Amr ibn Awf in Quba, he said: Your husband is in this village. Abu Salamah was residing there, so enter it with the blessing of Allah. Then he returned to Mecca. Umm Salamah used to say: I do not know of any family in Islam that suffered what the family of Abu Salamah suffered, and I have never seen a companion more honorable than Uthman ibn Talhah. Uthman ibn Talhah ibn Abi Talhah al-Abdari embraced Islam after Hudaybiyyah.",location:"The City",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/33",icon:"star"},{id:34,year:"621 CE",hijri:"1  BH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The spread of Islam in Yathrib after the First Pledge of Aqabah.",desc:"After the first Pledge of Aqabah, the Ansar wrote to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, asking him to send someone to lead them in prayer and teach them the Quran. So, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sent them Mus'ab ibn Umayr. He stayed as a guest of Sa'd ibn Zurarah and began to call people to Islam. Islam spread among the people of Yathrib until there was no house in Yathrib that did not have a Muslim man or woman. Among those who embraced Islam at his hands were the two chiefs of the Aws, Sa'd ibn Mu'adh and Usayd ibn Hudayr.",location:"Yathrib",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/34",icon:"child"},{id:35,year:"622 CE",hijri:"1  BH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The Second Pledge of al-Aqabah.",desc:"Jabir reported: \"The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, stayed in Mecca for ten years, approaching people in their dwellings, at 'Ukaz, Majanna, and during the seasons in Mina, saying: 'Who will host me? Who will support me so that I may convey the message of my Lord, and for him shall be Paradise?' It reached the point that a man would come from Yemen or from Muzar, and his people would say to him: 'Beware of the Qurayshite youth, lest he tempt you.' He would walk among their men, and they would point at him with their fingers. Until Allah sent us from Yathrib to him, so we hosted him and believed in him. A man from us would go out, believe in him, and teach him the Quran. Then he would return to his family, and they would embrace Islam with his Islam, until there was no house among the houses of the Ansar but had a group of Muslims openly declaring Islam. Then we all consulted and said: 'How long shall we leave the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, to be driven away in the mountains of Mecca and live in fear?' So seventy men from us set out to him, until they met him during the season, and we promised him at the valley of al-'Aqabah. We gathered around him, a man or two at a time, until we were complete. We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we will pledge allegiance to you.' He said: 'You will pledge allegiance to me on hearing and obeying, in times of eagerness and reluctance, on spending in times of hardship and ease, on enjoining good and forbidding evil, and that you speak the truth about Allah, fearing no blame from any blamer. And that you support me, so you protect me when I come to you, just as you protect yourselves, your wives, and your children, and for you shall be Paradise.' He said: 'So we stood up to him and pledged allegiance. Al-As'ad ibn Zurarah, who was one of the youngest, took his hand and said: 'Hold on, O people of Yathrib. We did not travel on the backs of camels except that we knew he was the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. And his departure today means the separation of all Arabs, the killing of your best men, and that swords will bite you. So if you are people who can endure this, your reward is with Allah. But if you fear weakness in yourselves, then make it clear, and that is an excuse for you with Allah.' They said: 'Remove it from us, O As'ad. By Allah, we will never abandon this pledge, nor will we ever break it.' He said: 'So we stood up to him and pledged allegiance, and he took our pledge and stipulated conditions, and in return for that, he promised us Paradise.'\"",location:"The Pass of 'Aqabah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/35",icon:"heart"},{id:36,year:"622 CE",hijri:"0 AH",month:"",title:"The miracle of the splitting of the moon.",desc:"From Abdullah bin Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The moon was split into two parts during the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Bear witness.\" And from Anas bin Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the people of Mecca asked the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, to show them a sign, so he showed them the moon split into two parts, until they saw Hira between them. And in Sahih Muslim, from Abdullah bin Mas'ud, who said: The moon was split into two halves during the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. The mountain covered one half, and one half was above the mountain. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: \"O Allah, bear witness.\" It is widely known that the splitting of the moon occurred only once. As for what is mentioned in one of the narrations of Muslim: (...so he showed them the splitting of the moon twice), scholars have responded to it. Ibn al-Qayyim, Ibn Hajar, and others have favored that it occurred only once.",location:"Makkah Al-Mukarramah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/36",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:37,year:"622 CE",hijri:"1 AH",month:"",title:"The Islam of Abdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him.",desc:"Abdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, was a distinguished scholar among the Jews, a learned man. He said: \"When I heard about the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, I recognized his description, his name, and the time we were expecting him. I kept this to myself, remaining silent about it, until the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, arrived in Medina. When he stayed in Quba, among the Banu Amr ibn Awf, a man came and informed me of his arrival. I was at the top of my palm tree, working on it, and my aunt, Kh\u0101lidah bint al-\u1e24\u0101rith, was sitting below me. When I heard the news of the Messenger of Allah's arrival, peace and blessings be upon him, I exclaimed 'Allahu Akbar!' My aunt said to me upon hearing my exclamation: 'May Allah disappoint you! By God, if you had heard of Moses son of Imran arriving, you would not have done more!' I said to her: 'O aunt, he is, by God, the brother of Moses son of Imran, and on his religion, he has been sent with what he was sent with.' She said: 'O my nephew, is he the Prophet we were told would be sent with the coming of the Hour?' I said to her: 'Yes.' She said: 'Then that is he.' Then I went out to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and embraced Islam. Then I returned to my household and commanded them, and they embraced Islam. I concealed my Islam from the Jews. Then I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and said to him: 'O Messenger of Allah, the Jews are a deceitful people, and I would like you to admit me into one of your houses, and conceal me from them, and then ask them about me, so that they may tell you what I am like among them, before they know of my Islam. For if they know of it, they will deceive me and criticize me.' So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, admitted me into one of his houses, and they came to him and spoke with him and questioned him. Then he said to them: 'What kind of man is Abdullah ibn Salam among you?' They said: 'Our master and son of our master, our scholar and learned man.' When they finished speaking, I came out to them and said: 'O assembly of Jews, fear Allah and accept what has come to you. For by God, you know that he is indeed the Messenger of Allah. You find him written among you in the Torah by his name and description. I bear witness that he is the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and I believe in him, and I affirm him, and I recognize him.' They said: 'You lie.' Then they attacked me. I said to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him: 'Did I not tell you, O Messenger of Allah, that they are a deceitful people, a people of treachery, lies, and wickedness!' Then I revealed my Islam and the Islam of my household, and my aunt Kh\u0101lidah bint al-\u1e24\u0101rith embraced Islam, and her Islam was good.\"",location:"The City",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/37",icon:"dove"},{id:38,year:"622 CE",hijri:"1 AH",month:"",title:"The Prophet's peace be upon him treaty with the Jews.",desc:"Ibn Ishaq said: \"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, wrote a document between the Emigrants and the Helpers, and in it he made peace with the Jews, covenanted with them, confirmed them in their religion and their property, and stipulated conditions for them and they for him.\" This was when they refused to follow him, and this was before permission was given to fight and to take the jizya from those who refused Islam. Indeed, the majority of them refused except for disbelief. They were three tribes: Banu Qaynuqa, Banu an-Nadir, and Banu Qurayza. All three fought him. He showed favor to Banu Qaynuqa, exiled Banu an-Nadir, and killed Banu Qurayza, taking their descendants captive. Surah Al-Hashr was revealed concerning Banu an-Nadir, and Surah Al-Ahzab concerning Banu Qurayza.",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/38",icon:"hand-holding-heart"},{id:39,year:"622 CE",hijri:"1 AH",month:"",title:"The permissibility of the Adhan.",desc:"Ibn Umar narrated that when the Muslims arrived in Medina, they used to gather and wait for the prayers, but no one called them to prayer. One day, they discussed this matter. Some of them said, \"Let's take a bell like the Christians' bell.\" Others said, \"Let's take a horn like the Jews' horn.\" Abdullah bin Zayd said: \"When the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, ordered that a bell be made to strike for people to gather for prayer, a man carrying a bell in his hand passed by me while I was asleep. I said to him, 'O servant of Allah, will you sell the bell?' He asked, 'What will you do with it?' I replied, 'We will call people to prayer with it.' He said, 'Shall I not guide you to something better than that?' I said, 'Yes.' He then said: 'You should say: Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar. Ash-hadu an la ilaha illallah, Ash-hadu an la ilaha illallah. Ash-hadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah, Ash-hadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah. Hayya 'alas-Salah, Hayya 'alas-Salah. Hayya 'alal-Falah, Hayya 'alal-Falah. Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar. La ilaha illallah.' Then he moved a short distance away from me and said: 'And when you commence the prayer, say: Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar. Ash-hadu an la ilaha illallah. Ash-hadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah. Hayya 'alas-Salah. Hayya 'alal-Falah. Qad qamatis-Salah, Qad qamatis-Salah. Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar. La ilaha illallah.'\" When morning came, I went to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and told him what I had seen. He said, \"This is a true vision, God willing. Go with Bilal and tell him what you saw, and let him call to prayer with it, for he has a more resonant voice than you.\" So I went with Bilal and told him what I had seen, and he called to prayer with it. Umar bin Al-Khattab heard this while he was in his house. He came out dragging his cloak, saying, \"By Him Who sent you with the truth, O Messenger of Allah, I saw the same as he saw.\" The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said, \"Praise be to Allah.\"",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/39",icon:"book"},{id:40,year:"622 CE",hijri:"1 AH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The Hijra of the Companions to Medina.",desc:"When the Pledge of Al-Aqabah was completed, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, ordered those who were with him to migrate to Medina. So they left in groups. Among the first to migrate after the second Al-Aqabah was Mus'ab ibn Umayr, then 'Amir ibn Rab'ah, an ally of Banu 'Adi ibn Ka'b, with his wife Layla bint Abi Hathmah ibn Ghanam. Then 'Abdullah ibn Jahsh with his family and his brother 'Abd ibn Jahsh, Abu Ahmad, who was blind. Their dwelling and the dwelling of Abu Salamah were in Quba' among Banu 'Amr ibn 'Awf. When all of Banu Jahsh migrated with their women, Abu Sufyan seized their homes and took possession of them. 'Abdullah ibn Jahsh mentioned this to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, when he learned of it. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said to him: \"Are you not pleased, O 'Abdullah, that Allah will give you a house in Paradise better than it?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Then it is for you.\" When the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, conquered Mecca, Abu Ahmad spoke to him about their homes. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, delayed him. The people said to Abu Ahmad: \"O Abu Ahmad, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, dislikes that you should return concerning something that befell you for the sake of Allah.\" So he refrained from speaking to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. And Banu Ghanam ibn Duudan were people of Islam who had all migrated to Medina. Then 'Umar ibn Al-Khattab and 'Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah migrated with twenty riders and arrived in Medina. Hisham ibn Al-'As ibn Wa'il had embraced Islam and had agreed with 'Umar ibn Al-Khattab to migrate with him, saying: \"You will find me or I will find you at the watering place of Banu Ghifar.\" His people realized this and prevented him from migrating. Then Abu Jahl and Al-Harith ibn Hisham left until they arrived in Medina while the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, was in Mecca. They spoke to 'Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah, who was their brother by their mother and their cousin, and informed him that their mother had vowed not to wash her head or seek shade until she saw him. His heart softened, and he believed them and set out returning with them. They then bound him on the road and brought him to Mecca, in broad daylight, bound. Then they said: \"O people of Mecca, do this to your foolish ones as we have done to our foolish one.\" So they imprisoned him there until Allah, the Exalted, delivered him afterward through the supplication of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, in the Qunut of prayer: \"O Allah, save Al-Walid ibn Al-Walid, and Salamah ibn Hisham, and 'Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah.\" When Suhayb ibn Sinan intended to migrate, the disbelievers of Quraysh said to him: \"You came to us as a penniless, insignificant person, and your wealth increased among us, and you reached what you reached, and now you want to leave with your wealth and yourself. No, by Allah, that will not happen.\" Suhayb said to them: \"What if I give you all my wealth, will you let me go?\" They said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Then I have given it to you.\" This reached the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and he said: \"Suhayb has profited, Suhayb has profited.\" Then the emigrants continued to migrate to Medina until the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, migrated.",location:"The City",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/40",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:41,year:"622 CE",hijri:"1 AH",month:"\u0635\u0641\u0631",title:"The polytheists' meeting in Dar al-Nadwah and their agreement to kill the Prophet, peace be upon him.",desc:"When the polytheists saw that the companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, had prepared and departed, carrying and driving their families, children, and wealth to the Aws and Khazraj in Yathrib, they were overcome by gloom and sorrow, and anxiety and worry besieged them. The chieftains of Mecca gathered in Dar al-Nadwah to make a decisive decision on this matter. Their meeting took place approximately two and a half months after the Pledge of Aqabah. All the representatives of the Quraysh tribes converged for this meeting to discuss a decisive plan that would ensure the swift elimination of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and his call. When they arrived at Dar al-Nadwah at the appointed time, Iblis intercepted them in the guise of a venerable old man and stood at the door. They asked, \"Who is this old man?\" He replied, \"An old man from Najd who heard about what you have agreed upon and has come to join you to hear what you say, and perhaps he may offer you his opinion and advice.\" They said, \"Yes, enter.\" So he entered with them. After the meeting was complete and a lengthy discussion ensued, Abu al-Aswad said, \"Let us expel him from among us and banish him from our lands, and we will not care where he goes or where he ends up. We will have rectified our affairs and restored our unity as it was.\" The Najdi old man said, \"No, by God, this is not a good opinion for you. Have you not seen the eloquence of his speech, the sweetness of his tongue, and how he has captivated the hearts of men with what he brings? By God, if you do this, you will not be safe from him settling among some Arab tribe, and then marching with them against you, after they have followed him, until he tramples you in your lands and then does to you whatever he wishes. Devise a different plan for him.\" Abu al-Bakhtari said, \"Let us imprison him in chains and lock him behind a door, and then wait for him to meet the fate of those like him among the poets who came before him \u2013 Zuhayr and al-Nabighah \u2013 and those who have passed from this death, until he meets the same end.\" The Najdi old man said, \"No, by God, this is not a good opinion for you. By God, if you imprison him, as you say, his cause will emerge from behind the door you have locked him behind to his companions, and they will soon rise up against you, snatch him from your hands, and then increase their numbers against you until they overcome you in your affairs. This is not a good opinion for you, so consider something else.\" After this, the chief of Mecca's criminals, Abu Jahl ibn Hisham, came forward with a sinful proposal that was agreed upon by all present. Abu Jahl said, \"By God, I have an opinion on this that I do not think you have yet considered.\" They asked, \"And what is it, O Abu al-Hakam?\" He replied, \"I suggest that we take a strong, noble, and respected young man from each tribe. Then we give each of these young men a sharp sword, and they all go to him and strike him with a single blow, killing him. Thus, we will be rid of him. For if they do this, his blood will be dispersed among all the tribes, and the Banu Abd Manaf will not be able to fight all their people. They will then accept blood money from us, and we will pay it to them.\" The Najdi old man said, \"The statement is as the man said. This is the opinion, and there is no other opinion.\" They unanimously agreed to this sinful proposal.",location:"Dar al-Nadwah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/41",icon:"route"},{id:42,year:"622 CE",hijri:"1 AH",month:"\u0635\u0641\u0631",title:"The migration of the Messenger, peace be upon him, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, to Medina.",desc:"Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said to the Muslims: \"I have been shown the land of your emigration, a land of date palms between two lava fields.\" These are the two Harrahs. So, those who had emigrated to Medina did so, and most of those who had emigrated to the land of Abyssinia returned to Medina. Abu Bakr prepared to go to Medina, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said to him: \"Take it easy, for I hope I will be given permission.\" Abu Bakr said: \"May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, do you hope for that?\" He said: \"Yes.\" So Abu Bakr detained himself to accompany the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and he fed two riding beasts he owned with the leaves of the samur tree \u2013 which is called khabat \u2013 for four months. Ibn Shihab said: Urwah said: Aisha said: One day, while we were sitting in Abu Bakr's house at the height of noon, someone said to Abu Bakr: \"Here is the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, veiled, at an hour he does not usually visit us.\" Abu Bakr said: \"May my father and mother be sacrificed for him, by Allah, he has not come at this hour except for something important.\" She said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came and asked for permission, and he was granted permission and entered. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said to Abu Bakr: \"Send out those who are with you.\" Abu Bakr said: \"They are only your family, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah.\" He said: \"I have been given permission to leave.\" Abu Bakr said: \"To accompany you, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah?\" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Yes.\" Abu Bakr said: \"Then take, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah, one of these two riding beasts of mine.\" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"For a price.\" Aisha said: We prepared them with the best possible preparation, and we made provisions for them in a waterskin. Asma bint Abi Bakr cut a piece from her belt and tied it around the mouth of the waterskin. It was because of this that she was named \"Dhat al-Nitaqayn\" (She of the Two Belts). She said: Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and Abu Bakr joined them in a cave on Mount Thawr, and they hid there for three nights. Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, a young, intelligent, and quick-witted boy, would spend the night with them, and then leave them before dawn to be with Quraysh in Mecca as if he had spent the night there. He would hear nothing of any plan devised against them without memorizing it, until he would bring them news of it when darkness fell. Amir ibn Fuhayrah, the freed slave of Abu Bakr, would bring them a herd of sheep for grazing, and bring them to rest with them when an hour after Isha had passed. They would spend the night on the milk of their herd and their drink until Amir ibn Fuhayrah would call them at dawn. He did this every night of those three nights. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and Abu Bakr hired a man from the tribe of Banu al-Dil, who was from Banu Abd ibn Adi, a guide and an expert navigator \u2013 and an expert navigator is one skilled in guidance. He had an alliance with the family of Al-As ibn Wa'il al-Sahmi, and he was on the religion of the disbelievers of Quraysh. So they entrusted him with their riding beasts and agreed to meet him at the cave of Thawr after three nights, with their riding beasts, on the morning of the third day. Amir ibn Fuhayrah and the guide set off with them, and they took the coastal route. Surraqah ibn Ju'shum said: Messengers from the disbelievers of Quraysh came to us, offering a reward for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and Abu Bakr, whoever killed or captured either of them. While I was sitting in a gathering of my people, Banu Mudlij, a man from them came and stood before us while we were sitting, and said: \"O Surraqah, I have just seen some dark shapes on the coast. I think it is Muhammad and his companions.\" Surraqah said: I knew it was them, so I said to him: \"They are not them, but you saw so-and-so and so-and-so. They have gone from our sight.\" I then remained in the gathering for a while, then I got up and went inside and ordered my slave girl to bring out my horse from behind a hill and keep it for me. I took my spear and went out with it from the back of the house, and I struck its tip against the ground, and lowered its upper part, until I reached my horse and mounted it. I urged it on, and it brought me closer, until I was near them. My horse stumbled, and I fell off it. I got up and reached for my quiver, and took out the arrows for divination, and cast lots to see if I would harm them or not. The result I disliked came out. I mounted my horse and disobeyed the arrows, and it brought me closer, until when I heard the recitation of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and he did not look back, and Abu Bakr kept looking back, my horse's legs sank into the ground, up to its knees. I fell off it, then I urged it on and it rose, and it could hardly pull its legs out. When it stood upright, there was a cloud of dust rising into the sky like smoke from the tracks of its legs. I cast lots again, and the result I disliked came out. I called out to them offering safety, and they stopped. I mounted my horse and rode until I reached them. When I encountered what I encountered of being held back from them, it occurred to me that the cause of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, would prevail. I said to him: \"Your people have offered a reward for you, and I informed them of what people intended for them. I offered them provisions and supplies, but they did not take anything from me nor ask me, except that he said: 'Conceal yourselves from us.'\" I asked him to write me a letter of security, so he ordered Amir ibn Fuhayrah to write it on a piece of leather. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, departed. Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, met al-Zubayr with a group of Muslims who were traders returning from Syria. Al-Zubayr clothed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and Abu Bakr in white garments. The Muslims in Medina heard of the Messenger of Allah's departure from Mecca, so they would go out every morning to the Harrah and wait for him until the heat of noon drove them back. One day, after waiting for a long time, they returned. When they returned to their homes, a man from the Jews climbed one of their fortresses to look at something. He saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and his companions, dressed in white, shimmering in the heat haze. The Jew could not help but say in a loud voice: \"O Arabs, this is your ancestor whom you await!\" The Muslims rushed to their weapons and met the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, at the back of the Harrah. He turned them to the right, until he settled them in the territory of Banu Amr ibn Awf. This was on Monday in the month of Rabi al-Awwal. Abu Bakr stood up for the people, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sat in silence. Those who came from the Ansar \u2013 who had not seen the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him \u2013 began to greet Abu Bakr, until the sun shone on the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. Abu Bakr came and shaded him with his cloak, and then the people recognized the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him.",location:"The City",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/42",icon:"flag"},{id:43,year:"622 CE",hijri:"1 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The construction of the Quba Mosque.",desc:"The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, stayed in the Banu Amr ibn Awf in Quba for over ten nights, and there he founded the mosque which was founded on piety, and the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, prayed in it.",location:"Quba Mosque",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/43",icon:"mountain"},{id:44,year:"622 CE",hijri:"1 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The construction of the Prophet's Mosque.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, mounted his riding beast from Quba and set out, people walking with him until it knelt near the Mosque of the Messenger, peace be upon him, in Medina. On that day, men from among the Muslims were praying there. It was a date palm drying ground belonging to Suhail and Sahl, two orphaned boys under the care of As'ad ibn Zurarah. When his riding beast knelt, the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said, \"This, God willing, is the place of dwelling.\" Then the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, called the two boys and negotiated with them for the drying ground to make it a mosque. They said, \"No, rather we gift it to you, O Messenger of God.\" But the Messenger of God refused to accept it as a gift until he bought it from them. Then he built a mosque. The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, began to carry bricks with them in its construction, and while carrying bricks, he would say, \"This is a burden, not the burden of Khaybar. This is more righteous and purer for our Lord.\" And he would say, \"O God, the reward is the reward of the Hereafter, so have mercy on the Ansar and the Muhajirun.\" Anas, may God be pleased with him, said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, arrived in Medina and stayed in the upper part of the city in a tribe called Banu 'Amr ibn 'Awf. The Prophet, peace be upon him, stayed with them for fourteen nights. Then he sent for Banu an-Najjar, and they came, girded with swords, as if I were looking at the Prophet, peace be upon him, on his riding beast, with Abu Bakr behind him, and the notables of Banu an-Najjar around him, until he arrived at the courtyard of Abu Ayyub. He used to like to pray wherever prayer found him, and to pray in sheep pens. He ordered the construction of the mosque, so he sent for a group of Banu an-Najjar and said, \"O Banu an-Najjar, name your price for this courtyard of yours.\" They said, \"By God, we will not ask for its price except from God.\" Anas said: It contained, as I tell you, the graves of the polytheists, ruins, and palm trees. The Prophet, peace be upon him, ordered the graves of the polytheists to be exhumed, then the ruins to be leveled, and the palm trees to be cut down. They lined up the palm trees as the qiblah of the mosque, made its doorposts of stone, and began to carry rocks, chanting, and the Prophet, peace be upon him, was with them, saying, \"O God, there is no good except the good of the Hereafter, so forgive the Ansar and the Muhajirun.\" Abdullah ibn Umar said: The mosque in the time of the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, was built with bricks, its roof was made of palm fronds, and its columns were made of palm wood.",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/44",icon:"sun"},{id:45,year:"622 CE",hijri:"1 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The brotherhood between the Emigrants and the Helpers.",desc:"Among the consequences of his migration, and that of his Companions, to Medina was the brotherhood established between the Emigrants and the Helpers, may Allah be pleased with them. In the beginning, some of them would inherit from others. It is narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, regarding the verse: {And for each, We have appointed heirs} he said: \"Heirs.\" And regarding: {And those with whom your right hands have covenanted} he said: \"When the Emigrants arrived in Medina, an Emigrant would inherit from an Helper, to the exclusion of his own relatives, due to the brotherhood that the Prophet, peace be upon him, had established between them. When the verse {And for each, We have appointed heirs} was revealed, it abrogated that. Then he said: {And those with whom your right hands have covenanted} meaning: \"Except for support, assistance, and sincere advice. Inheritance was then abolished, but one would still make a will for them.\" Anas narrated: When the Prophet, peace be upon him, arrived in Medina, the Emigrants came to him and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, we have not seen a people more generous than those among whom we have landed, nor more compassionate with little than they are with much. They have spared us hardship and shared their ease with us, to the extent that we fear they will take all the reward.\" The Prophet, peace be upon him, replied: \"Not so, as long as you supplicate to Allah for them and praise them.\" Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When we arrived in Medina, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, established brotherhood between me and Sa'd ibn al-Rabi'. Sa'd ibn al-Rabi' said: \"I am the wealthiest of the Helpers. I will give you half of my wealth, and look at whichever of my two wives you desire; I will divorce her for you, and when her waiting period is over, you may marry her.\" Abd al-Rahman said: \"I have no need for that. Is there a market where trade occurs?\" He said: \"The market of Qaynuqa'.\" Abd al-Rahman went there and brought back some dried yogurt and ghee. He continued to go daily, and before long, Abd al-Rahman returned with a trace of yellow on him. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, asked: \"Have you married?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He asked: \"And whom?\" He said: \"A woman from the Helpers.\" He asked: \"What did you pay as dowry?\" He said: \"The weight of a date pit of gold \u2013 or a date pit of gold.\" The Prophet, peace be upon him, said to him: \"Hold a wedding feast, even if it be with a sheep.\"",location:"The City",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/45",icon:"users"},{id:46,year:"623 CE",hijri:"1 AH",month:"\u0634\u0639\u0628\u0627\u0646",title:"The expedition of Ubaydah ibn al-Harith ibn al-Muttalib to the valley of Rabigh.",desc:"The Prophet, peace be upon him, sent Ubaydah ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib on a mission to Batn Rabigh \u2013 which is the miqat for the people of Sham, Egypt, Turkey, and those who follow their route \u2013 in Sha'ban, eight months after the Hijra. He gave him a white banner, which was carried by Mis\u1e6da\u1e25 ibn Uthathah ibn al-Muttalib ibn Abd Manaf. They were sixty emigrants, with no Ansar among them. They encountered Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, who was with two hundred men at Batn Rabigh, ten miles from al-Juhfah. There was archery between them, but they did not draw their swords, nor did they line up for battle. It was merely a skirmish. Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas was among them, and he was the first to shoot an arrow in the cause of Allah. Then, both parties returned to their respective encampments.",location:"Batin Rabigh",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/46",icon:"scroll"},{id:47,year:"623 CE",hijri:"1 AH",month:"\u0631\u0645\u0636\u0627\u0646",title:"The expedition of Hamza to the Sea of Reeds.",desc:"The Prophet, peace be upon him, sent a detachment to the coast of the sea, appointing Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib as its commander. He sent him with thirty riders from the Emigrants. Hamza's banner was the first banner that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, tied, and it was white. Its bearer was Abu Murthad Kannaz ibn Husayn al-Ghanawi, may Allah be pleased with him. The objective of this detachment was to intercept a caravan of Quraysh coming from Sham, which included Abu Jahl ibn Hisham with three hundred men. They reached the coast of the sea near Al-`Is - a place near Medina on the coast of the sea - and they met and lined up for battle. Majdi ibn Amr al-Juhani - who was an ally to both sides - walked between them until he separated them, so they did not fight. Hamza's detachment, may Allah be pleased with him, was at the same time as Ubaydah ibn al-Harith's detachment, may Allah be pleased with him, to Rabigh.",location:"Sea Sword",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/47",icon:"shield"},{id:48,year:"623 CE",hijri:"1 AH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The expedition of Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, to al-Kharrar.",desc:"The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, with twenty of the Emigrants, nine months after the Hijra. It is also said that it was eight. They were sent to Al-Kharrar. He tied a white banner for him, which was carried by Al-Miqdad ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with him. Al-Kharrar is a valley from which one reaches Al-Juhfah. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, instructed him not to go beyond it, so that they might intercept a caravan of Quraysh passing by. They set out on foot, hiding during the day and traveling at night, until they reached the mentioned location at dawn on the fifth day. They found that the caravan had already passed the day before. So they turned back to Medina without engaging in any battle.",location:"Al-Kharrar",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/48",icon:"moon"},{id:49,year:"623 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0635\u0641\u0631",title:"The Battle of al-Abwa' (Waddan) and the truce with Banu Damrah.",desc:"The first expedition the Prophet, peace be upon him, undertook was to Al-Abwa, then to Buwat, then to Al-Ushairah. Al-Ushairah is a village in the region of Al-Fur', located twenty-three miles from Al-Juhfah in the direction of Medina. It is said to have been named thus due to the plague that was there. This was the expedition of Waddan, with a stressed \"dal\". Ishaq said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, went out on an expedition himself until he reached Waddan, which is Al-Abwa. He departed from Medina twelve months after his arrival in Medina, intending to confront Quraysh. He made a treaty with Banu Damrah bin Bakr bin Abd Manat, from Kinanah. Their chief, Makhshi bin Amr al-Damri, made a treaty with him, and he returned without fighting. The Prophet, peace be upon him, had appointed Sa'd bin Ubadah as his deputy in Medina. It is also said that when the Prophet, peace be upon him, reached Al-Abwa, he sent Ubaidah bin Al-Harith with sixty men. They encountered a group from Quraysh, and they exchanged arrows. Sa'd bin Abi Waqqas shot an arrow, and he was the first to shoot an arrow in the cause of Allah.",location:"Al-Abwa",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/49",icon:"star"},{id:50,year:"623 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The First Battle of Badr (Wadi Safwan).",desc:"The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, embarked on the first expedition to Badr until he reached the valley of Safwan. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, set out thirteen months after his Hijra, seeking Kurz ibn Jabir al-Fihri. Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, carried his banner, which was white. He appointed Zayd ibn Harithah as his deputy in Medina. Kurz had raided the herds of Medina \u2013 the camels and livestock that grazed in the morning \u2013 and had driven them away. He was grazing them in the protected pasture. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, pursued him until he reached a valley called Safwan, in the vicinity of Badr. However, Kurz escaped him and he did not catch him, so he returned to Medina.",location:"Wadi Safwan",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/50",icon:"child"},{id:51,year:"623 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The Battle of Bu'at, near Yanbu.",desc:"Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: We were on an expedition with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) to the valley of Buwat, and he was seeking Majdi bin Amr al-Juhani. We were taking turns riding the camel, five, six, or seven of us. It was the turn of an Ansari man to ride his camel. He dismounted it and rode it, then urged it on, but it was slow to respond. He said to it: \"Get up, may Allah curse you.\" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: \"Who is it that has cursed his camel?\" He replied: \"I did, O Messenger of Allah.\" He said: \"Get off it, and do not accompany us while you are cursed. Do not invoke curses upon yourselves, nor upon your children, nor upon your property, lest you happen to ask Allah for something at a time when He grants requests, and He answers you.\" Buwat is a mountain in the mountains of Juhaynah, near Yanbu.",location:"Buwat",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/51",icon:"heart"},{id:52,year:"623 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u062c\u0645\u0627\u062f\u0649",title:"The Battle of Al-Ushairah (Al-Usayrah).",desc:"It was said to Zayd ibn Arqam: \"How many expeditions did the Prophet, peace be upon him, participate in?\" He said: \"Nineteen.\" It was said: \"How many did you participate in with him?\" He said: \"Seventeen.\" It was said: \"Which one was the first?\" He said: \"Al-Usayrah, or Al-Ushayr.\" The expedition of Al-Ushayr occurred before the Battle of Badr. The Prophet, peace be upon him, took the path of Bani Dinar, then the plain of Al-Khabar, and camped under a tree in Bat\u1e25\u0101' Ibn Azhar called Dhat Al-Saq. He prayed there... Food was prepared for him there, and he ate from it, and the people ate with him... Water was brought for him from a well called Al-Mushtarib. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, departed, leaving the people to his left, and took a path called Shu'bat Abdullah... Then he descended to the left until he reached Yallil, where he camped at the confluence of Yallil and the confluence of Al-Dab'ah. He drank from a well in Al-Dab'ah, then took the path of Farsh, the path of Malal, until he reached the road at Suhayrat, at Sukhayrat Al-Yamam. Then the road straightened for him until he reached Al-Ushayr from the valley of Yanbu'. He stayed there during Jumada al-Ula and some nights of Jumada al-Akhirah. There, he made a truce with Bani Mudlij and their allies from Bani Damrah, and then returned to Medina without encountering any hardship.",location:"The Clan",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/52",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:53,year:"624 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0631\u062c\u0628",title:"The expedition of Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, to Rabigh.",desc:"Ibn Asakir narrated from Al-Zuhri, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sent a detachment including Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, to a region of the Hijaz called Rabegh. The polytheists attacked the Muslims, and on that day, Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, came to them with his arrows. He was the first to shoot an arrow in the cause of Allah, and this was the first battle in Islam.\"",location:"Rabegh",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/53",icon:"dove"},{id:54,year:"624 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0631\u062c\u0628",title:"The expedition of Abdullah ibn Jahsh al-Asadi to Nakhla.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, sent his messenger Abdullah ibn Jahsh al-Asadi to Nakhlah in Rajab, seventeen months after the Hijra, with twelve men from the Muhajirun, each pair sharing a camel. They reached the valley of Nakhlah to scout a caravan of Quraysh. In this expedition, Abdullah ibn Jahsh was named the Commander of the Faithful. The Messenger of God had written him a letter and ordered him not to look at it until he had traveled for two days, then to look at it. When he opened the letter, he found: \"When you look at this letter of mine, proceed until you descend to Nakhlah, between Mecca and Ta'if, and scout Quraysh there, and learn their news for us.\" He said, \"We hear and obey,\" and informed his companions of this, stating that he did not compel them. Whoever desired martyrdom should proceed, and whoever disliked death should return. As for me, I am proceeding. So they all proceeded. While on the way, Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas and Utbah ibn Ghazwan lost a camel they were sharing, so they fell behind to search for it. Abdullah ibn Jahsh proceeded until he reached Nakhlah. A caravan of Quraysh passed by him carrying raisins, dried meat, and merchandise, which included Amr ibn al-Hadrami, Uthman and Nawfal ibn Abdullah ibn al-Mughirah, and al-Hakam ibn Kaysan, a freed slave of Banu al-Mughirah. The Muslims consulted among themselves and said, \"We are on the last day of the sacred month of Rajab. If we fight them, we will violate the sacred month. If we leave them tonight, they will enter the sanctuary.\" Then they agreed to confront them. One of them threw a spear at Amr ibn al-Hadrami and killed him, and they captured Uthman and al-Hakam, while Nawfal escaped. Then they returned with the caravan and the two captives, having set aside the fifth, which was the first fifth in Islam, and the first killing in Islam, and the first two captives in Islam. The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, disapproved of what they had done, and Quraysh's hostility and denial intensified. They claimed to have found a pretext, saying: Muhammad has made the sacred month permissible. This weighed heavily on the Muslims until God Almighty revealed: {They ask you about the sacred month, about fighting in it. Say, \"Fighting in it is great [sin], but barring [people] from the way of God and disbelief in Him and [preventing access to] al-Masjid al-Haram and the expulsion of its people from it are greater [evil] with God. And affliction is greater than killing.} [Al-Baqarah: 217].",location:"Palm tree",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/54",icon:"hand-holding-heart"},{id:55,year:"624 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0634\u0639\u0628\u0627\u0646",title:"The Change of the Qibla to the Kaaba.",desc:"From Al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: \"When the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, arrived in Medina, he prayed towards Jerusalem for sixteen or seventeen months. He loved to be directed towards the Kaaba, so Allah the Almighty revealed: {We have certainly seen the turning of your face, [O Muhammad], toward the heaven, and We will surely turn you to a qiblah with which you shall be well-pleased} [Al-Baqarah: 144]. So he was directed towards the Kaaba. A man prayed the 'Asr prayer with him, then he went out and passed by some of the Ansar. He said: I bear witness that I prayed with the Prophet, peace be upon him, and that he was directed towards the Kaaba. So they turned while they were bowing in the 'Asr prayer.\"",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/55",icon:"book"},{id:56,year:"624 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0634\u0639\u0628\u0627\u0646",title:"The obligation of fasting the month of Ramadan was revealed.",desc:"In this year, the fasting of the month of Ramadan was ordained. It has also been said that the obligation of fasting the month of Ramadan was before the Battle of Badr.",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/56",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:57,year:"624 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0631\u0645\u0636\u0627\u0646",title:"The obligation of Zakat al-Fitr was ordained.",desc:"Allah, the Exalted, has ordained the Zakat al-Fitr, which is obligatory upon every Muslim, whether free or slave, male or female, young or old, who is able to pay it. The wisdom behind it is to purify the fasting person from any idle talk or obscenity they may have committed during fasting, and to provide assistance to the poor, bringing them joy on the day of Eid.",location:"Undetermined",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/57",icon:"route"},{id:58,year:"624 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0631\u0645\u0636\u0627\u0646",title:"The Expedition of 'Umayr ibn 'Adi, may Allah be pleased with him, to kill 'Asma bint Marwan.",desc:"Asma bint Marwan, from Banu Umayyah ibn Zayd, wife of Yazid ibn Zayd ibn Hisn al-Khatmi, used to criticize Islam, harm the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and incite against him. She would throw menstrual cloths in the mosque of Banu Khatmah. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, declared her blood forfeit. Umayr ibn Adi vowed that if the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, returned to Medina from Badr, he would kill her, and he did so, may Allah be pleased with him. When Umayr returned, he found her sons with a group burying her. They said, \"O Umayr, did you kill her?\" He replied, \"Yes, so plot against me all together, then give me no respite. By Him in whose hand is my soul, if all of you said what she said, I would strike you with this sword of mine until I die or I kill you.\" On that day, Islam appeared among Banu Khatmah, and whoever among them had embraced Islam used to conceal it. The first among Banu Khatmah to embrace Islam was Umayr ibn Adi, who is also called Al-Qari.",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/58",icon:"flag"},{id:59,year:"624 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0631\u0645\u0636\u0627\u0646",title:"The Battle of Badr (the Great) and the Victory of the Muslims.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, dispatched a group of Muslims to intercept the Quraysh caravan coming from Syria, which was led by Abu Sufyan. Abu Sufyan later learned of the Muslims' departure and sent a message to Quraysh calling for their aid to rescue their trade. He also changed his route to save the caravan. Then, the Muslims and the polytheists met at Badr, a place between Mecca and Medina, and closer to Medina than Mecca. The number of polytheists was nearly a thousand, while the number of Muslims was over three hundred. The battle began with the famous duel, then the fighting started and was intense. Prominent leaders of Quraysh, such as Abu Jahl and Umayyah ibn Khalaf, and others were killed, with their total dead reaching seventy men and the same number taken captive. Fourteen Muslims were killed, and some say sixteen. Thus, a great victory was achieved by the Muslims. God Almighty aided them and sent angels to fight alongside them. Regarding the captives, Umar suggested killing them, while Abu Bakr suggested ransoming them. The Prophet, peace be upon him, accepted Abu Bakr's opinion, but revelation descended in agreement with Umar's view. As for the spoils of war, the chapter of Al-Anfal was revealed concerning them.",location:"Badr",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/59",icon:"mountain"},{id:60,year:"624 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0631\u0645\u0636\u0627\u0646",title:"The passing of Ruqayyah, may Allah be pleased with her.",desc:"She is Ruqayyah, daughter of the Master of Mankind, peace be upon him. She was the wife of Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with them both. Ruqayyah was initially married to Utbah ibn Abi Lahab. When the Prophet, peace be upon him, was sent [with his message], Abu Lahab ordered his son to divorce her. Uthman then married her and migrated with her to Abyssinia. When the Messenger of Allah called upon the people to go out to intercept the caravan of Abu Sufyan, he ordered Uthman to remain with Ruqayyah to nurse her and care for her. This was the reason for his absence. The Prophet, peace be upon him, granted him a share [of the spoils] as he did for those who were present at Badr. Then Ruqayyah did not remain long in her illness before she passed away, may Allah be pleased with her.",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/60",icon:"sun"},{id:61,year:"624 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The Expedition of Salim ibn Umayr, may Allah be pleased with him, to Abu Afak the Jew.",desc:"The expedition of Salim bin Umayr, may Allah be pleased with him, to Abu Afak the Jew took place in Shawwal, at the beginning of the twentieth month after the Hijra of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. Abu Afak was from the Banu Amr bin Awf, an old man who had reached the age of one hundred and twenty years. He was a Jew, greatly disbelieving, and a harsh critic of the Muslims. His heart was filled with hatred and envy towards the Muslims as he saw the Aws and Khazraj rallying to support the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. His malice towards Islam and its people intensified after he saw the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, growing stronger and more established in Medina and its surroundings after the Battle of Badr. He could not bear it, so he began to recite poetry, denouncing the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, inciting enmity towards him, and ridiculing the opinion of the Ansar for following and supporting the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. Salim bin Umayr, who was one of the \"weepers\" \u2013 meaning during the Battle of Tabuk \u2013 and a participant in the Battle of Badr, said: \"I vow to kill Abu Afak or die trying.\" He waited, seeking an opportunity, until one summer night when Abu Afak slept in the courtyard. Salim bin Umayr heard of this, approached him, placed his sword on his abdomen, and then leaned on it until it sank into his body. The enemy of Allah cried out, and some of his followers came to him, brought him into his house, and buried him. Abu Afak was among those whose hypocrisy became evident when the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, killed Al-Harith bin Suwayd bin Al-Samit. Salim participated in the Battle of Badr, Uhud, the Trench, and all the other battles with the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. He passed away during the caliphate of Muawiyah bin Abi Sufyan, may Allah be pleased with them both.",location:"The City",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/61",icon:"users"},{id:62,year:"624 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The Battle of Banu Qaynuqa.",desc:"Most historians of battles and biographies mentioned that it occurred after Badr, and Ibn Hajar favored this view. It took place on Saturday, the middle of Shawwal. The report of the expulsion of Banu Qaynuqa' is established in the two Sahih collections. It is narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said: \"An-Nadir and Qurayzah fought, so he expelled Banu An-Nadir and let Qurayzah and those with them remain, until Qurayzah fought. Then he killed their men and divided their women, children, and property among the Muslims, except for some of them who joined the Prophet, peace be upon him, and he granted them safety and they embraced Islam. And he expelled all the Jews of Medina: Banu Qaynuqa' \u2013 who were the kin of Abdullah ibn Salam \u2013 and the Jews of Banu Harithah, and all the Jews of Medina.\"",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/62",icon:"scroll"},{id:63,year:"624 CE",hijri:"2 AH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The Expedition of As-Sawiq, or the Expedition of Banu Sulaym (Qarqarat al-Kadr).",desc:"After the defeat of the polytheists at Badr, Abu Sufyan vowed that no water from ritual impurity would touch his head until he invaded Muhammad, peace be upon him, and his companions and took revenge on the Muslims. So he set out with two hundred riders from Quraysh, to fulfill his oath. He reached the outskirts of Medina at night and sought refuge with Banu Nadir. He went to Huyayy ibn Akhtab and knocked on his door, but Huyayy refused to open it for him, fearing him. So Abu Sufyan turned away from him to Salam ibn Mishkam, who was the chief of Banu Nadir at that time and the keeper of their treasure. Abu Sufyan asked for permission to enter, and he was granted it. Salam hosted him, gave him wine to drink, and informed him of the news of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. Then Abu Sufyan left in the latter part of that night and went to his companions. He sent some men from Quraysh, who went to an area of Medina called Al-Arid \u2013 a valley in Medina. They set fire to the fruit-bearing trees and date palms of the Muslims. They found a man from the Ansar and his ally in their fields and killed them, then they turned back. When this news reached the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, he set out in pursuit of them with two hundred of the Emigrants and Helpers. He appointed Abu Lubaba Bashir ibn Abd al-Mundhir, may Allah be pleased with him, as governor of Medina. Abu Sufyan and his companions began to throw down sacks of saweeq \u2013 roasted wheat or barley, then ground, and used as provisions, mixed with water, ghee, or honey \u2013 which was the majority of their provisions, to lighten their load for escape. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, reached Qaraqarah al-Kadr \u2013 Qaraqarah: level ground. Al-Kadr: a water source belonging to Banu Sulaym, and the origin of Kadr is a bird with a dusky color, after which the place or water was named. Then he turned back towards Medina, as Abu Sufyan and his companions had escaped him. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, was away for five days. When the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, returned with them, the Muslims said, \"O Messenger of Allah, do you hope that this will be a raid for us?\" He said, \"Yes.\" This raid is also called the \"Raid of Saweeq\" because the most of what the people threw away from their provisions was saweeq, so the Muslims returned with a lot of saweeq. This occurred in Shawwal in the second year of the Hijra.",location:"The rumbling of the turbid",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/63",icon:"shield"},{id:65,year:"624 CE",hijri:"3 AH",month:"",title:"The Killing of Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf.",desc:"Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf was a man from the tribe of Tayy, and his mother was from the Banu Nadir, a Jewish tribe. He was a poet who used to lampoon the Prophet, peace be upon him, and his companions, inciting hatred against them and causing them harm. When the Battle of Badr occurred, he was disheartened and humiliated, saying, \"The belly of the earth is better than its surface today.\" He then went to Mecca, wept for the slain Quraysh, and incited them with poetry. Jabir ibn Abdullah narrated that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said, \"Who will deal with Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf? For he has indeed harmed Allah and His Messenger.\" Muhammad ibn Maslamah said, \"O Messenger of Allah, would you like me to kill him?\" He replied, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Grant me permission to speak.\" The Prophet said, \"Speak.\" So, Muhammad ibn Maslamah went to Ka'b and said to him, mentioning what transpired between them, \"This man has asked for charity and has burdened us.\" When Ka'b heard this, he said, \"And by Allah, you will soon be weary of him.\" Muhammad replied, \"We have followed him now, and we dislike leaving him until we see what becomes of his affair. He also said, \"And I wanted you to lend me some money.\" Ka'b asked, \"What will you give me as collateral?\" Muhammad replied, \"What do you want?\" Ka'b said, \"You will give us your women as collateral.\" Muhammad replied, \"You are the most handsome of the Arabs; shall we give you our women as collateral!?\" Ka'b then said, \"You will give me your children as collateral.\" Muhammad replied, \"One of our children is insulted, and it is said, 'He was ransomed for two measures of dates.' But we will give you our weapons as collateral.\" Ka'b said, \"Then fine.\" He promised to bring him Al-Harith, Abu 'Abs ibn Jabr, and 'Abbad ibn Bishr. Muhammad said, \"So they came and called him at night, and he came down to them.\" Sufyan said, \"Others than 'Amr said: His wife said to him, 'I hear a sound like the sound of blood.' He said, 'It is only Muhammad ibn Maslamah, his companion, and Abu Na'ilah. Indeed, the generous man, if invited to a stab in the night, would respond.' Muhammad said, 'When he comes, I will reach for his head, and when I have a firm grip on him, then it is yours.' He said, 'When he came down, he was wearing perfume. They said, \"We detect the scent of perfume on you.\" He said, \"Yes, so-and-so is under me; she is the most fragrant of the Arab women.\" He said, \"Will you permit me to smell it?\" He said, \"Yes, smell it.\" So he took it and smelled it, then said, \"Will you permit me to return?\" He said, \"He gained a firm grip on his head, then said, 'It is yours.'\" He said, \"So they killed him.\" (...Then they came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and informed him.)",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/65",icon:"moon"},{id:66,year:"624 CE",hijri:"3 AH",month:"\u0635\u0641\u0631",title:"The raid of Ghatafan on Najd (the Raid of Dhu Amr).",desc:"This was the Ghazwah of Dhu Amarr, in the region of Najd. The reason for it was that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, received news that a group from Banu Tha'labah ibn Sa'id ibn Dhubyan ibn Baghidh ibn Ra'ith ibn Ghatafan, and Banu Muharib ibn Khasafah ibn Qays, at Dhu Amarr, had gathered, intending to raid the frontiers of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. A man from them, called Du'thur ibn Al-Harith ibn Muharib, had united them. So, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, summoned the Muslims and set out with four hundred and fifty men, accompanied by several horses. He appointed Uthman ibn Affan as his deputy in Medina. They encountered a man from them at Dhu al-Qassah, called Jabbar from Banu Tha'labah. The Muslims asked him, \"Where are you going?\" He replied, \"I am going to Yathrib to scout for myself and look around.\" He was brought to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and informed him of their news. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said, \"They will not meet you. If they hear of your march, they will flee to the mountain tops. I am going with you.\" So, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, invited Jabbar to Islam, and he embraced Islam. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, attached him to Bilal, and Jabbar led them along a path, bringing them down to them. The people heard of the march of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and fled to the mountain tops. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, reached a water source called Dhu Amarr and camped there. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and his companions were struck by heavy rain, and the clothes of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and his companions became wet. So, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, descended under a tree and spread his clothes to dry, and lay down, while the polytheists could see him. The Muslims were occupied with their affairs. The polytheists sent a brave man from them, called Du'thur ibn Al-Harith, who was their chief and bravest. He was girded with a sword. Du'thur rushed forward, drawing his sword, until he stood over the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, with his sword drawn, and said, \"O Muhammad, who will protect you from me today?\" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, replied, \"Allah.\" Gabriel pushed him in the chest, and the sword fell from his hand. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, took it and said to him, \"Who will protect you from me?!\" He replied, \"No one.\" And I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. By Allah, I will never gather a force against you again. So, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, gave him his sword. Then he came to his people, and they asked him, \"What happened to you? Woe to you!\" He said, \"I saw a tall man who pushed me in the chest, and I fell on my back. I knew he was an angel, and I testified that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. By Allah, I will never gather a force against him again.\" And he began to call his people to Islam. And Allah, the Almighty, revealed: \"O you who have believed, remember the favor of Allah upon you when a people intended to extend their hands [in aggression] against you, but He restrained their hands from you.\" [Al-Ma'idah, 11]. And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, returned to Medina without encountering any harm. His absence was fifteen nights. It was also said that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, stayed in Najd for the entire month of Safar.",location:"Dhu Ammar",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/66",icon:"star"},{id:67,year:"624 CE",hijri:"3 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The Battle of Bu'ran between Al-Fur' and Medina.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, went on an expedition intending to confront Quraysh, and he appointed Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum, may God be pleased with him, as his deputy in Medina. He proceeded until he reached Buhran, in the vicinity of al-Fur'. The reason for this expedition was that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, learned that a large gathering of Banu Sulaym ibn Mansur was present there. So he set out with three hundred of his companions, and he did not reveal the purpose of his journey. When he was one night's journey from Buhran, he encountered a man from Banu Sulaym, who informed them that the people had dispersed. He detained the man with another companion and proceeded until he arrived at Buhran, finding no one there. He stayed for a few days, then returned without encountering any hostility, and released the man. Then he turned back, returning to Medina.",location:"Two seas",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/67",icon:"child"},{id:68,year:"624 CE",hijri:"3 AH",month:"\u062c\u0645\u0627\u062f\u0649",title:"The expedition of Zayd ibn Harithah.",desc:"After their defeat in the Battle of Badr, the polytheists of Mecca began searching for another route for their trade to Syria. Some of them suggested the route of Najd in Iraq, and they indeed took it. Merchants set out from them, including Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, Safwan ibn Umayyah, and Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza, carrying silver and many goods valued at one hundred thousand dirhams. This news reached the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, through Sulait ibn al-Nu'man, may Allah be pleased with him. So, he sent Zaid ibn Harithah with one hundred riders to intercept the caravan. Zaid encountered the caravan at a watering place called al-Qardah, which is one of the watering places of Najd. Its men fled in terror, and the Muslims seized the caravan and its contents. They captured its guide, Furat ibn Hayyan, who embraced Islam at the hands of the Prophet, peace be upon him. They returned to Medina, and the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, distributed one-fifth of the spoils and divided the remainder among the members of the expedition.",location:"Monkeys",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/68",icon:"heart"},{id:69,year:"625 CE",hijri:"3 AH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The Battle of Uhud and the Reason for the Muslims' Defeat.",desc:"The reason for this was that when the polytheists suffered casualties among their nobles at the Battle of Badr, and the caravan with its trade goods, which was with Abu Sufyan, escaped, upon their return to Mecca, the sons of those who were killed and the leaders of those who remained said to Abu Sufyan: \"Amass these funds for the war against Muhammad.\" So they spent them for that purpose, gathered armies and allied tribes, and marched with nearly three thousand men, until they encamped near Uhud, facing Medina. On Friday, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, prayed. After finishing, he prayed over a man from Banu Najjar, named Malik ibn Amr. He then consulted the people, asking whether they should march out to meet the enemy or remain in Medina. Abdullah ibn Ubayy suggested staying in Medina. He argued that if they stayed, they would be in a terrible confinement, and if the enemy entered, the men would fight them face-to-face, while the women and children would pelt them with stones from above. If the enemy retreated, they would return in defeat. Other Companions, who had not witnessed Badr, suggested marching out to meet them. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, went inside, donned his armor, and marched out to meet them. He set out with a thousand of his Companions. When they reached a place called Ash-Shawtt, Abdullah ibn Ubayy turned back with a third of the army, angered because his opinion had not been heeded. He and his followers said: \"If we knew there would be fighting today, we would have followed you, but we do not see you fighting today.\" The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, continued his march until he reached the valley of Uhud, at the entrance of the valley. He positioned his back and his camp towards Uhud and prepared for battle with seven hundred of his Companions. He appointed Abdullah ibn Jubayr, brother of Banu Amr ibn Awf, as the commander of the archers, who numbered fifty men that day. He instructed them: \"Repel the cavalry from us with arrows, so they do not come at us from behind. Whether we gain victory or suffer defeat, remain in your position, so that we are not attacked from your side.\" The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, wore two coats of mail and gave the banner to Mus'ab ibn Umayr, brother of Banu Abd ad-Dar. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, permitted some of the young boys to participate that day and deferred others. Quraysh mobilized, numbering three thousand men, with two hundred horses prepared for battle. They appointed Khalid ibn al-Walid to command the right flank of the cavalry and Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl to command the left flank. The banner was given to Banu Abd ad-Dar. Then, what happened between the two factions occurred. Initial signs of victory began to appear for the Muslims. When the Muslims saw the polytheists retreating, the archers neglected the Prophet's command to them and descended to collect spoils. Khalid ibn al-Walid seized the opportunity, circled behind them, and engaged them in battle, which led to a reversal of fortunes. The battle concluded with the martyrdom of seventy Muslims, including Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, Mus'ab ibn Umayr, Abdullah ibn Haram, the father of Jabir, and Abdullah ibn Jubayr, the commander of the archers.",location:"Uhud",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/69",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:70,year:"625 CE",hijri:"3 AH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The martyrdom of Hamza, may Allah be pleased with him, in the Battle of Uhud.",desc:"'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Adi ibn al-Khiyar narrated that he asked Wahshi, the killer of Hamza, \"Will you not tell us about the killing of Hamza?\" He replied, \"Yes. Hamza killed Tu'aymah ibn 'Adi ibn al-Khiyar at Badr. My master, Jubayr ibn Mut'im, said to me, 'If you kill Hamza for my uncle, you are free.' So, when the people went out for the battle of 'Aynayn, and 'Aynayn is a mountain opposite Uhud, with a valley between them, I went out with the people to fight. When they lined up for battle, Sib\u0101' came out and said, 'Is there any duelist?' Hamza ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib came out to him and said, 'O Sib\u0101', O son of Umm Anmar, the cutter of the clitorises, do you defy Allah and His Messenger (peace be upon him)?' Then he attacked him, and he was like yesterday's gone. I lay in wait for Hamza behind a rock. When he came near me, I threw my spear at him, and it lodged in his chest until it came out from between his legs. That was the last I saw of him. When the people returned, I returned with them. I stayed in Mecca until Islam became widespread. Then I went to Ta'if. They sent a messenger to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and I was told, 'He does not harm messengers.' So I went out with them until I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). When he saw me, he said, 'Are you Wahshi?' I said, 'Yes.' He said, 'Did you kill Hamza?' I said, 'What reached you of the matter has happened.' He said, 'Can you hide your face from me?' So I left. When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) passed away, Musaylimah the Liar emerged. I said, 'I will go out to Musaylimah, perhaps I will kill him and make it equivalent for Hamza.' So I went out with the people, and what happened, happened. I saw a man standing in a breach of a wall, like a tawny camel with disheveled hair. I threw my spear at him, and it lodged between his chest and his navel until it came out from between his shoulders. Then a man from the Ansar rushed at him and struck him on the head with his sword.\"",location:"Uhud",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/70",icon:"dove"},{id:71,year:"625 CE",hijri:"3 AH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The Battle of Hamra al-Asad.",desc:"Ibn Kathir said in his Tafsir of the verse: \"{Those who responded to Allah and the Messenger, even after they had been wounded}\" This was on the day of Hamra' al-Asad. When the polytheists inflicted what they inflicted upon the Muslims \u2013 meaning on the day of Uhud \u2013 they turned back towards their lands. As they continued on their journey, they regretted not having finished off the people of Medina and made it the decisive blow. When this reached the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, he called upon the Muslims to go after them to instill fear in them and show them that they possessed strength and resilience. He did not permit anyone except those who had been present at the battle of Uhud, except for Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him. The Muslims responded, despite their wounds and exhaustion, in obedience to Allah the Almighty and His Messenger, peace be upon him. It was narrated from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said regarding the verse: \"{Those who responded to Allah and the Messenger, even after they had been wounded \u2013 for those among them who did good and feared Allah will have a great reward.}\" She said to Urwah: \"O son of my sister, your fathers were among them: Al-Zubayr and Abu Bakr. When what happened to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, happened on the day of Uhud, and the polytheists departed from him, he feared they might return. He said: 'Who will go after them?' So seventy men volunteered from them. She said: 'Abu Bakr and Al-Zubayr were among them.'\" Ibn Kathir said after mentioning this Hadith: \"(This narration is very strange, for it is well-known among the scholars of Maghazi that those who went out with the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, to Hamra' al-Asad were all who had witnessed Uhud, and they were seven hundred. Seventy of them were killed, and the rest remained).\" The apparent meaning is that there is no contradiction between the words of Aisha and the scholars of Maghazi, because the meaning of her statement: \"So seventy men volunteered for it\" is that they preceded others, and then the rest joined them. Hamra' al-Asad is located eight miles from Medina.",location:"Hamra' al-Asad",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/71",icon:"hand-holding-heart"},{id:72,year:"625 CE",hijri:"4 AH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The expedition of Abdullah ibn Abi Sarh, may Allah be pleased with him, to Qatan.",desc:"The expedition of Abu Salamah Abdullah bin Abdul-Asad, who was the cousin of the Prophet, peace be upon him, through his paternal aunt Barrah bint Abdul-Muttalib, and his foster brother, as they were both nursed by Thuwibah. Qatan is a mountain, and it is also said to be a water source belonging to Banu Asad. The reason for this expedition was that a man from Tayy, named Al-Walid bin Zuhayr bin Tarif, came to Medina to visit his niece, Zaynab, who was married to Tulayb bin Umayr bin Wahb. He was informed that Tulayhah and Salamah, the sons of Khuwaylid, had gathered their people and those who obeyed them to wage war against the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. Qays bin Al-Harith advised them against it, but they disobeyed. When this reached the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, he summoned Abu Salamah, may Allah be pleased with him, and said, \"Go forth on this expedition, for I have appointed you in command of it.\" He tied a banner for him and said, \"Travel until you reach the land of Banu Asad bin Khuzaymah, and raid them before their forces can gather against you.\" He also advised him to fear Allah, the Almighty, and to treat the Muslims with him well. So, one hundred and fifty men went with him on this expedition, and the man from Tayy accompanied them as a guide. He traveled swiftly, diverting them from the main road, and journeyed day and night. They outpaced any news and arrived at Dhi Qatan, a water source of Banu Asad, where their forces were gathered. They raided their livestock and captured three shepherds, while the rest escaped. They went to their people and informed them of the news, warning them about Abu Salamah's forces and exaggerating their numbers. Consequently, the gathered forces dispersed in all directions. Abu Salamah arrived at the water source to find the gathering had dispersed. He made camp and divided his companions into three groups to pursue the camels and sheep. One group remained with him, while the other two raided in different directions. He instructed them not to go too far in their pursuit and to return to him if they were safe, and not to separate. He appointed a leader from among them for each group. They all returned to him safely, having acquired camels and sheep, and without encountering anyone. Abu Salamah then headed back to Medina with all the spoils, accompanied by the man from Tayy. After they had traveled for a night, Abu Salamah divided the spoils, setting aside the special share for the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, which was a slave. He also set aside the fifth (khums) and gave the guide from Tayy his satisfaction from the spoils. Then he divided the remainder among his companions, and each person received seven camels. He arrived in Medina with all of this without facing any opposition.",location:"Dhi Qatn",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/72",icon:"book"},{id:73,year:"625 CE",hijri:"4 AH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The expedition of Abdullah ibn Unays al-Juhani to Sufyan ibn Khalid in Batn Urnah.",desc:"Abdullah bin Unais, may Allah be pleased with him, departed from Medina on Monday, five nights into Muharram, thirty-five months after the Hijra of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. It reached the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, that Sufyan bin Khalid bin Nabih al-Hudhali, then al-Lahyani \u2013 who resided in Urnah and its vicinity with some of his people and others \u2013 intended to gather forces against the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and that many people from various backgrounds had joined him. Abdullah bin Unais, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, summoned me and said: \"It has reached me that Sufyan bin Khalid bin Nabih is gathering people against me to attack me, and he is in Nakhla or Urnah. Go to him and kill him.\" I said: O Messenger of Allah, describe him to me so I can recognize him. He said: \"The sign between you and him is that when you see him, you will be awestruck and fearful of him, and you will feel a shiver, and you will remember Satan.\" Abdullah said: I was not afraid of men, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have never been afraid of anything. He said: \"Yes; that is the sign between you and him: you will feel a shiver when you see him.\" He said: I asked permission from the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, to speak. He said: \"Say what you wish.\" And he said: \"Claim kinship with Khuz\u0101'ah.\" So I took my sword and added nothing to it, and I went out claiming kinship with Khuz\u0101'ah. When I reached the valley of Urnah, I met him walking, with a group of Abyssinians behind him. When I saw him, I was awestruck by him and recognized him by the description the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, had given me. I said: Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth. The time for Asr prayer had arrived when I saw him, so I prayed while walking, gesturing with my head. When I approached him, he said: \"Who is the man?\" I said: \"A man from Khuz\u0101'ah. I heard about your gathering against Muhammad, so I came to be with you in it.\" He said: \"Yes; I am indeed among those gathered against him.\" So I walked with him and talked to him. He found my conversation pleasant, and I recited poetry to him, saying: \"How strange is what Muhammad has introduced of this new religion! He has abandoned the ways of our fathers and deemed their wisdom foolish.\" He said: \"I have not met anyone like me, nor anyone who fights as well as I do.\" He was leaning on a staff, striking the ground with it, until he reached his tent, and his companions dispersed to nearby dwellings, while they surrounded him. He said: \"Come here, O brother of Khuz\u0101'ah.\" I approached him. He said: \"Sit down.\" I sat with him until the people calmed down and slept. I took advantage of the opportunity. In most narrations, it is said that I walked with him until, when I had the opportunity, I struck him with my sword and killed him, and took his head. Then I turned and ascended a mountain, and entered a cave. The pursuit of horses and men came from all directions, while I was hidden in the cave. A man approached with his water skin and sandals in his hand. I was afraid. He put down his water skin and sandals and sat down to urinate near the mouth of the cave. Then he said to his companions: \"There is no one in the cave.\" So they turned back. I went out to the water skin, drank what was in it, and took the two sandals and put them on. I traveled by night and hid by day until I came to Medina. I found the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, in the mosque. When he saw me, he said: \"May your face be successful.\" I said: \"And may your face be successful, O Messenger of Allah.\" I placed the head before him and told him my story. He gave me a staff and said: \"Use this to lean on in Paradise; for those who lean on staffs in Paradise are few.\" The staff remained with Abdullah bin Unais until his death approached. He instructed his family to wrap the staff in his shroud. They did so. Ibn 'Uqbah said: \"They claim that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, informed them of Abdullah bin Unais killing Sufyan bin Khalid before Abdullah bin Unais, may Allah Almighty be pleased with him, arrived.\"",location:"The valley of Urnah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/73",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:74,year:"625 CE",hijri:"4 AH",month:"\u0635\u0641\u0631",title:"The Expedition of the Day of Ar-Raj'i.",desc:"Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent out a detachment of ten men as scouts, and appointed Asim bin Thabit Al-Ansari, the grandfather of Asim bin Umar bin Al-Khattab, as their commander. They set out until they reached Al-Hada, which is between Usfan and Mecca. They were discovered by a tribe of Hudhayl called Banu Lihyan. Nearly two hundred archers from their tribe mobilized and tracked their footsteps until they found traces of their meal, dates they had provisioned from Medina. They said, \"These are dates from Yathrib.\" They continued to track their footsteps. When Asim and his companions saw them, they took refuge in a mound of sand, and the enemy surrounded them. They called out to them, \"Come down and surrender yourselves, and you will have our covenant and pledge, and we will not kill any of you.\" Asim bin Thabit, the commander of the detachment, said, \"As for me, by Allah, I will not descend under the protection of a disbeliever today. O Allah, inform Your Prophet about us.\" So they shot arrows at them, and killed Asim and seven of his companions. Three men then descended to them under the covenant and pledge: Khubayb Al-Ansari, Ibn Dathinah, and another man. When they had control over them, they loosened the strings of their bows and bound them. The third man said, \"This is the first act of treachery. By Allah, I will not accompany you. I have an example in these slain men,\" referring to the killed companions. They dragged him and tried to persuade him to accompany them, but he refused, so they killed him. They then took Khubayb and Ibn Dathinah and sold them in Mecca after the Battle of Badr. Khubayb was bought by the sons of Al-Harith bin Amir bin Nawfal bin Abd Manaf. Khubayb had killed Al-Harith bin Amir on the day of Badr. Khubayb remained a captive with them. Ubayd Allah bin Iyad informed me that the daughter of Al-Harith told him that when they gathered, she lent Khubayb a razor to shave with. She said that she found him sitting on his thigh with the razor in his hand, holding her son. She was frightened, a fear that Khubayb recognized in her face. He said, \"Are you afraid that I will kill him? I would never do that. By Allah, I have never seen a better captive than Khubayb. By Allah, I once found him eating a bunch of grapes in his hand, while he was bound in chains, and there were no fruits in Mecca at that time.\" She used to say, \"This is sustenance from Allah that He provided for Khubayb.\" When they went out of the sanctuary to kill him in the open, Khubayb said to them, \"Let me pray two rak'ahs.\" They let him. He prayed two rak'ahs, then said, \"Were it not that you would think I was afraid, I would have prolonged it. O Allah, count them and destroy them. Then he recited: \"I do not care when I am killed as a Muslim, On whichever side it may be for Allah's decree. And if He wills, He will bless the scattered limbs. Al-Harith's son killed him. Khubayb was the one who initiated the two rak'ahs for every Muslim who is killed patiently. Allah answered the prayer of Asim bin Thabit when he was struck. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, informed his companions of their news and what had befallen them. Some of the disbelievers of Quraysh sent to Asim, when they heard he was killed, to bring something from him as identification. He had killed one of their great men on the day of Badr. Then, a swarm of wasps, like a canopy, was sent upon Asim, and it protected him from their messengers, so they were unable to cut any of his flesh.",location:"The Calm",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/74",icon:"route"},{id:75,year:"625 CE",hijri:"4 AH",month:"\u0635\u0641\u0631",title:"The Expedition of Bi'r Ma'unah.",desc:"Anas bin Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated that Ri'l, Dhakwan, 'Usayyah, and Bani Lihyan sought the help of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, against an enemy. So he sent them seventy of the Ansar, whom we used to call the Qurra' (reciters) in their time. They used to gather firewood during the day and pray at night. Until they reached Bi'r Ma'unah, where they were killed and betrayed. When this news reached the Prophet, peace be upon him, he performed the Qunut for a month, supplicating in the morning prayer against certain tribes of the Arabs, namely Ri'l, Dhakwan, 'Usayyah, and Bani Lihyan. Anas said: \"We used to recite a portion of the Quran about them, then that was abrogated: 'Convey from us to our people that we have met our Lord, and He is pleased with us and has pleased us.' And in a narration with Bukhari, it is mentioned that those seventy Ansar were killed at Bi'r Ma'unah. Anas bin Malik also narrated: Some people came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and said: 'Send us men to teach us the Quran and the Sunnah.' So he sent them seventy men from the Ansar, who were called the Qurra'. Among them was my uncle Haram. They used to recite the Quran and study at night to learn. During the day, they would bring water and place it in the mosque, and they would gather firewood, sell it, and buy food with it for the people of the Suffah and the poor. The Prophet, peace be upon him, sent them to those people. They were ambushed and killed before they reached their destination. They said: 'O Allah, convey to our Prophet that we have met You, and we are pleased with You, and You are pleased with us.' It was narrated that a man came up behind Haram, the uncle of Anas, and stabbed him with a spear until it pierced him. Haram said: 'By the Lord of the Ka'bah, I have succeeded!' The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said to his companions: 'Your brothers have been killed, and they said: O Allah, convey to our Prophet that we have met You, and we are pleased with You, and You are pleased with us.'\"",location:"Bi'r Ma'unah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/75",icon:"flag"},{id:76,year:"625 CE",hijri:"4 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The Expedition of Banu Nadir.",desc:"The reason for this was that when the companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, were killed at the Well of Ma'unah, and they were seventy in number, and 'Amr ibn Umayyah al-Dhamri escaped from them. When he was on his way back to Medina, he killed two men from Banu 'Amir, who had a treaty and a pledge of safety from the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, which 'Amr did not know about. When he returned, he informed the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him... So the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, went out to Banu an-Nadir seeking their help in the blood money for those two men. The dwellings of Banu an-Nadir were outside Medina, a few miles to its east. Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Yasar said in his book \"Al-Sirah\": Then the Messenger of Allah went out to Banu an-Nadir, seeking their help in the blood money for those two killed men from Banu 'Amir, whom 'Amr ibn Umayyah al-Dhamri had killed, due to the protection that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, had granted them... When the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, came to them seeking their help in the blood money for those two killed men, they said: Yes, O Abu al-Qasim, we will help you in whatever you wish and have sought our help for. Then they conferred among themselves and said: You will not find the man in such a situation as this \u2013 and the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, was beside a wall of their houses \u2013 so who will go up onto this house and throw a rock at him, and rid us of him? 'Amr ibn Jahsh ibn Ka'b, one of them, volunteered and said: I will do it. So he climbed up to throw a rock at him as they had said, and the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, was with a group of his companions, including Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them. Then the news came to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, from heaven about what the people intended, so he stood up and left, returning to Medina. When the Prophet, peace be upon him, was delayed, his companions went looking for him and met a man coming from Medina. They asked him about him, and he said: I saw him entering Medina. So the companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, came back until they reached him, and he informed them of the news of what the Jews had intended to do to him treacherously. And the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, ordered them to prepare for war against them and to march towards them. Then he marched until he reached them, and they fortified themselves in their fortresses. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, ordered the cutting down and burning of their palm trees. They called out to him: O Muhammad, you used to forbid corruption and criticize those who did it, so what about cutting down and burning the palm trees?! A group from Banu 'Awf ibn al-Khazraj, including 'Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul, Wardi'ah, Malik ibn Abi Qawqal, Suwayd, and Da'is, had sent to Banu an-Nadir saying: Stand firm and resist, for we will not hand you over. If you are fought, we will fight with you, and if you are expelled, we will go out with you. They waited for this support from them, but it did not come, and Allah cast terror into their hearts. So they asked the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, to expel them and spare their blood, on the condition that they would have whatever their camels could carry of their property, except for the rings. So he agreed. They carried away from their property what the camels could bear, and a man among them would dismantle his house from the frame of his door, place it on the back of his camel, and take it away. So they went out to Khaybar, and some of them went to Syria, leaving the property to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and it was exclusively for the Messenger of Allah. It is confirmed in Sahih al-Bukhari that the Prophet, peace be upon him, expelled Banu an-Nadir. And the Surah Al-Hashr was revealed concerning them.",location:"The Dwellings of Banu an-Nadir",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/76",icon:"mountain"},{id:77,year:"628 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The Battle of Dhat al-Riqa'.",desc:"Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, went out to Dhat al-Riqa' from Nakhla. He encountered a gathering of Ghatafan, but there was no fighting. The people frightened each other. Then the Prophet, peace be upon him, prayed the prayer of fear. And Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, said: We went out with the Prophet, peace be upon him, on an expedition, and we were six men. We took turns riding a camel. Our feet became blistered, and my feet became blistered, and my fingernails fell off. We used to wrap rags around our feet. So it was named the expedition of Dhat al-Riqa' because of the rags we tied around our feet. Abu Musa narrated this, then he disliked it and said: I wish I had not mentioned it, as if he disliked that anything of his deeds was revealed. And from Salih ibn Khawwat, from one who witnessed the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, praying the prayer of fear on the day of Dhat al-Riqa': A group stood in prayer with him, and another group faced the enemy. He prayed one rak'ah with those who were with him, then he remained standing, and they completed the prayer on their own. Then they departed and stood facing the enemy. The other group came, and he prayed with them the remaining rak'ah of his prayer, then he remained seated, and they completed the prayer on their own, and he gave the salutation with them. This was the expedition against Muharib, Khasafa, and Banu Tha'labah of Ghatafan. And from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both: The Prophet, peace be upon him, prayed the prayer of fear with his companions in the seventh expedition, the expedition of Dhat al-Riqa'. And from Jabir, he said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, prayed with them on the day of Muharib and Tha'labah.",location:"Dhat al-Riqa",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/77",icon:"sun"},{id:78,year:"626 CE",hijri:"4 AH",month:"\u0634\u0639\u0628\u0627\u0646",title:"The Battle of Badr al-Maw'id (Second Badr).",desc:"This expedition is called the Lesser Badr, the Small Badr, the Second Badr, and Badr of the Promise. This is due to the appointment made with Abu Sufyan ibn Harb on the day of Uhud. When Abu Sufyan departed from Uhud, he called out, \"Your appointment is Badr, next year.\" When the fourth year of the Hijra arrived, the Messenger, peace be upon him, set out with fifteen hundred of his companions to Badr, accompanied by ten horses. His banner was carried by Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him. This was in anticipation of the arrival of the Quraysh according to the appointed time set since the Battle of Uhud with Abu Sufyan, the leader of the Quraysh. The Muslims waited for eight days without the Quraysh arriving. Abu Sufyan had set out with two thousand men and fifty horses. When they reached Marr al-Dhuhran, forty miles from Mecca, they turned back, claiming that the year was one of drought. Their breaking of the promise had the effect of strengthening the position of the Muslims and restoring their prestige. The Muslims continued to send their detachments to various parts of Najd and Hijaz to discipline the Bedouins. Abu Ubaidah ibn al-Jarrah led a detachment to Tayy and Asad in Najd, and they dispersed into the mountains without any fighting occurring. The Messenger, peace be upon him, led an army of one thousand fighters in the month of Rabi al-Awwal in the fifth year towards Dumat al-Jandal, having been informed of a gathering of polytheists there. However, the gathering dispersed upon learning of the Muslims' arrival. The Muslims stayed in the area for several days, during which they sent out detachments. They encountered no resistance and returned to Medina after making a truce upon their return with Uyaynah ibn Hisn al-Fazari.",location:"Badr",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/78",icon:"users"},{id:79,year:"626 CE",hijri:"5 AH",month:"",title:"The Incident of the Lie",desc:"When the Prophet, peace be upon him, went out to the Banu Mustaliq to wage war against them, he drew lots among his wives to decide who would accompany him. The lot fell upon Aisha, the pure, may Allah be pleased with her. On the journey back, she was carried in a howdah. Allah decreed that on that day, she would be outside her howdah searching for a necklace that had fallen from her. When they intended to depart, they did not realize her absence and left. She was young and light in weight. When she returned, she did not see them. Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: \"I made my way to the place where I had been, thinking that the people would miss me and return for me. While I was sitting in my place, sleep overcame me, and I slept. Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal al-Sulami, then al-Dhukwani, had camped behind the army and traveled at night. He came upon my resting place in the morning and saw the silhouette of a sleeping person. He came to me and recognized me when he saw me, as he had seen me before the veil was imposed upon me. I awoke to his exclamation of recognition. I covered my face with my garment, and by Allah, he did not speak a word to me, nor did I hear a word from him except his exclamation. Then he made his mount kneel, and it stepped on her hand, so I mounted it. He set off, leading the mount with me on it, until we reached the army, after they had dismounted in the heat of noon. So, those who were destroyed were destroyed concerning my affair. The one who bore the greatest part of it was Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul...\" They accused her along with Safwan, and this clear slander began to spread in Medina. Aisha remained for a month, knowing nothing of it, until she learned of it directly from Umm Mistah. All this time, the truth had not been clarified. Then verses were revealed, to be recited until the Day of Resurrection, from His saying, the Almighty: {Indeed, those who came with the slander were a group among you. Do not think it evil for you; rather, it is good for you.} [An-Nur: 11] to His saying: {But Allah purifies whom He wills, and Allah is All-Hearing and All-Knowing.} [An-Nur: 21] Verses that exonerate the pure, the purified, from what the slanderers fabricated.",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/79",icon:"scroll"},{id:80,year:"626 CE",hijri:"5 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The Expedition of Dumat al-Jandal.",desc:"The raid on Dumat al-Jandal \u2013 a place on the edge of Syria, five nights from it and Damascus, and fifteen or sixteen nights from Medina \u2013 took place in Rabi' al-Awwal of the fifth year of the Hijra. Its cause was that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, received news that there was a large gathering of tribes in Dumat al-Jandal, and that they wronged those who passed by them, plundered what they had, and intended to approach Medina to attack it. So the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, called the people to arms, and appointed Sibaa' ibn 'Arfatah al-Ghifari as his deputy in Medina. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, set out with a thousand Muslims. They traveled by night and hid by day. With him, peace be upon him, was a guide from Banu 'Udhrah named Madhkur. When they approached Dumat al-Jandal, they attacked their livestock and shepherds, and seized what they could. Those who could, fled in all directions. When the people of Dumat al-Jandal learned of this, they dispersed. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, encamped in their vicinity but found no one there. He stayed for a few days and dispatched scouting parties, which returned without finding anyone. One man was captured. The Messenger of Allah asked him about them, and he replied: \"They fled when they heard that you had captured their livestock.\" The Messenger of Allah offered him Islam, and he accepted. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, returned to Medina without encountering any opposition.",location:"Dumat al-Jandal",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/80",icon:"shield"},{id:83,year:"627 CE",hijri:"5 AH",month:"\u0634\u0639\u0628\u0627\u0646",title:"The Battle of Banu al-Mustaliq (al-Muraysi')",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, learned that Banu al-Mustaliq \u2013 who were a clan from the Azd Yamani tribe of Khuza'ah \u2013 were gathering to fight him. Their leader was Al-Harith ibn Abi Dirar. When the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, heard about them, he marched out to meet them until he encountered them at a watering place of theirs called al-Muraysi'. This is why this expedition is also called the Expedition of al-Muraysi'. The people advanced and fought, and God defeated Banu al-Mustaliq, killing those who were killed among them. The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, took their sons, women, and wealth as spoils of war, which God granted him. Among those taken captive was Juwayriyah, may God be pleased with her, the daughter of Al-Harith ibn Abi Dirar, the chief of Banu al-Mustaliq. The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, married her when she came to him seeking his help in her manumission contract. He made forty captives from her people her dowry, and the people freed those captives they held because of the status of Juwayriyah, may God be pleased with her. During the return journey from this expedition, the people of slander spoke what they spoke concerning the Mother of the Believers, Aisha, may God be pleased with her, the truthful daughter of the truthful one, who was exonerated from above the seven heavens. In this expedition also, Ibn Ubayy ibn Salul said his statement: \"If we return to Medina, the mighty will expel the lowly from it!\" Zayd ibn Arqam heard him, and the chapter \"Al-Munafiqun\" was revealed.",location:"Al-Muraysi'",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/83",icon:"moon"},{id:84,year:"627 CE",hijri:"5 AH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The Battle of the Trench (The Confederates)",desc:"The cause of this Ghazwah was the expulsion of the Jews of Banu Nadir from Medina. Envy and malice had taken hold of their hearts, causing them to harbor enmity and seek opportunities for revenge against those who had driven them out of Medina and its surroundings. When the Jews of Khaybar, especially Banu Nadir, could not confront the Muslims, they resorted to cunning and incitement. Ibn Ishaq mentioned, with his chain of narration from a group, that those who incited the Confederates were a group of Jews, including: Sallam bin Abi al-Huqayq, Huyayy bin Akhtab al-Nadari, Kinanah bin Abi al-Huqayq al-Nadari, Hawdha bin Qays al-Wa'ili, and Abu Ammar al-Wa'ili, along with a group from Banu Nadir. When they approached Quraysh, they invited them to war against the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and said: \"We will be with you against him until we uproot him.\" Quraysh said to them: \"O assembly of Jews, you are the people of the first Scripture and have knowledge of what we and Muhammad differ about. Is our religion better or his?\" They replied: \"Rather, your religion is better than his, and you are more deserving of the truth than he!!!\" The scholars differed on the timing of this Ghazwah. Bukhari leaned towards the opinion of Musa bin Uqbah that it occurred in Shawwal in the fourth year (of Hijra), while the vast majority held that it was in the fifth year. Al-Dhahabi said: \"This is the established view.\" Ibn al-Qayyim said: \"This is the more correct opinion.\" The Prophet (peace be upon him) and his companions (may Allah be pleased with them) dug a trench in the exposed area in front of the invaders. Ibn Uqbah mentioned that digging the trench took nearly twenty nights. Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: \"The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) went out to the trench, and the Muhajirun and Ansar were digging on a cold morning. They had no slaves to do this for them. When he saw their exhaustion and hunger, he said: 'O Allah, verily, life is the life of the Hereafter, so forgive the Ansar and the Muhajirah.' They responded to him, saying: 'We are those who have pledged allegiance to Muhammad... to strive in jihad as long as we live, forever.'\" Al-Bara' (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated: \"When it was the day of the Confederates, and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) dug the trench, I saw him carrying dirt from the trench, until the dust covered the skin of his stomach. He was a man with much hair. I heard him reciting the words of Ibn Rawahah, as he carried the dirt, saying: 'By Allah, if not for You, we would not have been guided, nor would we have given charity, nor would we have prayed. So send down tranquility upon us, and make our feet firm if we meet the enemy. Indeed, those who have wronged us have rebelled against us, and if they intended to try us, we would refuse.'\" He said: \"Then he would extend his voice with the last part of it.\" Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said regarding the verse: \"{When they came upon you from above you and from below you, and when eyes shifted and hearts reached the throats...}\" (Quran 33:10), \"That was the day of the trench.\" When the Confederates reached Medina, they were surprised by the presence of the trench, and they made several attempts to breach it, but they failed. The siege lasted for twenty-four nights. The situation became difficult for Quraysh due to the wind that overturned their cooking pots and tents. The Muslims also sowed discord between the Jews and the polytheists, forcing them to retreat. Allah the Almighty defeated them and averted their evil from Medina.",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/84",icon:"star"},{id:85,year:"627 CE",hijri:"5 AH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The revelation of the verse of the hijab.",desc:"The reason for its revelation was that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him: \"If you would order your wives to veil themselves, for indeed, the righteous and the wicked speak to them.\" Then the Almighty's saying was revealed: \"O you who have believed, do not enter the houses of the Prophet except when you are permitted...\" to His saying: \"...And when you ask of them [his wives] any material thing, ask them from beyond a partition. That is purer for your hearts and their hearts.\"",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/85",icon:"child"},{id:86,year:"627 CE",hijri:"5 AH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The Battle of the Banu Qurayza Jews.",desc:"This expedition occurred immediately after the Battle of the Trench. Its cause was the breach of the covenant by Banu Qurayzah with the Prophet, peace be upon him, instigated by Huyayy ibn Akhtab an-Nadri. The Prophet, peace be upon him, had sent Zubayr to ascertain their intentions, and then followed him with the two Sa'ds \u2013 Sa'd ibn Ubadah and Sa'd ibn Mu'adh \u2013 and Ibn Rawahah, and Khawwat ibn Jubayr, for the same purpose, to confirm their treachery. Allah the Almighty commanded His Prophet, peace be upon him, to fight them after his return from the Trench and laying down of arms. So, the Prophet, peace be upon him, instructed his companions to proceed to Banu Qurayzah, saying to them: \"Let none of you pray the Asr prayer except in Banu Qurayzah.\" This is according to the narration of Bukhari, or \"Dhuhr\" according to the narration of Muslim. The Messenger, peace be upon him, then besieged Banu Qurayzah for approximately twenty-five nights, until they submitted to the judgment of the Messenger, peace be upon him. He wished to entrust their judgment to one of the chiefs of Aws, as they were allies of Banu Qurayzah. Thus, he made Sa'd ibn Mu'adh the judge over them. When he approached the Muslims, the Messenger, peace be upon him, said to the Ansar: \"Stand up for your chief \u2013 or your best.\" Then he said: \"These have submitted to your judgment.\" Sa'd ibn Mu'adh said: \"Their fighting men shall be killed, their women and children shall be taken captive, and their wealth shall be divided.\" The Prophet, peace be upon him, said to him: \"You have judged with the judgment of Allah the Almighty.\" The Messenger, peace be upon him, carried out this judgment upon them. They were approximately four hundred in number. Only a few of them escaped. Then the Messenger, peace be upon him, divided their wealth and their children among the Muslims.",location:"Banu Qurayza",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/86",icon:"heart"},{id:87,year:"627 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"",title:"The killing of Sallam ibn Abi al-Huqayq, the Jew.",desc:"He is Abu Rafi', Salam bin Abi al-Huqayq, a Jewish poet and knight, one of those who mobilized the factions against the Muslims in the Battle of the Confederates. He aided them with abundant provisions and wealth. When Allah killed Ka'b bin al-Ashraf at the hands of men from the Aws after the Battle of Badr, Abu Rafi', Salam bin Abi al-Huqayq was among those who incited the Confederates against the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. He was not killed with Banu Qurayza as his companion Huyayy bin Akhtab was. The Khazraj desired to kill him, seeking to equalize the Aws in reward. Allah, Glorified and Exalted, had made these two tribes compete in good deeds before the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. They sought permission from the Messenger of Allah to kill him, and he granted them permission. Al-Bara' bin Azib narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sent some men from the Ansar to Abu Rafi' the Jew. He appointed Abdullah bin Atik as their leader. Abu Rafi' used to harm the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and aid against him. He was in a fortress of his in the land of Hijaz. When they approached him, the sun had set, and people were returning with their livestock. Abdullah said to his companions: \"Sit where you are, for I am going to approach and try to get close to the gatekeeper, perhaps I can enter.\" He approached until he was near the gate. Then he covered himself with his garment as if he was relieving himself. As people were entering, the gatekeeper called out to him: \"O Abdullah, if you want to enter, then enter, for I am about to close the gate.\" So I entered and hid. When the people entered, he closed the gate, then hung the locks on a peg. He said: \"I went to the keys and took them, and opened the gate.\" Abu Rafi' used to have evening gatherings. He was in an upper chamber. When his guests left, I went up to him. As I opened each door, I locked it behind me from the inside. I said: \"If the people realize I am here, they will not be able to reach me until I kill him.\" I reached him, and he was in a dark room amidst his family. I did not know where he was in the room. I said: \"O Abu Rafi',\" he said: \"Who is this?\" I moved towards the voice and struck him with a sword, in my bewilderment. It did not avail him, and he cried out. I left the room and stayed a short distance away, then I returned to him and said: \"What is this sound, O Abu Rafi'?\" He said: \"Woe to your mother, a man was in the room and struck me with a sword before.\" He said: \"I struck him a blow that wounded him deeply but did not kill him. Then I placed the tip of the sword in his belly until it went through his back. I knew then that I had killed him. I began to open the doors, one by one, until I reached a staircase. I placed my foot, thinking I had reached the ground, and fell on a moonlit night. My leg broke. I tied it with my turban, then I set off until I sat by the door. I said: 'I will not leave tonight until I know if I have killed him.' When the rooster crowed, the announcer stood on the wall and said: 'I announce the death of Abu Rafi', the merchant of the people of Hijaz.' I went to my companions and said: 'Escape! Allah has killed Abu Rafi'.' I went to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and told him. He said: 'Stretch out your leg.' I stretched out my leg, and he wiped it, and it was as if I had never complained of it.\"",location:"The Land of the Hijaz",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/87",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:88,year:"627 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The Battle of Dhu Qarad.",desc:"'Uyaynah ibn Hisn, among the Banu Abd Allah ibn Gha\u1e6dfan, raided the she-camels of the Prophet, peace be upon him, which were grazing in al-Ghaba. He seized them and killed their shepherd, a man from Ghifar, and they took his wife. The first to raise the alarm against them was Salamah ibn Amr ibn al-Akwa', al-Aslami, may Allah be pleased with him. Salamah said: \"I went out before the first call to prayer. The she-camels of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, were grazing at Dhi Qarad. He said: 'A slave of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf met me and said: \"The she-camels of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, have been taken.\" I asked: \"Who took them?\" He said: \"Gha\u1e6dfan.\" He said: \"So I cried out three times, 'Oh, morning!' I made my voice heard between the two lava fields of Medina. Then I set off at full speed until I caught up with them at Dhi Qarad, as they were beginning to drink from the water. I started pelting them with my arrows, for I was a good archer, and I said: 'I am the son of al-Akwa'... and today is the day of the sucklings.'\" I recited poetry until I rescued the she-camels from them, and I took thirty cloaks from them. He said: \"Then the Prophet, peace be upon him, and the people came. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have prevented the people from the water while they were thirsty, so send them now.' He said: 'O son of al-Akwa', you have gained possession, so be lenient.' Then he said: 'They are now being hosted by Gha\u1e6dfan.'\" The cry for help in Medina reached the Banu Amr ibn Awf, and reinforcements came. Horses continued to arrive, and men on foot and on camels until they reached the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, at Dhi Qarad. The Prophet, peace be upon him, reached a watering place called Dhi Qarad. He slaughtered a she-camel from those he had recovered and stayed there for a day and a night, then returned to Medina. In this raid, al-Akhram, who was Muhriz ibn Nadlah, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed. Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Uyaynah killed him and rode away on his horse. Abu Qatadah then charged him and killed him, and recovered the horse, which had belonged to Mahmud ibn Maslamah. The captive woman came on a she-camel of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, having vowed that if Allah saved her upon it, she would slaughter it. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: \"What a bad reward she has given! A person has no vow for what he does not own, nor for disobedience.\" And he took back his she-camel. Muslim narrated in his Sahih from Salamah ibn al-Akwa' concerning this incident, saying: \"We returned to Medina, and we stayed only three nights until we went out to Khaybar.\"",location:"Dhi Qarad",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/88",icon:"dove"},{id:89,year:"627 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The expedition of Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, to al-Qurata.",desc:"The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, with thirty riders to Al-Qur\u1e6d\u0101\u02be \u2013 a seven-night journey from Medina. They were a clan of Banu Bakr, and their leader was \u02bfUbayd ibn Kil\u0101b. Muhammad ibn Maslamah and his companions set out, traveling by night and hiding during the day. When they raided them, the rest fled after Muhammad killed a number of them. The Muslims seized camels and sheep and arrived in Medina on the last night of Mu\u1e25arram, bringing with them Thumamah ibn Uth\u0101l al-\u1e24anaf\u012b, the chief of Banu \u1e24an\u012bfah. He had gone out in disguise to assassinate the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, by the order of Musaylimah the Liar. The Muslims captured him, and when they brought him, they tied him to one of the pillars of the Mosque. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, went out to him and said, \"What do you have, O Thumamah?\" He replied, \"I have good, O Muhammad. If you kill, you kill one who has blood [to avenge]. If you bestow favor, you bestow it upon one who is grateful. And if you desire wealth, ask, and you will be given whatever you wish.\" So he left him. Then he passed by him again and said the same thing to him, and he replied as he had before. Then he passed by a third time and, after the previous conversation, said, \"Release Thumamah.\" So they released him. He went to a palm grove near the Mosque, bathed, then came and embraced Islam. He said, \"By Allah, there was no face on the face of the earth more hateful to me than your face, but now your face has become the most beloved of faces to me. And by Allah, there was no religion on the face of the earth more hateful to me than your religion, but now your religion has become the most beloved of religions to me. Your cavalry captured me while I intended to perform \u02bfUmrah.\" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, gave him glad tidings and ordered him to perform \u02bfUmrah. When he arrived among the Quraysh, they said, \"Have you apostatized, O Thumamah?\" He said, \"No, by Allah, but I have embraced Islam with Muhammad, peace and blessings be upon him. And by Allah, not a single grain of wheat will come to you from Yam\u0101mah unless the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, permits it.\" Yam\u0101mah was the fertile region of Mecca. He returned to his land and prevented the transport of goods to Mecca until the Quraysh were in dire straits. They wrote to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, imploring him by their kinship ties to write to Thumamah to allow them to transport food. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, did so.",location:"Al-Qur\u1e6d\u0101\u02be",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/89",icon:"hand-holding-heart"},{id:90,year:"627 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The expedition of Ukashah ibn Mihsan (may Allah be pleased with him) to al-Ghamr (the water of Banu Asad).",desc:"The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent Ukkasha ibn Mihsan, may Allah be pleased with him, to Al-Ghamr \u2013 a water source belonging to Banu Asad \u2013 with forty men, including Thabit ibn Aqram and Shuja ibn Wahb. They set out quickly, but the people were alerted and fled to the higher ground of their land. He found their dwellings empty, meaning their inhabitants were absent. He then sent Shuja ibn Wahb as a scout. He saw tracks of camels and sheep. They moved on and found someone who guided them to some of their livestock. They granted him safety, and he led them to camels belonging to his cousins. They raided them and seized two hundred camels. They sent the man back and drove the camels to Medina. They arrived at the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, without encountering any harm.",location:"The Flood",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/90",icon:"book"},{id:91,year:"627 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The expedition of Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may Allah be pleased with him, to Banu Tha'labah and Banu 'Awal at Dhi al-Qassah.",desc:"The Messenger of God, Muhammad, peace be upon him, sent Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may God be pleased with him, along with ten men, to Banu Tha'labah of Ghatafan and Banu 'Awalin of Tha'labah, who were at Dhi al-Qassah. They arrived upon them at night, and the people surrounded them. They were one hundred men. They exchanged arrows for a while during the night, then the Arabs attacked them with spears and killed them all. Muhammad ibn Maslamah fell wounded. They struck his ankle, and he did not move, so they thought he was dead. They stripped him of his clothes and left. A Muslim man passed by Muhammad and his companions and uttered \"Indeed, to God we belong and to Him we shall return.\" When Muhammad heard him saying this, he moved. The man took him and carried him to Medina. At that point, the Messenger of God, Muhammad, peace be upon him, sent Abu Ubaidah ibn al-Jarrah with forty men to the place where they fell. They found no one, but they found camels and sheep, and they drove them to Medina.",location:"Dhi al-Qassah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/91",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:92,year:"627 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The expedition of Abu Ubaidah ibn al-Jarrah to Dhi al-Qassah.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, sent Abu Ubaidah ibn al-Jarrah, may God be pleased with him, to Dhi al-Qassah \u2013 a place near Medina \u2013 in the aftermath of the killing of the companions of Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may God be pleased with him, along with forty Muslims, as they were performing the Maghrib prayer. He marched to them on foot until they reached Dhi al-Qassah at the break of dawn \u2013 the remaining darkness of the night. They raided them, but they escaped into the mountains, eluding them. They captured one man who embraced Islam, so they left him. They also seized some of their livestock and scattered household items. They brought these back to Medina. The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, took one-fifth of the spoils and distributed the rest among them. Ibn Sa'd states in his Tabaqat that the reason for sending Abu Ubaidah, may God be pleased with him, to Dhi al-Qassah was the news that Banu Muharib ibn Khasfah, Tha'labah, and Anmar \u2013 who are from Ghatafan \u2013 had agreed to raid the livestock of Medina as it grazed at Hayfa, a place seven miles from Medina. It is possible that the Messenger, peace be upon him, sent Abu Ubaidah, may God be pleased with him, to Dhi al-Qassah twice, or that the expedition was a single event with two reasons: to avenge the slain companions of Muhammad ibn Maslamah, may God be pleased with him, and to repel those who intended to raid the livestock of Medina.",location:"Dhi al-Qissah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/92",icon:"route"},{id:93,year:"627 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The expedition of Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, to al-Jumum (a watering place on the road to Mecca).",desc:"The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, to Banu Sulaym. He traveled until he reached al-Jamum \u2013 which is a watering place on the road to Mecca, to the left of Batn Nakhla. There, they encountered a woman from Muzaynah, named Halimah. She guided them to a settlement of Banu Sulaym. In that settlement, they seized camels, sheep, and captives. Among the captives was the husband of Halimah al-Muzayniyyah. When Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, returned to Medina with what he had acquired, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, granted Halimah herself and her husband to her.",location:"The Abundant Flow",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/93",icon:"flag"},{id:94,year:"627 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u062c\u0645\u0627\u062f\u0649",title:"The Expedition against Banu Lihyan (who betrayed on the Day of Ar-Raj'i).",desc:"Banu Lihyan: They were the ones who betrayed Khubayb ibn Adi, may Allah be pleased with him, and his companions on the Day of Ar-Raji. Since their lands extended deep into the Hijaz region, bordering Mecca, and due to existing feuds between the Muslims on one side, and Quraysh and the Bedouins on the other, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, deemed it unwise to venture into lands close to the greatest and primary enemy, Quraysh. When the confederates faltered and their resolve weakened, he saw that the time had come to raid Banu Lihyan and avenge the companions of Ar-Raji. So, the Messenger, peace and blessings be upon him, marched out to them with two hundred of his companions, seeking retribution for the blood of his companions, accompanied by twenty horses. He appointed Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum, may Allah be pleased with him, as his deputy in Medina. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, gave the impression that he intended to go to Sham to catch Banu Lihyan by surprise. Then he hastened his march until he reached Wadi Ghurran, between Amj - a place between Mecca and Medina - and Usfan - a village between Mecca and Medina. These were the dwelling places of Banu Lihyan, and where his companions had met their fate. He prayed for mercy upon them and supplicated for them. Banu Lihyan heard of his arrival, so they fled and took refuge in the mountain peaks. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, could not reach any of them. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, stayed in their land for a day or two, and sent out raiding parties in every direction, but they could not capture anyone. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, marched with his companions to Usfan, so that Quraysh would hear of it and be filled with dread, and to show them his strength. He sent Abu Bakr As-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, with ten horsemen to Kur al-Ghamim - a place between Mecca and Medina. Then Abu Bakr As-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, returned without encountering anyone.",location:"Wadi Ghuran",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/94",icon:"mountain"},{id:95,year:"627 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u062c\u0645\u0627\u062f\u0649",title:"The expedition of Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, to Al-\u02bf\u012a\u1e63.",desc:"The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, in a detachment of one hundred and seventy riders to Al-`Is, a place near Medina on the coast of the sea, with the aim of intercepting a caravan of Quraysh that was coming from Syria, led by Abu Al-`As ibn Al-Rabi`. They caught up with it, seized it and what was in it, and that day they took much silver belonging to Safwan ibn Umayyah. They also captured people from among those who were in the caravan, including Abu Al-`As ibn Al-Rabi`, and brought them to Medina. Abu Al-`As was among the distinguished men of Mecca in terms of trade, wealth, and trustworthiness. He was the husband of Zaynab, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. His mother was Halah bint Khuwaylid, the sister of Khadijah, may Allah be pleased with her. Abu Al-`As came to Zaynab, may Allah be pleased with her, at night. Zaynab had migrated before him, leaving him in his polytheism. He sought her protection, and she granted him protection. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, approved her protection.",location:"Al-\u02bfAys",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/95",icon:"sun"},{id:96,year:"627 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u062c\u0645\u0627\u062f\u0649",title:"The expedition of Zayd ibn Harithah to Banu Tha'labah at al-Tarf (a watering place near Medina).",desc:"The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, to Al-Taraf \u2013 which is a watering place thirty-six miles from Medina. He went out to Banu Tha'labah with fifteen men. The Arabs fled, fearing that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, had marched against them, and that these men were a vanguard. He seized twenty camels from their livestock and returned to Medina without encountering any opposition. He was away for four nights. Their war cry was: \"Kill, kill.\"",location:"The Party",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/96",icon:"users"},{id:97,year:"628 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u0634\u0639\u0628\u0627\u0646",title:"The Expedition of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, to Dumat al-Jandal.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, called Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may God be pleased with him, and said to him: \"Prepare yourself, for I am sending you on a mission from this day, or tomorrow if God Almighty wills.\" Abd al-Rahman awoke and went to the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, who seated him before him and turbaned him with his own hand. Then the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, tied the banner with his own hand, or ordered Bilal to hand it to him, and then said to him: \"Take it in the name of God and His blessings.\" Then he praised God Almighty, and then the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said: \"Go forth in the name of God and in the cause of God, and fight those who disbelieve in God! Do not commit treachery \u2013 treachery is betrayal in spoils of war and theft from the booty before distribution \u2013 do not break your covenant, and do not kill a child.\" Then the Messenger of God ordered him to march to the tribe of Banu Kalb in Dumat al-Jandal, and to invite them to Islam. He said to him: \"If they respond to you, then marry their king's daughter.\" Abd al-Rahman, may God be pleased with him, marched with his companions, who numbered seven hundred men, until he reached Dumat al-Jandal. He stayed there for three days, inviting them to Islam. When the third day came, their chief and king, al-Asbagh ibn Amr al-Kalbi, who was a Christian, embraced Islam, and many of his people embraced Islam with him. Abd al-Rahman then sent Rafi' ibn Makith as a bearer of good news to the Prophet, peace be upon him, informing him of the victory God had granted him and writing to him about it. Abd al-Rahman married Tamadir bint al-Asbagh and brought her to Medina. She later bore him Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf.",location:"Dumat al-Jandal",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/97",icon:"scroll"},{id:98,year:"628 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u0631\u0645\u0636\u0627\u0646",title:"The expedition of Abu Bakr Al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, to Banu Fazara in Wadi al-Qura.",desc:"The clan of Fazara intended to assassinate the Prophet, peace be upon him. So, the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, sent a detachment led by Abu Bakr Al-Siddiq, may God be pleased with him. They raided them, killing, capturing, and taking spoils. Among their notorious figures was Umm Qirfa, who had prepared thirty horsemen from her household to assassinate the Messenger of God, peace be upon him. They were killed, and her daughter was captured. The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, ransomed some Muslim captives in Mecca with her. Imam Muslim narrated in his Sahih from Salamah bin Al-Akwa', may God be pleased with him, who said: \"We went on an expedition against Fazara, and Abu Bakr was our commander, appointed by the Messenger of God, peace be upon him. When we were an hour away from the water, Abu Bakr ordered us to encamp. Then he launched the raid and reached the water. He killed those he killed there and took captives. I saw a group of people, including children, and I feared they would reach the mountain before me, so I shot an arrow between them and the mountain. When they saw the arrow, they stopped. I brought them, driving them. Among them was a woman from Banu Fazara wearing a garment of tanned leather (meaning a leather garment), with her was a daughter, one of the most beautiful Arab women. I drove them until I brought them to Abu Bakr, and Abu Bakr gave me her daughter as a spoil. When we arrived in Medina, I had not uncovered her. The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, met me in the market and said: 'O Salamah, gift me the woman.' I said: 'O Messenger of God, by God, she has pleased me, and I have not uncovered her.' The next day, the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, met me again in the market and said to me: 'O Salamah, gift me the woman, may your father be sacrificed for you!' I said: 'She is yours, O Messenger of God, and by God, I have not uncovered her.' So, the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, sent her to the people of Mecca, and with her, he ransomed some Muslims who had been captured in Mecca.\"",location:"Wadi al-Qura",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/98",icon:"shield"},{id:99,year:"628 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The Expedition of Kurz ibn Jabir al-Fihri to the 'Arniyyin.",desc:"In this year, the expedition of Kurz ibn Jabir al-Fihri took place against the Uraynah tribe. These were the ones who killed the shepherd of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and drove away the livestock. So, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sent Kurz ibn Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, with twenty horsemen in pursuit of them. Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated that a group from Ukl and Uraynah came to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and said: \"We are people of milk-producing animals, and we are not people of fertile land. The climate of Medina did not suit us.\" So, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, ordered for them a herd of camels and a shepherd, and instructed them to go out with them and drink from their urine and milk. They set off, and when they reached the area of Al-Harrah, they killed the shepherd of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and drove away the herd. They renounced Islam after having embraced it. The Prophet, peace be upon him, sent out in pursuit of them. He ordered that their hands and feet be cut off, and their eyes be branded with hot iron nails. He left them in the area of Al-Harrah until they died in that state. The reason their eyes were branded was because they had branded the eyes of the shepherds.",location:"Al-Harrah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/99",icon:"moon"},{id:100,year:"628 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The Pledge of Ridwan.",desc:"Before the conclusion of the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, had sent Khirash ibn Umayyah al-Khuzai to Mecca, riding his camel named al-Tha'lab. When he entered Mecca, Quraysh hamstrung his camel and intended to kill Khirash, but the Ah\u0101b\u012bsh (who were Banu al-Harith ibn Abd Manah ibn Kinanah, al-Hawn ibn Khuzaymah ibn Mudrikah, and Banu al-Mustaliq from Khuz\u0101'ah) prevented them until he reached the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. He then called for Umar to send him to Mecca. Umar said, \"O Messenger of Allah, I fear Quraysh for myself, and there is no one from Banu Adi here to protect me. Quraysh know my enmity towards them and my harshness against them. However, I can direct you to a man who is more honored than I, Uthman ibn Affan.\" The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, called for Uthman and sent him to Quraysh to inform them that he had not come for war, but as a visitor to this House, honoring its sanctity. Uthman set out until he reached Mecca, where Aban ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As met him. Aban dismounted his mount, placed Uthman before him and behind him on his mount, and gave him protection until he delivered the message of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. Uthman then went to Abu Sufyan and the dignitaries of Quraysh and conveyed to them what the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, had sent him with. They said to Uthman, \"If you wish, you may circumambulate the Kaaba.\" He replied, \"I would not do so until the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, circumambulates it.\" Quraysh then detained him. It reached the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and the Muslims that Uthman had been killed. Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Pledge of Ridwan took place after Uthman had gone to Mecca. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, placed his right hand on his left hand and said, \"This is the hand of Uthman,\" and struck it with his hand, saying, \"This is for Uthman.\" Jabir ibn Abdullah said: On the day of Hudaybiyyah, we were one thousand and four hundred. We pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and Umar was holding his hand under the tree, which was a samurah tree. The Prophet, peace be upon him, said to us, \"Today, you are the best people on earth.\" Then Jabir said: If I were able to see, I would show you the location of the tree. Ma'qil ibn Yasar said: I saw myself on the day of the tree, and the Prophet, peace be upon him, was taking the pledge from the people, and I was holding up a branch of it over his head, and we were fourteen hundred. The narrations differ regarding their number. Ibn Hajar said: The reconciliation between these differences is that they were more than one thousand and four hundred. Whoever said one thousand and five hundred accounted for the fraction, and whoever said one thousand and four hundred disregarded it.",location:"Al-Hudaybiyyah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/100",icon:"star"},{id:101,year:"628 CE",hijri:"6 AH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The Treaty of Hudaybiyyah.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, went out with about fourteen hundred of his companions, intending to perform Umrah. When they reached Al-Hudaybiyyah, which is about 22 km from Mecca, news reached him that there were those who intended to fight him. He said: \"I have not come for fighting; rather, I have come for Umrah.\" The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, sent Uthman ibn Affan to Mecca to inform them of this. Then the news spread that Uthman had been killed. So the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, ordered the companions, may God be pleased with them, to pledge allegiance to him for fighting, and they pledged allegiance to him. This was the Pledge of Ridwan. Then the polytheists came to Al-Hudaybiyyah, and several negotiations took place between the leaders of the polytheists and the Prophet, peace be upon him, until Suhail ibn Amr came, and the Prophet, peace be upon him, made peace with him on the condition that war would be suspended for ten years, during which people would be safe and refrain from harming each other. It was also agreed that whoever came to the Messenger of God from his companions without the permission of his guardian, the Messenger of God would return him to them, and whoever came to the Quraysh from his companions, they would not return him. And that there would be a sealed chest between them, meaning no deceit. And that there would be no stealing or treachery. And that he would return in this year without entering Mecca, but he would be allowed to enter it for three days in the following year, with the weapons of a traveler, and not to enter it without swords in their sheaths. Then the Prophet, peace be upon him, ordered his companions to sacrifice their animals and shave their heads, but they did not do so until Umm Salamah, may God be pleased with her, advised him to do it himself without speaking to anyone. When they saw him sacrifice, they rushed to the sacrificial animals and did the same. Then the Prophet, peace be upon him, returned to Medina without performing Umrah and without fighting.",location:"Al-Hudaybiyyah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/101",icon:"child"},{id:102,year:"628 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The Battle of Khaybar.",desc:"The historians differ regarding its timing, with two opinions. It was said: in the seventh year. This is the opinion of Ibn Ishaq and others. And it was said: in the sixth year. This is the opinion of Malik and others. Ibn al-Qayyim said: The majority hold that it was in the seventh year. Ibn Hajar said: These opinions are close, and the most likely among them is what Ibn Ishaq mentioned. It is possible to reconcile them by saying that whoever stated the sixth year did so based on the beginning of the year from the actual month of the Hijra, which is Rabi' al-Awwal. Anas narrated: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, went on an expedition to Khaybar. He said: \"We prayed the dawn prayer there in the early morning darkness. Then the Prophet of Allah, peace be upon him, mounted, and Abu Talha mounted, and I was riding behind Abu Talha. The Prophet of Allah, peace be upon him, rode through a narrow alley of Khaybar, and my knee was touching the thigh of the Prophet of Allah, peace be upon him. The lower garment of the Prophet of Allah, peace be upon him, slipped, and I saw the whiteness of the thigh of the Prophet of Allah, peace be upon him. When he entered the town, he said: 'Allahu Akbar! Khaybar is ruined! When we descend upon a people's courtyard, then it is a bad morning for those who are warned.' He said this three times. He said: The people had gone out to their work, and they said: 'Muhammad! By Allah!' (Abdul Aziz said: And some of our companions said: 'Muhammad and the army'). He said: 'And we conquered it by force. The captives were gathered. Then Dihya came to him and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, give me a slave girl from the captives.' He said: 'Go and take a slave girl.' So he took Safiyyah bint Huyayy. Then a man came to the Prophet of Allah, peace be upon him, and said: 'O Prophet of Allah, you gave Safiyyah bint Huyayy, the chief of Qurayzah and an-Nadir, to Dihya! She is only fit for you.' He said: 'Call him with her.' He said: 'So he brought her. When the Prophet, peace be upon him, looked at her, he said: 'Take a slave girl from the captives other than her.' He said: 'And he freed her and married her...'\" Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: The Prophet, peace be upon him, went out to Khaybar. He came to it at night. When he came to a people at night, he would not attack them until morning. When morning came, the Jews came out with their shovels and baskets. When they saw him, they said: \"Muhammad! By Allah! Muhammad and the army!\" Then the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Allahu Akbar! Khaybar is ruined! When we descend upon a people's courtyard, then it is a bad morning for those who are warned.\" And Allah Almighty made the conquest of Khaybar at the hands of Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him. Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, gave Khaybar to the Jews to work it and cultivate it, and they would have half of what came out of it. So the Prophet, peace be upon him, let them remain in their land, and they would have half.",location:"Khaybar",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/102",icon:"heart"},{id:103,year:"628 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The prohibition of the flesh of domestic donkeys and the temporary marriage of women on the day of Khaybar.",desc:"During the Battle of Khaybar, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, prohibited the meat of domestic donkeys, stating that it was an impurity. He ordered that the pots be thrown away while they were boiling with their meat, and he later ordered the pots to be washed. At that time, he permitted the meat of horses and fed it to them. He also prohibited temporary marriage (mut'ah) of women.",location:"Khaybar",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/103",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:104,year:"628 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The Hijra of Abu Musa al-Ash'ari and his companions, may Allah be pleased with them, to Medina, passing through Abyssinia.",desc:"Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) reported: News of the departure of the Prophet (peace be upon him) reached us while we were in Yemen. So, my two brothers and I, I being the youngest, set out as emigrants to him. One of them was Abu Burda, and the other was Abu Ruhm. He said, \"Either in a few, or he said, in fifty-three, or fifty-two men from my people.\" We boarded a ship, and our ship cast us ashore to the Negus in Abyssinia. We met Ja'far bin Abi Talib and his companions there. Ja'far said, \"The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) sent us here and commanded us to stay, so stay with us.\" We stayed with him until we all arrived. We met the Prophet (peace be upon him) when he conquered Khaybar. He gave us a share of it \u2013 or he said, he gave us from it. He did not distribute anything from it to anyone who was absent from the conquest of Khaybar, except to those who witnessed it with him, except for the companions of our ship with Ja'far and his companions; he distributed to them along with them.",location:"Abyssinia",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/104",icon:"dove"},{id:105,year:"628 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The Jews' attempt to poison the Messenger of God, peace be upon him.",desc:"When the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, felt secure in Khaybar after its conquest, Zaynab bint al-Harith, the wife of Sallam ibn Mishkam, presented him with a roasted sheep. She had inquired which part of the sheep the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, preferred, and was told it was the foreleg. She then put a large amount of poison in it, and also poisoned the rest of the sheep. She brought it to him, and when she placed it before the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, he took the foreleg and chewed a morsel from it, but he could not swallow it and spat it out. Then he said, \"This bone informs me that it is poisoned.\" He then called for her, and she confessed. He asked, \"What drove you to do this?\" She replied, \"I thought if he were a king, we would be rid of him, and if he were a prophet, he would be informed.\" He pardoned her. Bishr ibn al-Bara' ibn Ma'rur was with him and ate a portion of it, which he swallowed. He died from it. There are differing narrations regarding the pardon of the woman and her execution. They agree that he initially pardoned her, but when Bishr died, he killed her in retribution.",location:"Khaybar",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/105",icon:"hand-holding-heart"},{id:106,year:"628 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0645\u062d\u0631\u0645",title:"The Prophet's correspondence with the kings, inviting them to Islam.",desc:"After the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, returned from Al-Hudaybiyyah, he wrote to the kings, inviting them to Islam. When he intended to write to them, it was said to him, \"They do not read a letter unless it has a seal on it.\" So the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, took a silver ring, the engraving of which was: \"Muhammad, Messenger of Allah.\" This engraving was in three lines: \"Muhammad\" on one line, \"Messenger\" on another, and \"Allah\" on a third. Anas bin Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, said: \"I can almost see the gleam of the ring on the Prophet's finger - or on his palm.\" He chose messengers from his companions who had knowledge and experience and sent them to the kings. He sent Dihyah bin Khalifa Al-Kalbi to Caesar, the King of Rome, who read the letter but did not embrace Islam. He sent Abdullah bin Hudhafah As-Sahmi to Khosrow, the King of Persia, who tore the letter to pieces when it was read to him. When this reached the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, he said: \"May Allah tear his kingdom apart.\" And so it was. He sent Amr bin Umayyah Ad-Damri to Al-Najashi, the King of Abyssinia. When he gave him the letter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, he took it, placed it on his eyes, and descended from his throne to the ground. He sent Hatib bin Abi Balta'ah to Al-Muqawqis, the ruler of Alexandria. He took the Prophet's letter, placed it in an ivory box, sealed it, and then wrote a letter to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, but did not embrace Islam. He gifted him two slave women, Maria and Shireen, and a mule named Dul-dul. He sent Amr bin Al-As As-Sahmi to Ja'far and 'Abbad, the sons of Al-Julanda Al-Azdiyyin, the kings of Oman. They both responded to Islam and believed in the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him. He sent Sulait bin Amr, one of the Bani Amir bin Lu'ayy, to Hawdha bin Ali Al-Hanafi, the King of Yamamah, who gave a lukewarm response. He wrote to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him: \"How excellent and beautiful is what you call to. The Arabs respect my position, so grant me some authority and I will follow you.\" He sent Al-Ala' bin Al-Hadrami to Al-Mundhir bin Sawa Al-Abdi, the King of Bahrain, who embraced Islam upon receiving the letter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. He sent Shuja' bin Wahb Al-Asadi to Al-Harith bin Abi Shimr Al-Ghassani, the King of the Syrian borders. When the letter reached him, he threw it away and said, \"Who will take my kingdom from me? I am marching to him.\" He did not embrace Islam. He sought permission from Caesar to fight the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, but Caesar dissuaded him from his resolve. Al-Harith, however, rewarded Shuja' bin Wahb with clothing and provisions and sent him back with kindness. He sent Al-Miqdad bin Al-Aswad Al-Makhzumi to Al-Harith bin Abd Kulal Al-Himayri, the King of Yemen, who had already embraced Islam along with his brothers.",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/106",icon:"book"},{id:107,year:"628 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The expedition of Ghalib ibn Abdullah al-Laythi to the Banu al-Mulawwah at Qudayd.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, sent a detachment led by Ghalib ibn Abdullah al-Laythi to the Banu al-Mulawwah in Qudayd. The Banu al-Mulawwah had killed the companions of Bashir ibn Suwayd, so this detachment was sent to exact revenge. They launched a night raid, killing whom they killed and seizing livestock. A large enemy army pursued them until, as they neared the Muslims, rain fell, and a great flood came between the two groups. The Muslims succeeded in completing their withdrawal.",location:"Qudayd",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/107",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:108,year:"628 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u062c\u0645\u0627\u062f\u0649",title:"The expedition of Zayd ibn Harithah to the land of Hisma.",desc:"The Messenger, peace be upon him, sent a letter to Caesar of Rome. The bearer of the letter was Dihyah ibn Khalifah al-Kalbi. When Caesar did what he did, he rewarded Dihyah ibn Khalifah al-Kalbi with money and clothing. As Dihyah was on the road in Hisma, some people from Judham encountered him and robbed him, leaving him with nothing. Dihyah came to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, before entering his house and informed him. So, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sent Zayd ibn Harithah to Hisma, which is beyond Wadi al-Qura, with five hundred men. Zayd launched a raid against Judham, killing many of them and seizing their camels, sheep, and captives. He took a thousand camels, five thousand sheep, and one hundred women and children as captives. There was a truce between the Prophet, peace be upon him, and the tribe of Judham. Zayd ibn Rifaa'ah al-Judhami, one of the leaders of this tribe, quickly presented a protest to the Prophet, peace be upon him. He and some men from his people had embraced Islam and had helped Dihyah when his way was blocked. The Prophet, peace be upon him, accepted his protest and ordered the return of the spoils and the captives.",location:"Hisma",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/108",icon:"route"},{id:109,year:"628 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0634\u0639\u0628\u0627\u0646",title:"The expedition of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, to Turbah, a group of Hawazin.",desc:"The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sent Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, with thirty men to the rear of Hawazin \u2013 a place between him and Mecca, four nights away on the road to Sana'a, called Turbah. And Allah's peace be upon him sent a guide from Banu Hilal. He would travel by night and hide by day. The news reached Hawazin, so they fled. Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, came to their encampments and found no one from them, so he returned to Medina. When he was six miles between him and Medina, the guide said to him, \"Do you have another gathering from Khath'am?\" Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him, \"The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, did not order me to [fight] them; he only ordered me to fight Hawazin.\"",location:"Soil",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/109",icon:"flag"},{id:110,year:"628 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0634\u0639\u0628\u0627\u0646",title:"The expedition of Bashir ibn Sa'd al-Ansari to Banu Murrah in the region of Fadak.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, sent a detachment led by Bashir ibn Sa'd al-Ansari to Banu Murrah in the vicinity of Fadak, consisting of thirty men. They went out to them and seized sheep and camels. Then they returned, and the pursuers caught up with them at night. They shot arrows at them until Bashir and his companions ran out of arrows, and they were all killed except for Bashir. He was carried to Fadak with a flicker of life remaining, and he stayed with the Jews until his wounds healed, then he returned to Medina.",location:"Fadak",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/110",icon:"mountain"},{id:111,year:"629 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0631\u0645\u0636\u0627\u0646",title:"The expedition of Ghalib ibn Abd Allah al-Laythi to Banu Uwal.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, sent a detachment led by Ghalib ibn Abd Allah al-Laythi to Banu 'Uwal and Banu 'Abd ibn Tha'labah in al-Mayfa'ah, and it is said to the Hurqat of Juhaynah, consisting of one hundred and thirty men. They raided them all, killed those who came out to them, and seized camels and sheep. In this detachment, Usamah ibn Zayd killed Nahik ibn Mirdas after he said, \"There is no god but Allah.\" When they returned and the Prophet, peace be upon him, was informed, he was greatly displeased and said, \"Did you kill him after he said, 'There is no god but Allah'?\" Usamah replied, \"He only said it seeking refuge.\" The Prophet then said, \"Did you not split open his heart to know if he was truthful or lying?\"",location:"The Mayfa'ah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/111",icon:"sun"},{id:112,year:"629 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The expedition of Abdullah ibn Rawahah to Khaybar to kill the Jew Yassir ibn Razzam.",desc:"It reached the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, that Yasir bin Rizam the Jew was gathering Ghatafan to invade Medina. So, the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, sent Abdullah bin Rawahah with thirty riders to Yasir bin Rizam until they reached him in Khaybar. They said to him: \"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, has sent us to you to appoint you over Khaybar.\" They persisted with him until he followed them with thirty men, each man accompanied by a Muslim rider. When they reached Qarqaratu Niyar, Yasir bin Rizam regretted it and reached for the sword of Abdullah bin Rawahah. Abdullah bin Rawahah noticed this, urged his camel forward, and charged, leading the men until he was able to reach Yasir and struck his leg, severing it. Yasir then charged, and in his hand was a whip made of acacia wood, with which he struck Abdullah bin Rawahah's face, inflicting a severe wound that reached the skull. Each of the Muslim men turned to his rider and killed him, except for one Jew who outran them in speed and did not harm any of the Muslims. The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, spat into Abdullah bin Rawahah's wound, and it did not fester or cause him pain until he died.",location:"Khaybar",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/112",icon:"users"},{id:113,year:"629 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The expedition of Abu Hadrad al-Aslami to al-Ghaba.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, sent a detachment led by Abu Hadrad al-Aslami to al-Ghaba. The reason for this was that a man from Jusham ibn Mu'awiyah had arrived in al-Ghaba with a large number of people, intending to rally Qays to fight the Muslims. So, the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, sent Abu Hadrad with two men to gather intelligence and information about them. They reached the people at sunset. Abu Hadrad hid in one area, and his companions hid in another. Their shepherd was delayed until the darkness of night fell. The leader of the people then stood up alone. As he passed by Abu Hadrad, Abu Hadrad shot him with an arrow in the heart, and he fell without speaking. Abu Hadrad then beheaded him and rushed towards the camp, shouting \"Allahu Akbar.\" His companions also shouted \"Allahu Akbar\" and rushed forward. The people had no choice but to flee, and the three Muslims drove off a large number of camels and sheep.",location:"The Forest",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/113",icon:"scroll"},{id:114,year:"629 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The Umrah of Hudaybiyyah, also known as the Umrah of Fulfillment.",desc:"Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and his companions arrived in Mecca, weakened by the fever of Yathrib. The polytheists said: \"Tomorrow, a people will come to you weakened by fever, having suffered greatly from it.\" So they sat down next to the Hijr. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, ordered them to perform *ramal* (a brisk walk) for three circuits and to walk between the two corners, so that the polytheists would see their strength. The polytheists said: \"These are the ones you claimed were weakened by fever; they are stronger than so-and-so.\" Ibn Abbas said: Nothing prevented him from ordering them to perform *ramal* for all the circuits except his concern for them. And in this Umrah, the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, married Maymunah bint Al-Harith, may Allah be pleased with her.",location:"Makkah al-Mukarramah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/114",icon:"shield"},{id:115,year:"629 CE",hijri:"7 AH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The expedition of Ibn Abi al-Awja' to Banu Sulaym.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, sent the detachment of Ibn Abi Al-Awja' to Banu Sulaym, with fifty men, to invite them to Islam. They said, \"We have no need for what you have invited us to,\" and then they fought fiercely. Abu Al-Awja' was wounded in it, and two men from the enemy were captured.",location:"Banu Sulaym",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/115",icon:"moon"},{id:116,year:"629 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"",title:"The passing of Zaynab, may Allah be pleased with her, daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him.",desc:"She is Zaynab, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and the eldest of her sisters. Her cousin, Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi', married her during her mother's lifetime. She bore him Umamah, whom Ali ibn Abi Talib married after Fatima, and she bore him Ali ibn Abi al-As. Zaynab embraced Islam and migrated six years before her husband embraced Islam. Umm Atiyyah said: When Zaynab, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, died, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said to us: \"Wash her an odd number of times, three or five, and in the fifth time, put camphor, or something of camphor. When you finish washing her, inform me.\" She said: \"We informed him, and he gave us his lower garment and said: 'Put it next to her skin.'\"",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/116",icon:"star"},{id:117,year:"629 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"",title:"The Expedition of Aw\u1e6d\u0101s (a valley between Mecca and Ta'if).",desc:"When the Prophet, peace be upon him, finished with Hunayn, he sent Abu 'Amir with an army to Aw\u1e6das. They encountered Durayd ibn al-\u1e62immah, and Durayd was killed, and Allah defeated his companions. Abu Musa al-Ash'ari said: \"Abu 'Amir 'Ubayd al-Ash'ari sent me with him. Abu 'Amir was wounded in his knee by an arrow shot by a Jushami, which struck him in the knee. I reached him and asked, 'Uncle, who shot you?' He pointed to him and said, 'That is my killer who shot me.' I went for him and caught up with him. When he saw me, he turned to flee, so I pursued him, saying, 'Are you not ashamed? Why don't you stand your ground?' He stopped, and we exchanged two sword blows, and I killed him. Then I said to Abu 'Amir, 'May Allah kill your companion.' He said, 'Pull this arrow out.' I pulled it out, and water gushed forth. He said, 'Nephew, give my greetings to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and tell him to ask for my forgiveness.' Abu 'Amir appointed me as his successor over the people. He remained for a short while and then died. I returned and entered upon the Prophet, peace be upon him, in his house. He was lying on a worn-out bed with a mattress, and the rough fibers of the bed had left marks on his back and sides. I informed him of our news and the news of Abu 'Amir. He said, 'Tell him to ask for my forgiveness.' He then called for water, performed ablution, raised his hands, and said, 'O Allah, forgive 'Ubayd, Abu 'Amir.' I saw the whiteness of his armpits. Then he said, 'O Allah, make him on the Day of Resurrection above many of Your creation among mankind.' I said, 'And appoint me, and forgive me.' He said, 'O Allah, forgive the sin of 'Abd Allah ibn Qays, and admit him on the Day of Resurrection into a noble entrance.' Abu Burda said: 'One of these supplications was for Abu 'Amir, and the other was for Abu Musa.'\"",location:"Awtas",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/117",icon:"child"},{id:118,year:"629 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0635\u0641\u0631",title:"The Islam of Amr ibn al-As, Khalid ibn al-Walid, and Uthman ibn Talhah, may Allah be pleased with them.",desc:"Khalid ibn al-Walid said: \"When Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, willed the good He willed for me, He cast Islam into my heart and guided my reason. I said, 'I have witnessed all these battles against Muhammad, peace be upon him, and there was no battle I witnessed except that I would leave feeling that I was in the wrong, and that Muhammad would prevail.' When the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, went out to Hudaybiyyah, I went out with the polytheists' cavalry. I stood opposite him in 'Usfan and confronted him. He prayed the Dhuhr prayer with his companions in front of us, and we intended to raid them, but we were not able to. Then he prayed the Asr prayer with his companions, the prayer of fear, and that had a significant impact on us. I said, 'This man is protected.' So we parted ways. When he made peace with Quraysh at Hudaybiyyah, and then the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, entered Mecca for the 'Umrah of Qada', he asked for me but did not find me. He wrote me a letter saying: 'In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. To proceed, I have not seen anything more astonishing than your opinion turning away from Islam and your intellect. Would anyone be ignorant of something like Islam?' Then he inquired about me, and it was said to him, 'Allah will bring him.' He said, 'Someone like him being ignorant of Islam, and if he were to strike the polytheists alongside the Muslims, it would be better for him, and we would have preferred him over others.' When his letter reached me, I was eager to go out, and it increased my desire for Islam. I was pleased that he inquired about me. When I decided to go out to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, I met Uthman ibn Talhah and offered him Islam, and he responded quickly. He went out with me until we reached al-Haddah, where we found Amr ibn al-'As. He said, 'Welcome, O people.' We said, 'And to you.' He asked, 'Where are you headed?' We said, 'To enter Islam and follow Muhammad, peace be upon him.' He said, 'And that is what brought me here.' So we accompanied each other until we entered Medina. I put on some of my best clothes, then I went to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. He had been informed of our arrival and was pleased. He kept smiling when he saw me until I stood before him. I greeted him with prophethood, and he responded with a cheerful face. I said, 'I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that you are the Messenger of Allah.' He said, 'Praise be to Allah who has guided you. I used to think you had a mind, and I hoped He would not lead you astray except to good.' I said, 'O Messenger of Allah, you have seen what I used to witness in those battles against you. So pray to Allah to forgive them for me.' The Prophet, peace be upon him, said, 'Islam erases what came before it.'\"",location:"The City",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/118",icon:"heart"},{id:119,year:"629 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0635\u0641\u0631",title:"The expedition of Ghalib ibn Abd Allah al-Laythi to the companions of Bashir ibn Sa'd at Fidak.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, sent an expedition led by Ghalib bin Abdullah to the injured companions of Bashir bin Sa'd in Fadak, with two hundred men. They seized camels from Banu Murrah and killed some of them.",location:"Fadak",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/119",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:120,year:"629 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The expedition of Ka'b ibn 'Umayr al-An\u1e63\u0101r\u012b to Ban\u016b Ku\u1e0d\u0101\u02bfah.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, sent a detachment to Dhat Atlah under the command of Ka'b ibn 'Umayr al-Ansari with fifteen men to Banu Qudha'ah. This was because they had gathered large numbers to raid the Muslims. They encountered the enemy and invited them to Islam, but they did not respond and showered them with arrows until all of them were killed except for one man, who was recovered from among the slain.",location:"Banu Quda'ah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/120",icon:"dove"},{id:121,year:"629 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The expedition of Shuja' ibn Wahb, may Allah be pleased with him, to Banu Hawazin.",desc:"The Banu Hawazin had repeatedly extended a helping hand to the enemies of the Muslims. So, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sent Shuja' ibn Wahb al-Asadi with twenty-five men to them. They were at al-Siyy, in the vicinity of Rukbah, beyond the mine, which is five nights' journey from Medina. He ordered him to raid them. So, he traveled by night and hid by day until he surprised them while they were heedless. He had instructed his companions not to pursue them excessively. They captured a large number of camels and sheep without encountering any resistance, and drove them until they arrived in Medina and divided the spoils. Each man received fifteen camels, and they valued a camel at ten sheep. Their absence lasted fifteen nights.",location:"Kneeling",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/121",icon:"hand-holding-heart"},{id:122,year:"629 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u062c\u0645\u0627\u062f\u0649",title:"The Battle of Mu'tah.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, dispatched a detachment of three thousand fighters, with Zayd ibn Harithah as their commander. If he were killed, then Ja'far ibn Abi Talib would take command, and if he were killed, then Abdullah ibn Rawahah would take command. The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, saw them off and bid them farewell, then he returned. The army set out. When they reached Ma'an in the land of Sham, the news reached them that Heraclius, the king of Rome, had descended upon the land of Bani Ma'ab \u2013 the land of Balqa \u2013 with one hundred thousand Romans and another hundred thousand from the Christians of Sham. The Muslims stayed in Ma'an for two nights, consulting about engaging their enemy, who numbered two hundred thousand. They said, \"Let us write to the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, to inform him of the number of our enemy, so that he may command us or send us reinforcements.\" Abdullah ibn Rawahah said, \"O people, what you dislike is what you set out to seek \u2013 meaning martyrdom. We do not fight people with numbers or strength; rather, we fight them with this [faith] with which God has honored us. So, march forth, for it is one of two good outcomes: either victory or martyrdom.\" The army agreed with this opinion and set out. When they reached the borders of Balqa, they encountered some of the legions with Heraclius near a village called Mu'tah. They fought, and Zayd ibn Harithah was killed. Ja'far ibn Abi Talib took the banner, and fought until his right hand was severed. He took the banner with his left hand, and it was severed. He then embraced it and was killed in that manner. Abdullah ibn Rawahah took the banner and hesitated for a moment before dismounting, then he resolved and fought until he was killed. Thabit ibn Aqram took the banner and said, \"O Muslims, agree upon one of your own.\" They said, \"You.\" He said, \"No.\" The people agreed upon Khalid ibn al-Walid. When he took the banner, he defended the people and rallied them. Then he retreated, and they retreated with him, until he withdrew with the Muslims who were with him. Khalid ibn al-Walid said, \"On the day of Mu'tah, nine swords broke in my hand, and nothing remained in my hand but a Yemeni blade.\" This implies that they inflicted heavy casualties upon the enemy, for otherwise, they would not have been able to extricate themselves. For this reason and others, some scholars have concluded that the Muslims were victorious in this battle and were not defeated. Anas, may God be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, informed the people of the deaths of Zayd, Ja'far, and Ibn Rawahah before their news reached them. He said, \"Zayd took the banner and was struck down. Then Ja'far took it and was struck down. Then Ibn Rawahah took it and was struck down, and his eyes were shedding tears, until a sword from the swords of God took it, and God granted them victory.\"",location:"Mu'tah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/122",icon:"book"},{id:123,year:"629 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u062c\u0645\u0627\u062f\u0649",title:"The Battle of Dhat al-Salasil.",desc:"This occurred after the Battle of Mu'tah, and it was named Dhat al-Salasil because it took place near a water source called al-Salasil. The Prophet, peace be upon him, received news that a gathering of the Quda'ah tribe had assembled and intended to approach the outskirts of Medina. The Prophet, peace be upon him, summoned Amr ibn al-'As, gave him a white banner, and dispatched him with three hundred of the prominent Emigrants and Helpers. Amr ibn al-'As, may Allah be pleased with him, was their commander. Amr said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sent for me and said: \"Take your clothes and your weapons, then come to me.\" I came to him while he was performing ablution. He looked up at me, then lowered his head, and said: \"I intend to send you on a mission, and Allah will grant you safety and spoils, and I desire for you a good amount of wealth.\" I said: \"O Messenger of Allah, I did not embrace Islam for the sake of wealth, but rather I embraced Islam out of a desire for Islam and to be with the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him.\" He said: \"O Amr, good wealth is indeed good for a good man.\" During the expedition, his companions asked him to light a fire, but he forbade them. They spoke to Abu Bakr, who then spoke to him about it. He said: \"No one among them shall light a fire unless I throw him into it.\" He said: \"They encountered the enemy and defeated them. They wanted to pursue them, but he forbade them. When that army returned, they mentioned this to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and complained to him. He said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I disliked allowing them to light a fire lest their enemy see their small numbers, and I disliked them pursuing lest they receive reinforcements and turn back upon them.' So the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, praised his decision...\" In this expedition, Amr led the people in prayer while he was in a state of ritual impurity (junub) and had water. He did not do more than wash his private parts and perform tayammum. When Amr returned to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, he asked him about his prayer. Amr informed him, saying: \"By Him Who sent you with the truth, if I had performed a full bath, I would have died. I have never felt cold like that before. And Allah, the Almighty, said: {And do not kill yourselves. Indeed, Allah is to you ever Merciful.} [An-Nisa: 29].\" The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, smiled and said nothing to him.",location:"The Chain",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/123",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:124,year:"629 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0631\u062c\u0628",title:"The expedition of Abu Ubaidah ibn al-Jarrah, may Allah be pleased with him, to the coast of the Red Sea.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, sent Abu Ubaidah ibn al-Jarrah with three hundred men to the coast of the sea. The purpose of this expedition was to scout a caravan of Quraysh and to fight a tribe from Juhaynah. He provided them with a waterskin of dates, which Abu Ubaidah rationed out to them, to the point where he had to count them, giving each man one date per day. One day, when he distributed them, a date was missing for one man, and he noticed its absence that day. When their provisions ran out, they ate khabat, which is the leaves of the salim tree. The army was therefore named the \"Army of Khabat.\" They suffered severe hunger. Qais ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah, who was one of the soldiers in this expedition, slaughtered three young camels, then three more, and then three more. Abu Ubaidah then forbade him. The sea then cast forth an animal for them, called the 'anbar. They ate from it for twenty nights and used its fat for anointing, until their bodies recovered and improved. Abu Ubaidah took one of its ribs and measured it against the tallest man in the army and the tallest camel, and the camel was ridden under it. They also took provisions of its meat, dried in strips. When they returned to Medina, they came to the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, and told him about it. He said, \"It is a provision that God brought forth for you. Do you have any of its meat with you to feed us?\" They sent some of it to the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, and he ate it.",location:"Sword of the Sea",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/124",icon:"route"},{id:125,year:"629 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0634\u0639\u0628\u0627\u0646",title:"The expedition of Abu Qatadah al-Ansari to Khadrah.",desc:"The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent Abu Qatadah ibn Rib'i, may Allah be pleased with him, on a raid to Khadrah. This was because the Banu Ghaftan were gathering in Khadrah, which is the land of Muharib in Najd. So, he sent him, peace and blessings be upon him, with fifteen men. They killed some of them, took captives, and acquired spoils. His absence lasted fifteen nights.",location:"Greenness",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/125",icon:"flag"},{id:126,year:"630 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0631\u0645\u0636\u0627\u0646",title:"The expedition of Abu Qatadah, may Allah be pleased with him, to the valley of Idham.",desc:"When Quraysh and those with them broke the treaty between them and the Muslims at Hudaybiyyah, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, resolved to march against them. He ordered the people to prepare and informed them that he was heading to Mecca, saying: \"O Allah, blind Quraysh to our movements and intelligence, so that we may surprise them in their land.\" To further conceal and obscure his intentions, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sent a detachment of eight men, under the command of Abu Qatadah ibn al-Ribi', to Batn Idham, between Dhi Khashab and Dhi al-Marwah, three days' journey from Medina, at the beginning of this noble month. This was so that anyone who thought might assume that the Prophet, peace be upon him, was heading in that direction, and that news would spread accordingly. This detachment continued its journey until, upon reaching their assigned location, they learned that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, had already departed for Mecca. They then proceeded to join him.",location:"The valley of Idhm",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/126",icon:"mountain"},{id:127,year:"630 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0631\u0645\u0636\u0627\u0646",title:"The Conquest of Mecca",desc:"The reason for this was that the polytheists broke the treaty between them and the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. They raided one of the tribes allied with the Messenger, peace be upon him, which was the tribe of Khuza'ah. When the Prophet, peace be upon him, learned of the matter, he ordered the people to prepare without informing them of his destination. Then he proceeded until he encamped at Marr al-Dhahr\u0101n, a valley near Mecca. It is narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet, peace be upon him, set out from Medina in Ramadan with ten thousand men. This was eight and a half years after his arrival in Medina. He and the Muslims with him marched to Mecca, fasting and being fasted. When they reached al-Kad\u012bd, a water source between 'Usfan and Qudayd, he broke his fast, and they broke theirs. Abu Sufyan had seen the army of the Prophet, peace be upon him, before their entry into Mecca, and was terrified by what he saw. He then embraced Islam. After that, he went to his people and cried out to them, warning them that they had no strength against the army of Muhammad, peace be upon him. He told them what the Prophet, peace be upon him, had said: \"Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe, and whoever locks his door is safe, and whoever enters the Masjid is safe.\" So the people dispersed to their homes and to the Masjid. It is narrated from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet, peace be upon him, entered Mecca in the year of the Conquest from Kada', which is in the upper part of Mecca. On the day of the Conquest, the Prophet, peace be upon him, declared the blood of some of the polytheists forfeit. He found around the Kaaba three hundred and sixty idols, and he began to strike them with a stick in his hand, saying: \"Truth has come, and falsehood has vanished. Truth has come, and falsehood can neither originate nor restore.\" The narrations differ regarding the duration of his stay, peace be upon him, in the year of the Conquest. The most probable view, and Allah knows best, is that it was nineteen days.",location:"Makkah Al-Mukarramah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/127",icon:"sun"},{id:128,year:"630 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0631\u0645\u0636\u0627\u0646",title:"The expedition of Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, to al-Uzza in Nakhla.",desc:"When the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, felt secure after the Conquest, he sent Khalid ibn al-Walid to al-Uzza, five nights remaining in this noble month, to demolish it. It was in Nakhla, and it belonged to Quraysh and all of Banu Kinana, and it was their greatest idol. Its custodians were Banu Shayban. Khalid set out to it with thirty horsemen until he reached it and demolished it. When he returned, the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, asked him, \"Did you see anything?\" He said, \"No.\" He said, \"Then you did not demolish it. Return to it and demolish it.\" Khalid returned, enraged, having drawn his sword. A naked, black woman with disheveled hair came out to him. The custodian began to shout at her, and Khalid struck her, cleaving her in two. Then he returned to the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, and informed him. He said, \"Yes, that was al-Uzza, and she despaired of ever being worshipped in your lands.\"",location:"Palm tree",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/128",icon:"users"},{id:129,year:"630 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0631\u0645\u0636\u0627\u0646",title:"The expedition of Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, to Suwa', the idol of Hudhayl.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, sent Amr ibn al-As to Suwa' to demolish it. Suwa' was an idol of the people of Noah, peace be upon him. It later belonged to the Banu Hudhayl, a branch of the Mudhar tribe. This idol remained erected, worshipped and venerated by Hudhayl. They even used to make pilgrimage to it until Mecca was conquered and Hudhayl entered the religion of God in multitudes. Its location was in Ruhat, approximately 150 kilometers northeast of Mecca. When Amr arrived, its custodian asked him, \"What do you want?\" Amr replied, \"The Messenger of God, peace be upon him, has commanded me to demolish it.\" The custodian said, \"You cannot do that.\" Amr asked, \"Why?\" He replied, \"You will be prevented.\" Amr said, \"Are you still on falsehood even now? Woe to you! Does it hear or see?\" Then he approached and broke it. He ordered his companions, and they demolished its treasury house, but found nothing in it. Then he said to the custodian, \"What did you see?\" He replied, \"I have submitted to God.\"",location:"A group",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/129",icon:"scroll"},{id:130,year:"630 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The expedition of Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, to Banu Judhaymah.",desc:"When Khalid ibn al-Walid returned from destroying al-Uzza, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sent him to Banu Judhaymah, inviting them to Islam, not to fight them. He set out with three hundred and fifty men from the Muhajirun, the Ansar, and Banu Sulaym. When he reached them, he invited them to Islam, but they did not respond well, saying, \"We have embraced Islam,\" and instead kept saying, \"We have apostatized, we have apostatized.\" Khalid took this as mockery of Islam, as the Quraysh used to say \"apostate\" to taunt those who embraced Islam. So Khalid began to kill them and take them captive. He handed over a captive to each man who was with him, and one day he ordered each man to kill his captive. Ibn Umar and his companions refused until they came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and told him about it. The Prophet, peace be upon him, raised his hands and said twice: \"O Allah, I declare myself innocent of what Khalid has done.\" Banu Sulaym were the ones who killed their captives, not the Muhajirun and the Ansar. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sent Ali, who paid the blood money for their slain and compensated for what was lost.",location:"Banu Jadhima",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/130",icon:"shield"},{id:131,year:"630 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0634\u0648\u0627\u0644",title:"The Battle of Ta'if after the Muslims besieged it for forty nights.",desc:"After the Muslims pursued the remnants of the fleeing Hawazin in Aw\u1e6das and Nakhla, they turned to eliminate Thaqif, who had fled from Hunayn and Aw\u1e6das and fortified themselves in their impregnable fortresses in Ta'if. In the Sahih of Muslim, it is narrated that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and the Muslims with him besieged the polytheists in Ta'if for forty nights. From Abu Najih al-Sulami, he said: \"We besieged the fortress of Ta'if with the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, say: 'Whoever hits with an arrow, he shall have a rank in Paradise.' He said: 'On that day, I hit sixteen arrows...'\" And twenty-three men fled from that fortress to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. From Abdullah bin Umar, he said: \"When the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, besieged Ta'if and did not achieve anything from them, he said: 'We are returning, God willing.' This weighed heavily on them, and they said: 'We are leaving without conquering it.' So he said: 'Go forth to battle.' They went forth and sustained injuries. Then he said: 'We are returning tomorrow, God willing.' This pleased them, and the Prophet, peace be upon him, smiled.\"",location:"Ta'if",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/131",icon:"moon"},{id:132,year:"630 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The Battle of Hunayn (Hawazin).",desc:"Hunayn: A valley next to Dhi al-Majaz, near Ta'if. It is a dozen miles from Mecca, in the direction of al-Sharai' and the Great Flood. It is said that it was named Hunayn after a man called Hunayn ibn Qabithah ibn Mahla'il. The Prophet, peace be upon him, had stayed in Mecca for 19 days until Hawazin and Thaqif came and camped in Hunayn, intending to fight the Prophet, peace be upon him. They had gathered before that when they heard of the departure of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, from Medina, thinking that he was coming for them. When they learned that he had arrived in Mecca, they began to prepare to confront him. They intended it to be a decisive battle, so they mobilized their wealth, women, and children so that none of them would flee and abandon their families and property. Their leader was Malik ibn 'Awf al-Nadri, and they mobilized Ghatafan and others with them. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, prepared to confront them. He borrowed thirty camels and thirty shields from Ya'la ibn Umayyah, and borrowed one hundred shields from Safwan ibn Umayyah. He appointed 'Attab ibn Asid ibn Abi al-'As as governor of Mecca. It is confirmed in the two Sahih collections that the freed slaves (al-tulaqa') went out with him to Hunayn. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, met the enemy with his army in the valley of Hunayn in the early morning. They descended into it, and upon entering the valley, they attacked Hawazin, who were routed. The Muslims then fell upon the spoils they had left behind. While they were in this state, Hawazin confronted them and rained down a volley of arrows upon them. The Muslims had not expected this, and the earth became straitened for them. They turned and fled, not looking back. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, turned to the right, saying: \"Where are the people? Come to me! I am the Messenger of Allah! I am the Messenger of Allah! I am Muhammad ibn Abdullah.\" The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, ordered his uncle Al-Abbas \u2013 who had a loud voice \u2013 to call out to the people to stand firm, specifically mentioning the companions of the Pledge of Ridwan. They rushed to him, then he called out to the Ansar, then to the Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj, and they flew to him, saying: \"Labbayk, Labbayk\" (Here we are, here we are). A fierce battle ensued against Hawazin. When the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, saw the battle intensifying, he said: \"This is when the furnace has heated up.\" Then he took some pebbles \u2013 or dust \u2013 and threw them at the faces of the disbelievers, saying: \"May the faces be disfigured.\" No one among them whom Allah created was spared from having his eyes filled with dust from that handful. They turned and fled, and the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: \"By the Lord of Muhammad, they are defeated.\" In another narration: \"By the Lord of the Ka'bah, they are defeated! By the Lord of the Ka'bah, they are defeated!\" It has been narrated that the killed from Banu Malik of Thaqif alone reached 70. At Awstas, 300 from Banu Malik were killed, and a large number from Banu Nasr ibn Mu'awiyah, then from Banu Ri'ab, were killed. It has been narrated that the captives of Hunayn, women and children, numbered 6,000. Meanwhile, four Muslims were killed.",location:"Longing",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/132",icon:"star"},{id:133,year:"630 CE",hijri:"8 AH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The Umrah of Ji'ranah.",desc:"It is established that the Prophet, peace be upon him, performed `Umrah from Al-Ji`ranah in the year of Hunayn. This `Umrah is the third after the `Umrah of Al-Hudaybiyah and the `Umrah of Al-Qada'. Ibn Hajar said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, entered the state of Ihram from Al-Ji`ranah and entered Mecca at night, then he completed the rites of `Umrah, and then returned at night and woke up in Al-Ji`ranah as if he had stayed overnight. Ibrahim Al-Nakha`i said: They used to prefer entering Mecca during the day and leaving it at night. And from `Ata': If you wish, enter at night, for you are not like the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. He was a leader, and he wanted to enter it during the day so that people could see him. End. The implication of this is that for one who is a leader to be followed, it is recommended for him to enter during the day.",location:"Al-Ji'ranah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/133",icon:"child"},{id:134,year:"630 CE",hijri:"9 AH",month:"",title:"The death of Umm Kulthum, daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him.",desc:"She is Umm Kulthum, daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him \u2013 the fourth noble daughter of the Prophet. Her mother was Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, may Allah be pleased with her. It is said that she was married to Utaybah ibn Abi Lahab, then he separated from her. She embraced Islam and migrated after the Prophet, peace be upon him. When her sister Ruqayyah passed away, Uthman married her in Rabi' al-Awwal in the third year (of Hijra), but she did not bear him any children.",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/134",icon:"heart"},{id:135,year:"630 CE",hijri:"9 AH",month:"",title:"The Year of Delegations.",desc:"Among the most prominent results of the Conquest of Mecca was that the Arab tribes and their individuals began to hasten their embrace of Islam. This was because they had been awaiting the outcome of the conflict between the Muslims and Quraysh. Amr ibn Salamah narrated, saying: \"We used to be at a watering place where people passed by, and travelers would pass us. We would ask them, 'What is happening with the people? What is happening with the people? Who is this man?' They would say, 'He claims that Allah sent him, and revealed to him \u2013 or: Allah revealed such and such.' I would memorize those words, as if they were imprinted in my chest. The Arabs used to delay their Islam until the Conquest, saying, 'Leave him and his people. If he prevails over them, then he is a true prophet.' So, when the Conquest of Mecca occurred, every tribe hastened to embrace Islam, and my father hastened my people to embrace Islam.\"",location:"Honorable Mecca",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/135",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:136,year:"630 CE",hijri:"9 AH",month:"",title:"The obligation of Hajj was revealed.",desc:"Pilgrimage was known before the prophethood. Abraham called people to pilgrimage, and Moses and other prophets performed it. Pilgrimage remained known, but the polytheists altered it as they pleased. The first to make alterations was Amr ibn Luhayy. Then, Allah the Almighty ordained pilgrimage upon Muslims who are able to undertake the journey, saying: {And [due] to Allah from the people is a pilgrimage to the House, for whoever is able to find thereto a way} [Al-Imran: 97]. The Messenger, peace be upon him, explained its rites in full during the Farewell Pilgrimage in the year 10 AH. There is disagreement regarding the year pilgrimage was ordained; it is said to have been ordained in the year six, or in the year seven, or in the year eight, or other opinions exist.",location:"Makkah Al-Mukarramah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/136",icon:"dove"},{id:137,year:"630 CE",hijri:"9 AH",month:"\u0631\u062c\u0628",title:"The death of the Negus, the ruler of Abyssinia, may Allah be pleased with him.",desc:"He is Ashama ibn Abjar, the King of Abyssinia. \"Najashi\" is a title for him and the kings of Abyssinia. The meaning of \"Ashama\" is \"gift,\" and it is also said to mean \"gift of God\" or \"generosity.\" He assumed rule after the death of his uncle. Years after his reign and the spread of his justice, he embraced Islam during the time of the Prophet, peace be upon him. He treated the Muslims who migrated to his land with kindness. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said to his companions about the Najashi: \"If you were to go to the land of Abyssinia, there is a king there under whom no one is wronged. It is a land of truth, until Allah grants you a way out.\" When he died, the Prophet, peace be upon him, led the funeral prayer in absentia for him in Medina. Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, informed them of the death of the Najashi, the ruler of Abyssinia, on the day he died, and said: \"Ask forgiveness for your brother.\" Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said when the Najashi died: \"A righteous man died today. So, stand up and offer the funeral prayer for your brother Ashama.\"",location:"Abyssinia",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/137",icon:"hand-holding-heart"},{id:138,year:"630 CE",hijri:"9 AH",month:"\u0631\u062c\u0628",title:"The Battle of Tabuk and the Army of Hardship.",desc:"Tabuk is a place between Wadi al-Qura and Sham. Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, may Allah be pleased with him, said: \"My companions sent me to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, to ask him for mounts for them, as they were with him in the Army of Hardship, which was the expedition to Tabuk...\" The hadith of Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, indicates the severe hardship the Companions faced in terms of wealth, provisions, and mounts. As narrated by Muslim, also from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, the Muslims suffered from a lack of provisions on the way to this expedition, to the extent that they sucked the date pits and drank water with them. In another narration by Muslim, they asked the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, for permission to slaughter their mounts to eat. And Allah Almighty said: {Allah has turned in forgiveness to the Prophet and to the Emigrants and Helpers who followed him in the hour of hardship, after the hearts of a party of them had falved. Then He turned to them in forgiveness. Indeed, He is to them Kind and Merciful.} [Quran 9:117]. Ibn Kathir said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, resolved to fight the Romans because they were the closest people to him and the most deserving of being invited to the truth due to their proximity to Islam and its people. And Allah Almighty said: {O you who have believed, fight those adjacent to you of the disbelievers and let them find harshness in you, and know that Allah is with the righteous.} [Quran 9:123]. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, clarified the objective of this expedition. From Mu'adh, may Allah be pleased with him, it is narrated that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: \"You will arrive tomorrow, Allah willing, at the spring of Tabuk. You will not reach it until the day has brightened. So whoever among you arrives there, let him not touch any of its water until I come.\" The well-known and preferred opinion is that the army of Tabuk numbered thirty thousand. However, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, did not encounter any fighting from the enemy. He returned to Medina victorious after staying in Tabuk for twenty nights.",location:"Tabuk",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/138",icon:"book"},{id:139,year:"631 CE",hijri:"9 AH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The death of the head of the hypocrites, Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul.",desc:"He is Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul, the head of the hypocrites. He fell ill in the remaining nights of Shawwal after the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, returned from the Battle of Tabuk. When he died, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sought forgiveness for him and prayed over him after Umar attempted to prevent him from praying over him. The Quran was revealed thereafter, agreeing with Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, only prayed over him to uphold the outward ruling, which was Islam, and for the religious benefit of reconciling the hearts of his people and followers. A large group of hypocrites pledged allegiance to him, so perhaps they would be influenced, turn away from their hypocrisy, take heed, and become sincere to Allah and His Messenger.",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/139",icon:"star-and-crescent"},{id:140,year:"631 CE",hijri:"9 AH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The Pilgrimage of Abu Bakr Al-Siddiq and the Revelation of Surat Bara'ah.",desc:"The Prophet, peace be upon him, sent Abu Bakr as the commander of the Hajj after the end of Dhu al-Qi'dah to perform the Hajj for the Muslims. The polytheists were still performing their Hajj in their usual way. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and the Muslims with him set out. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, also sent Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, after Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, to be with him. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was to personally deliver the declaration of disassociation to the polytheists on behalf of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, as he was his cousin and from his kin. Abu Hurayrah said: Abu Bakr sent me on that Hajj with some callers to prayer on the Day of Sacrifice, announcing in Mina: \"No polytheist shall perform Hajj after this year, and no one shall circumambulate the House naked.\" Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman said: Then the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sent Ali after him and ordered him to announce the declaration of disassociation. Abu Hurayrah said: Ali announced with us among the people of Mina on the Day of Sacrifice: \"No polytheist shall perform Hajj after this year, and no one shall circumambulate the House naked.\"",location:"Mina",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/140",icon:"route"},{id:141,year:"631 CE",hijri:"10 AH",month:"",title:"The succession of delegations to the Prophet, peace be upon him.",desc:"The delegations continued to arrive to the Messenger of God, peace be upon him. Among them was the delegation of the Christians of Najran, and he made peace with them, stipulating the jizya (poll tax) and granting them protection.",location:"Al-Madinah Al-Munawwarah",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/141",icon:"flag"},{id:142,year:"631 CE",hijri:"10 AH",month:"",title:"The death of Ibrahim, son of the Prophet, peace be upon him.",desc:"Ibrahim was the son of the Prophet, peace be upon him, and his mother was Maria al-Qibtiyya. All of the Prophet's children, peace be upon him, were from Khadijah, may Allah be pleased with her, except for Ibrahim, who was from Maria al-Qibtiyya the Egyptian, may Allah be pleased with her. Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, said: \"I have not seen anyone more compassionate to children than the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. He said: Ibrahim was being nursed in the high lands of Medina. The Prophet, peace be upon him, would go with us, and we would enter the house, and it would be smoky. His wet nurse was a blacksmith. He would take him, kiss him, and then return.\" Amr said: When Ibrahim died, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: \"Indeed, Ibrahim is my son, and he died while still nursing. He has two wet nurses who will complete his nursing in Paradise.\" Al-Mughira ibn Shu'bah said: The sun was eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, on the day Ibrahim died. The people said: The sun was eclipsed because of Ibrahim's death. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: \"The sun and the moon do not eclipse for the death of anyone, nor for their life. When you see that, then pray and supplicate to Allah.\" Abu Bakra said: The sun was eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. He went out, dragging his cloak, until he reached the mosque. The people gathered around him. He led them in two rak'ahs, and the sun became clear. He said: \"The sun and the moon are two signs from the signs of Allah, and they do not eclipse for the death of anyone. When that happens, then pray and supplicate until what is with you is revealed.\" This was because a son of the Prophet, peace be upon him, died, and he was called Ibrahim. The people spoke about this. Ibrahim was born to him in Medina from his concubine Maria al-Qibtiyya in the year eight of the Hijra. His freedman Abu Rafi' gave him the good news, so he gave him a slave. He died as an infant before weaning, and there is a difference of opinion as to whether he prayed over him or not, with two opinions.",location:"The High Grounds of Medina",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/142",icon:"mountain"},{id:143,year:"631 CE",hijri:"10 AH",month:"",title:"The Farewell Pilgrimage, also known as the Pilgrimage of Islam or the Pilgrimage of Conveyance.",desc:"It was named the Farewell Pilgrimage because the Prophet, peace be upon him, bid farewell to the people during it, and he did not perform Hajj after it. It was also named the Pilgrimage of Islam because the Prophet, peace be upon him, did not perform Hajj from Medina except for this one. It was also named the Pilgrimage of Conveyance because the Prophet, peace be upon him, conveyed to the people the law of Allah in Hajj, both in word and deed. There was nothing left of the pillars and foundations of Islam that the Prophet, peace be upon him, had not clarified. When he clarified, elucidated, and explained the Sharia of Hajj to them, Allah, the Exalted, revealed to him while he was standing at Arafat: \"{This day I have perfected for you your religion and completed My favor upon you and have approved for you Islam as religion}\" [Al-Ma'idah: 3]. When the Messenger, peace be upon him, announced his intention to perform Hajj in that year, a large number of people came to Medina, all seeking to follow the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and perform Hajj like him. He set out from Medina five nights before the end of Dhu al-Qi'dah. Many events occurred for the Prophet, peace be upon him, during this journey and his return.",location:"Arafat",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/143",icon:"sun"},{id:145,year:"632 CE",hijri:"11 AH",month:"\u0631\u0628\u064a\u0639",title:"The greatest calamity for the Muslims was the death of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him.",desc:"The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, fell ill after his return from the Farewell Pilgrimage, until his pain intensified while he was in the house of Maymunah, may God be pleased with her. He called his wives and asked their permission to be nursed in the house of Aisha, may God be pleased with her. Aisha, may God be pleased with her, said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, used to say during his illness in which he died: \"O Aisha, I still feel the pain of the food I ate in Khaybar, and now is the time when I feel my aorta breaking from that poison.\" His illness lasted for ten days. Ibn Hajar said: (There is a difference of opinion regarding the duration of his illness. The majority hold that it was thirteen days, and it was said that it was one day more, and it was said that it was one day less, and it was said: ten days. Sulayman al-Taymi was certain of this in his accounts of battles, and al-Bayhaqi narrated it with a sound chain of transmission). Then God Almighty took him on Monday, the twelfth of Rabi' al-Awwal, and he was 63 years old. He was shrouded, peace and blessings be upon him, in three white Sahuliyyah garments made of cotton, which did not include a shirt or a turban. The people prayed over him in groups, and he was buried in the place of his death in the chamber of Aisha, may God be pleased with her. May God Almighty reward him on behalf of the Muslims with the best reward He has ever given a prophet for his people.",location:"Aisha's Chamber",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/145",icon:"users"},{id:6103,year:"632 CE",hijri:"10 AH",month:"\u0630\u064a",title:"The revolt of Al-Aswad Al-Ansi in Yemen and his killing at the hands of Firuz al-Daylami.",desc:"Al-Aswad al-Ansi \u2013 whose name was Abhalah ibn Ka'b ibn Gawth \u2013 emerged in the final days of the life of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, with seven hundred fighters. He wrote to the agents of the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: \"O rebels against us, return to us what you have taken from our land, and keep what you have gathered, for we are more deserving of it, and you remain as you are.\" Then he proceeded with his fighters to Najran and captured it. Afterward, he intended for Sana'a. Shahar ibn Bazham came out to him, and they fought. Al-Aswad defeated and killed him, and married Shahar ibn Bazham's wife, who was the daughter of Fayruz al-Daylami's cousin. Her name was Azadh, and she was a believer in Allah and His Messenger, peace be upon him, and among the righteous. Al-Ansi occupied Sana'a. Mu'adh ibn Jabal and Abu Musa al-Ash'ari went to Hadhramaut, and the agents of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, retreated to Al-Tahir. Umar ibn Haram and Khalid ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As returned to Medina. Yemen became secured for Al-Aswad al-Ansi, and his affair began to spread like wildfire. His rule intensified, and his situation grew grave. A group of the people of Yemen apostatized. The Muslims there dealt with him with dissimulation. When the news of Al-Aswad al-Ansi reached him, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, sent his letter with Qays ibn Makshuh, ordering the Muslims there to fight and confront Al-Ansi. Mu'adh ibn Jabal took charge of this letter perfectly. Mu'adh ibn Jabal and those who rallied around him from the people of Yemen, and Qays ibn Abd Yaghuth, the commander of Al-Aswad's army, and Fayruz al-Daylami, agreed to assassinate Al-Aswad and kill him. They pledged to do so. When night fell, they entered his house. Fayruz al-Daylami approached him. Al-Aswad was sleeping on a silk mattress, his head sunk into his body, snoring heavily, with the woman sitting beside him. Fayruz attacked him and grappled with him, who was like a camel. He seized his head, crushed his neck, and placed his knees on his back until he killed him. Qays, Dadhuwayh, and Fayruz sat planning how to inform their followers. They agreed that in the morning, they would call out their rallying cry, which was between them and the Muslims. When morning came, one of them \u2013 Qays \u2013 stood on the rampart of the fortress and called out their rallying cry. The Muslims and the disbelievers gathered around the fortress. Qays then cried out: \"I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and that Abhalah is a liar!\" He threw his head down to them. His followers fled, and people pursued them, capturing and ambushing them on every road, taking them prisoner. Islam and its adherents prevailed. The deputies of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, returned to their posts. They agreed that Mu'adh ibn Jabal would lead the people in prayer. They wrote the news to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. The duration of his rule, from his emergence until his death, was three or four months.",location:"Sana'a",source:"https://dorar.net/history/event/6103",icon:"scroll"}]},1448,[]);
